      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>POULTRY PEN.</head>
        <p>POULTRY PEN.</p>
        <p>HINTS AND INTERESTING FACTS AND CHATS FOR AMATEURS.</p>
        <p>THE WEEK'S WORK IN BRIEF.</p>
        <p>Overhaul all houses and runs and do the necessary repair work. Any holes in netting should be repaired. Place new stakes too where they are needed. Give all stock kept in confined runs plenty of greenstuff. Look over the adult stock and sell any hens not required. Give the Young growing stock all the ground you can. weep the medicine chest well stocked, and treat any ailing bird at once.—" Poultry World."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>CARE OF THE CHICKENS.</head>
        <p>CARE OF THE CHICKENS.</p>
        <p>Many poultry-keepers rear chickens on a lawn, or a front 'garden where the grass is kept short will be found an, ideal place. If the coops are 'moved every -day the ground keeps fresh and the chickens always thrive upon it. However, if the former is not available one can easily convert a little space in the back garden into a grass patch. In the spring of the -year espe-Cially grass grows quicker than anything. It will only take but a few days to get a green patch by simply raking in a few grass seeds, to which might be added a little clover. We would emphasise the fact that to keep chickens in perfect health they must be pro- vided with a liberal supply of green food. When they have no access to a grass run, sprouted oats, lettuce, cress, also ,clover, can be easily grown in shallow boxes. A green turf, clippings from the lawn or garden will always be greatly relished, whilst finely-chopped dandelion leaves is also an excellent green food, as it greatly helps to minimise the chances of liver disease. Grit is another important necessity. Indeed, we shall not be out of place by advising that a small ,saucer filled with grit might be given to the chicks for their first feed. Ofttimes we have adopted this method, and at the same time placed a goodly heap of chicken meal, also a handful of small seeds and cereals near by, and in the majority of cases the chicks prefer to take the grit first, which is on:y natural, grit acting as Nature's great necessity to aid diges- tion.</p>
        <p>Clean _drinking water must also be supplied, and during hot weather given two or three times daily, keening the vessel from the direct rays of the sun. Sun-warmed water soon becomes fainted, and many of the ailments and disease in poultry yards have been traced to this common error.—" Feathered World."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>TO DESTROY THE GERM.</head>
        <p>TO DESTROY THE GERM.</p>
        <p>Now that the hatching season is pretty well over, a large number of eggs, from well-bred stock are disposed of for eating purposes. In order to provide against persons buying the eggs at eating prices and hatching valuable chickens therefrom, many poultry-keepers are in the habit of destroying the germs. This my be done in two or three ways without dis- figuring the eggs in any way. One very good way is to pick the broad end of the egg with a fine needle. This very effectively pievents the eggs from hatching. Another way is to dip the eggs momentarily into very hot water. This slightly coagulates the white and kills the germ.—Expert, in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>SEASONABLE HINTS.</head>
        <p>SEASONABLE HINTS.</p>
        <p>Do you know that to be successful next season you must be fixing your mind on the future now? By next season, I mean the year starting October or November next, when the winter eggs will be coming along. You want to count up all the stock and see just how many of the adult birds you can sell. You must not run yourself short. See just what breeds you have sufficient pullets in, and dispose of the adults in that breed to make up the number aimed at.</p>
        <p>The poultry farmer should have his eyes open just now. Let him watch his birds carefully and ascertain which hens are laying satis- factorily and which hens are not. It will pay him to keep these hens that are in profit a little longer. Those hens that have just left their chicks should receive extra food so that the owner can get a few more eggs from them. Have a separ- ate pen and place all hens therein after their rest with chicks.—" Poultry World."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>WINTER LAYERS.</head>
        <p>WINTER LAYERS.</p>
        <p>Upon the present management of the future winter layers a very great deal depends. If they are well cared for now good results may confidently be looked kir ; if they are neglected, they will never grow int 6 prolific layers. A critical stage, when the chickens require more- careful treatment than at any other period, is when thp chicken plumage is giving place to feathers. Neglect at this stage 'shows itself in stunted growth and ill-health rather than in the death of the youngsters. Frequently it happens that a flock of chickens is doing extremely well up to a month or six weeks of age, when suddenly they commence to mope and show evident signs of reduced vitality. The poultry keeper who has had experience knows when to expect this, and guards against it, but those who are new to the work do not realise what it means, and neglect the chickens, with the in- evitable result that a long time elapses before the birds are strong and vigorous again. Foods that contain a generous proportion of album- inoids should be provided while the new feathers are being formed. Bean or pea meal in small quantities is useful; linseed, too, is very valu- able at this stage, since it .greatly encourages the growth of feathers. To the drinking water there should be added a little sulphate of iron and sulphuric acid, in the proportion of -1-oz. of the former and ten drops of the latter to a quart of water.—Middlesex, in "Farm and Home."</p>
        <p>Eggs, Poultry, &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>KAY -,RIS</head>
        <p>KAY -,RIS</p>
        <p>The new Food for Poultry and Young Animals. (Hargreave's Patent). Makes Birds strong and healthy; eggs plentiful and fertile; lightens trouble and risk in rearing young stock; ensures good constitution; remarkable results.TßY IT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>Thomas MuHook,</head>
        <p>Thomas MuHook,</p>
        <p>PEDIGREE POULTRY FARM, MALPAS.</p>
        <p>The LARGEST in CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>With 300 Trap-nests</p>
        <p>ALL PRINCIPAL LAYING BREEDS KEPT. SPECIAL REDUCTION, UNTIL 30th JUNE, a few tested Layers and unrelated Cockerels from 3/6 to 5/. each.</p>
