      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>DANGER WITH FARM MACHINERY.</p>
        <p>The mechanical features of the farm have I been much improved in recent years; but the human element still remains an absolute necessity; and herein due care should be exer- cised. When a machinery accident occurs on a farm it may involve serious liability; and everything possible should be provided in the way of fencing and safety appliances to prevent such a casualty. New men especially, who are taking the places of skilled and experienced labourers, should be instructed by the prin- cipals as to the risks inherent on the more ex- tensive adoption of power-driven machines. In more than one instance overhead shafting has been used for hoisting heavy sacks, with serious or fatal results. Caution and warning are always cheap, but may be invaluable in their effects.—"'Agricultural Economist and Horti- cultural Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>COOLING MILK.</head>
        <p>COOLING MILK.</p>
        <p>Some useful experiments have been made in milk-cooling in South Australia. At one of the factories three suppliers, living at a distance of from nine to thirteen miles from the factory, were asked to send daily three 10-gallon cans of milk. Two of the cans were to be covered with wet calico, the third being left uncovered. At the close of each evening's milking five gallons of milk were to be put into each can, but one must have the cover saturated with water, placing the cans under similar con- ditions until morning, when the remaining five gallons were to be added, and additional water poured over the same cover, the other two re- maining as before. In conducting the test with the three cans, the milk in the can covered with wet calico had one to three points less acid, and the temperature, which was taken each morn- ing, was from 1 to 4 deg. lower in the covered can; the great difference in temperature pre- vailed when the weather was warm and the sun hot. The results of these experiments have been so satisfactory that this wet envelope system is now generally adopted by the sup- pliers, and, as they themselves say, it prevenls risk of rejection, and saves many a can of milk in a season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>EARLY MOWING.</head>
        <p>EARLY MOWING.</p>
        <p>I have found it a golden rule in times of scarcity of hands to put in the machine in good time, says a writer in "Farm and Home." Better cut a Meadow or two before the -crop has reached full bulk than drive it off so that the last fields get over-ripe, woody in bent, in- nutritious, and not much better than straw. This is precisely what happens where the mow- ing is delayed. The early-cut hay, if got well, is ever the best of all, as waggoner, herdsman and shepherd know well in winter time when they require a " tit-bit " for a sick horse, ail- ing cow, or suffering ewe. By starting early, too, you get the pick of the hands. A few meadows cut down and a few ricks set up in the stackyard before June is out and before your neighbours have made a start, and you are well on with your work whatever happens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>UNNECESSARY CARTING.</head>
        <p>UNNECESSARY CARTING.</p>
        <p>There is sometimes needless labour caused by carting hay further than is absolutely neces- sary. Maybe the crop cut from your top meadow is to be .sold, and, that being so, why cart it half a mile to the rickyard ? Just a few rough mounding thorns or a flight or two of post and rails, which may be put up at leisure and in brief time, th keep stock off the stack, and a lot of haulage may be saved. And what is more important, the carting-in may be done much more rapidly than when the rick and meadow are far apart. How many other cases there are where the work may be lessened for man and horse by stacking ricks, and not too large ones, where the hay is grown or near at hand may well be considered by all, for despatch in clearing a field may save the crop from being spoilt by rain. lam of opinion that we must not keep the hay on the fork quite so much as we should desire, but let the sun un- aided do the making to a considerable extent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>ARE BULBS LUXURIES?</p>
        <p>If the most directly useful industries, from the point of view of the existence of nations, have been the first, even in many neutral countries, to fall victims to the war, should matters not be the same with the trade in luxuries? One would think so. We give the following for the reader to reflect upon: Official statistics have demonstrated that, i 4 spite of the war, the exportation of Dutch bulbs in 1914 only decreased in Holland by 77 metric tons, which is insignificant when we consider the great trade in bulbs. In 1914 the exports amounted to 24.893.900 metric tons, and in 1913 to 24;960,900.—" Agricultural Economist and Horticultural Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>MULCHING.</head>
        <p>MULCHING.</p>
        <p>Mulching is essentially a iihase of surface cultivation, and a most valuable one too, since it ensures the advantages of hoeing or forking, and at the same time keeps the upper layers of roots cool and in a suitable condition for the imbibition of the nutrient matters contained in the dressing. Whether we look at crops in the vegetable, fruit or flower gardens, it will be found that in practically every instance mulch- ing with short sweet manure is beneficial, and all growers should do as rduch of it as possible. In the event of manure not being available, it is nearly always feasible to provide the mowings from the lawn, and these are far better than nothing at all; further than this, most of us have supplies of refuse manure from hot-beds, and these should be requisitioned for .precisely the same purpose, for although they will not convey any appreciable amount of food, they will do what is ,nearly as good, and that is-- ensure all the food that is in the soil passing into the plants instead of escaping into the atmosphere. In some instances the application of a mulching two or three inches in thickness renders the subsequent watering rather more difficult; in this event the simple way out of the trouble is to rake the material back from the immediate neighbourhood of the rows or clumps of plants, give the necessary thorough soaking of water and replace the stuff as soon afterwards as it is wise to tread on the soil.— N. Green.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>CLUSTER CUP FUNGUS.</head>
        <p>CLUSTER CUP FUNGUS.</p>
        <p>A reader sends me a number of pansy shoot.; that have been injured by this fungus, which attacks the cells within the leaf. The spore bearing part of the fungus bursts through the skin of the leaf, and the spores which the little cluster cups contain are distributed over the plants near by. The best way to get rid of this unsightly pest is to pull up all affected plants and burn them, growing the pansies in another part of. the garden next year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>RUNNER BEAKS IN DRY WEATHER.</head>
        <p>RUNNER BEAKS IN DRY WEATHER.</p>
        <p>Few crops in the kitchen garden so quickly feel the effects of a dry time as do runner beans, the plants either failing entirely or the blooms refusing to set To guard against this, we have found it a good plan to take out trenches, to manure these well, and to sow therein. If we adopt the trench system, any water or liquid manure that may be given finds its way to the roots. At the same time it is well to lay a mulch of manure along each side of the trench, and if a plentiful supply of water is afforded. the resulting. crop of pods will be heavy, and a long succession will be kept up. On heavy retentive soils there is not so much need of this attention. Watering is not so 'necessary, the mulch of manure being sufficient to perfect the crop. When beans are sown on the level water- ing is very difficult, in which case we have found it a good plan to draw up a ridge of soil on each side of the row previous to mulching, the water in this way more directly reaching the roots. In these days, when horse manure ,is so very searce, the mowings from the lawn cwne in very useful for mulching vegetable crops when the season, as is now the case, is very dry. For tomatoes growing on walls we have employed these grass mowings to advantage. Very often these lawn mowings are cast on one side as being of little value, but if they are collected in a heap and allowed to decay they will, if mixed with other vegetable refuse, come in very handy for digging into the ground dur- ing the winter.—Wick, in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
