        <p>Preliminary Technical Course (2nd year).— Distinction: John Blomeley, George E. Monks, George H. Webb, Isaac Wilkinson. First class: Ernest Brown, Paul Peters. Second class: Robert Bouffler, Edward Bowden, Arthur Brown, Eric Brawn, Fred Dunbavand, John Fairhurst, Andrew Fleming, Ernest Hutchin- son, James Lamb, James Lowe, William H. Molyneaux, George Pepperell, Walter Rigby, David Smith, Arthur Warburton, Cookery (Principles).—First class: Amy Red- dish. Second class: Agnes Booth, Dora Boscow, Ada Garnett, Lily Leah, Lizzie Moore, Marie Perdue, Lizzie Rigby.</p>
        <p>Cookery (Practic-al).—Second class: -Agnes Booth, Dora Boscow, Ada Garnett, Lily Leah, Lizzie Moore, Marie Perdue, Amy Reddish, Lizzie Rigby, Hannah Winstanley. Dressmaking (Intermediate) .—First class: Dora Boscow, Alice Hayes, Priscilla Hill, Ethel Hunt, Violet Lea, Lily 'Leah, Maggie Morrall, Lena Nixon, Cecily Ryan, Mary Ryan, Florence B. Shaw, Nellie Smith, Plicebe Smith, May Walker. Second class: Ada Bennett, Agnes Booth, Annie Booth. Jane B. Brierley. Mabel Burns, Marion Delahaye. Annie Forsyth; Ada Garnett, Marie Hardy, Winifred Malone, Eliza-</p>
        <p>beth Moore, Eva Nixon, Marie Perdue, Amy Reddish, Elizabeth Rigby, Ellen Sharpies, Emily Webb, Hilda Webb, Ethel Wright, Harriet Williams.</p>
        <p>Dressmaking (Advanced).—First class: Ger- trude Jones, Lily Shaw, Elizabeth Wilcock. Second class: Elizabeth Brookes, Ellen Burrows, Annie Chile, Amy Cragg, Ellen Dobson, Alice Fairhurst, Doris Garnett, Lauretta Hayes, Annie Hitchmough, Rosa Hodson, Elizabeth Holmes, Amy Lowe, Ellen McCormack, Ella M. Morley. Ada Phillips, Dora Smallwood, Bessie Speakman, Lily Sudlow, Ellen Tavlin, Agnes Vernon, Annie Webb, Ethel Whittaker, Annie Williams, Edith A. Yates. Elements of Home Nursing.—Second class: Elizabeth Brookes, Ellen Burrows, Doris Gar- nett, Rosa Hodson, Elizabeth Holmes, Amy Holt, Ada Phillips, Lily Shaw, Dora Smallwood, Ethel Whittaker, Annie Williams. Millinery (A4dvanced).—Distinction : Bessie Speakman. First class: Annie Chile, Amy ,Cragg, Alice Fairhurst, Amy Holt, Gertrude Jones, Rose Lawton, Maggie Morrall, Elsie M. Morris. Amy Reddish, Lily E. M. Shaw, Eliza- beth Willcock, Annie Williams. Second class: Lucy Ashurst, Hannah M. Booth, Dora Boscow. Jane B. Brierley, Elizabeth Brookes, Mabel Burns, Ellen Burrows, Ellen Dobson, Annie For- syth, Ada Garnett, Doris Garnett, Alice Hayes, Annie Hayes, Priscilla Hill, Annie Hitchmough, Rosa Hodson, Elizabeth Holmes, Ethel Hunt, Eva Jervis, Violet Lea, Lily Leah, Alice V. Lowe, Amy Lowe, Winifred Malone, Ellen McCormack, Elizabeth Moore, Marie Perdue, Ada Phillips, Elizabeth Rigby, Cecily- Ryan, Ellen Sharples, Florence B. „Shaw, Dora Small- wood, Phcebe Smith, Lily Sudlow. Agnes Ver- non. Ada Wallington, Annie Webb. Emily Webb, Hilda Webb, Ethel Whittaker, Lily Will- cock, Harriet Williams, Edith A. Yates. Needlework (Advaneed).—Distinction : Mar- garet Morrall. First class: Priscilla Hill, Ethel Hunt, Violet Lea, Nellie Smith. Second class: Annie Booth, Jane Brierley, Marion Delahave, Annie Forsyth. Alice Haves. Eva Jervis, Eva Nixon, Lena Nixon, Cicely Ryan, Mary Ryan, Florence Shaw, Hilda Webb.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>WALKING DAY.</head>
        <p>WALKING DAY.</p>
        <p>What The Different Churches Are Doing This Week-end.</p>
        <p>A PROCESSION WITH A PURPOSE.</p>
        <p>Altholigh Walking Day proper has been abandoned this year, some of the Churches are going to make the most of the remnant and give their children as good a time as the im- posed restrictions will permit.</p>
        <p>We have already, in a previous article, ex- pressed our opinion of the spirit that prompted the Catholics to total eclipse of the festival, So far as they were concerned, tlt.e same spirit, in a lesser degree, that, actuated a number of Anglicans in advocating the turning of the chil- dren's big treat into a mournful, musicless march, the sole justification for the holding of which would be the inspiration of the towns- people with a sense of their frivolity and of the need for solemnity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>SACKCLOTH AND SASHES.</head>
        <p>SACKCLOTH AND SASHES.</p>
        <p>Failing to see that the people had, by displays of extravagant gaiety or thoughtless selfishness, merited the suggestion that they needed chastening or subduing, we said so, at the risk of incurring the displeasure, of those who instigated the insinuation. We hold to our guns on the point, having no reason to desert them, but even supposing, for the sake of argu- ment, that some such beneficent influence were necessary to cure the citizens of Warrington of butterfly propensities; we do not admit that it would be a justifiable course of action for the Church authorities, or anyone else, to rout out all their reserves of crepe and sackcloth on the day which, by right of old custom, belongs to the children and -childish delights, to flags and frolic, to bands and banners and bunting. We have not heard that the girls have been told to walk without their pretty sashes, but we hope they have not. These things mean a lot to their wearers, and ought not to be discarded to satisfy the sackcloth spirit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>NONCONFORMISTS DIFFER.</head>
        <p>NONCONFORMISTS DIFFER.</p>
        <p>Without wishing to cast doubt on anyone's sincerity in the matter, we contend that Walk- ing Day was 'not the best day of the three hundred and sixty-five to settle on for a demonstration of solemnity, even if such a demonstration were needed. However, the arrangements are made, although the impres- sive demonstration wa§ not fixed for Friday, which should have been Walking Day, but the following Sunday (to-morrow). The Anglicans are having processions ,in the various parishes, and the Catholics are holding intercessory ser- vices. The processions will halt at various points and hymns will be sung. The Catholics are being consistent to the bitter end, and withholding all semblance of a treat from their little ones, whereas the Anglicans are com- promising and holding field-treats this after-- noon like the Nonconformists, who are openly making the best they can of the curtailed festival, and apparently do not deem it neces- sary to awe the people. Perhaps they are wrong, but we think they are wise. We are quite prepared to be accused of heart- less talk and told to remember that there is war in progress in which hundreds of thousands of our gallant sons are engaged. Whose sons and husbands and brothers are they who are fighting? The sons and husbands and brothers of the very people whom a well- meaning but misguided minority wish to im- press with the fact that, everything in the garden is not quite lovely! And what do these brave sons and husbands and brothers say about it? Do they cry for the stoppage of the children's pleasures? Not they. Judging from the messages from the Front referring to Walking Day, these men are proud to feel that it is what tliey have done and are doing that made a 1915 Walking Day possible, and -we have reason to believe that none, not even the youngsters themselves, will be more dis- appointed than Sir Thomas of trie Trenches that the affair, so 'far as Warrington, is con- cerned, has been clipped beyond recognition. As we mentioned in our previous article on the sul?ject, money as well as good wishes for Walking Day has come from the Front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>ROYAL AGRICULTURAL SHOW.</head>
        <p>ROYAL AGRICULTURAL SHOW.</p>
        <p>Local Winners.</p>
        <p>This year's Royal Agricultural Show is being held this week at Nottingham, and promises to be a greater success than the prevailed condi- tions would seem to demand. Although the entries in all departments are fewer and a tour of the showground can be accomplished in less time than in other years, it still takes many hours to gain even a general impression of all the interesting -exhibits that are spread over the 104 acres of the ground. The King was a highly successful exhibitor. The Prince 'of Wales made his first entry into the ranks of Royal Show exhibitors, but the Shorthorn bull from his farm at Stoke Clims- land, in Cornwall, was not quite up to winning standard.</p>
        <p>, In the hunter and hackney classes, Mr. H. Hinrich4en, of Over Peover, Knutsford, won a championship with his mare Lady Beckingham. Mr. Henry Gilding, of Gateacre, Liverpool, was a first prize-winner with Tissington Horden, and Sir Lees Knowles secured a second with his stallion Salford Victor.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Lousada, Higher Walton, Warrington; Mr. G. Norris Midwood, of North Rode, Congle- ton ; and Miss G. W. Samuel, of Woodbank Hall, Chester, Were successful exhibitors of polo ponies.</p>
        <p>In the hunter classes, Mr. Gerald Lonsada, Higher Walton, secured a second prize. Mr. H. ,Hinrichsen, of Over Peover, gained a 'second prize with a novice harness gelding, In the section for pigs there was little varia- tion from previous years. The entries were only 57 lower than last year; and the large whites, which Sir Gilbert Greenall and other breeders have improved so wonderfully, made up the most numerous classes. Sir Gilbert Greenall was awarded several prizes, and in one class he secured the first, second, and third.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>GARTONS PROMINENT.</head>
        <p>GARTONS PROMINENT.</p>
        <p>On a large and beautifully arranged stand, Messrs. Gartons, Limited, the well-known Agricultural Seed Experts, of Warri'ngton, I made an imposing display of their new and regenerated breeds of agricultural plants. A new breed of white oat they are introducing for 1916, named "The Hero," occupied a very prominent position, and was greatly admired by the numerous visitors. Their new breed of wheat benefactor introduced last year for the first time also occupied a prominent position, and created considerable interest amongst wheat growers. A beautiful display of speci- mens of many of Messrs. Garton's introductions of cereals were exhibited on the stand from the home farms of His Majesty the King, His Grace the Duke of Westminster, the Earl of Coventry, and 'many other of the leading agri- culturists of this country. A miscellaneous collection of grasses, clovers and other agri- cultural seeds all go up to make a comprehen- sive and imposing display.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>FARMERS' WAR DUTIES.</head>
        <p>FARMERS' WAR DUTIES.</p>
        <p>At the general meeting of members of the Royal Agricultural Society, held On the show ground, the increasing importance of agricul- ture under the conditions produced by the war was touched upon by several speakers, and there was a general agreement that farmers could do much to fortify the nation in the stern te§t of endurance which lies before it, and that their special task in the struggle was to increase the food supply by cultivating cereals and root crops and producing live stock to the extreme limit of their capacity. The Duke of Portland, who pre- sided, told the farmers that they, could have no doubt where their duty lay in the national emergency. It was to use all their skill and energy for the production .and conservation of food. For thatreason alone he held they were justified in holding the show and striving to , make it as successful as they could. He ex- pressed regret that the King would not be able to pay his customary visit, but his Majesty was warmly thankedlor manifesting continued,- in, terest in the show by sending so many •fine ex- hibits.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>THE EMPTY BASSINETTE.</p>
        <p>How A Drunken Man Attracted A Constable's Attention.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Monday morning—before the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), Aldermen Pierpoint and James Evans, and Messrs. S. Wright, G. Pilling, W. Roberts, W. Furness and T. Locker—William George Blackwell, of 27, Barnard-street, was charged with having been drunk in Market- street on Saturday and, further, with having stolen a bass,inette, valued at three guineas, the property of Mr. Sutton, Market-street.—ln answer to the first charge he said "I knew nothing until I woke on Sunday morning" and, in answer, to the second, "I don't remember." The Chief Constable told the Court that at about 9.20 on Saturday night P.C. Fish was on duty in Market-street, when his attention was drawn to the prisoner by the fact that he was drunk and was wheeling an empty bassinette. The officer arrested him and found that the bassinette had been stolen from Sutton's shop. When charged the man said "I only took the ' pram ' for a joke !" Whilst the man was in custody half a dozen bobbins of silk- fell from his pocket, and they were picked up by a boy and handed to the police officer. It "looked as if the man had been out on some sort of an expedition !" The prisoner was a stranger to Warrington, and was working at Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co.'s; he had only been there a few weeks. He was lodging with a brother. A letter had been received from his employers that morning, asking .that the man might be dealt with leniently as he was engaged on work that was urgently needed for military hospitals. As to the silk found on the man, the police took no action that day, as no owner had been found. Prisoner was fined 255., or 14 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>ASSAULT AT BANK QUAY.</head>
        <p>ASSAULT AT BANK QUAY.</p>
        <p>Young Boilerman Sentenced For Attack On Ticket-Collector.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. J. W. Hayward (in the chair) and Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes and W. T. Williams, at the Borough Police Court on Wednesday morning, John Davies, a boilerman, of sp, Hume-street, Warrington, was charged with being drunk and disorderly at Bank Quay Station, on June 29th, and asaulting a ticket- collector named Thomas Thomas, and P.C. Coombes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>"To A Certain Extent."</head>
        <p>"To A Certain Extent."</p>
        <p>Asked if he pleaded guilty, prisoner, a respectable-looking young fellow, said: "To a certain extent."</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said tie charges were of a serious character. The prisoner was on a platform at the low level, station on the night in question and was asked by Thomas what he was doing. Prisoner replied that he was seeing a friend off to Scotland. *hereupon the collec- tor told him he was on the wrong platform, and told him where to go. Prisoner told his friend, who was in the train, that the collector was coming to see his ticket. It was just after that the assault' took place. When P.C. Coombes came on, the scene, prisoner assaulted him. The ticket collector had to go to the doctor and have his head bandaged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Knocked Senseless on the Floor.</head>
        <p>Knocked Senseless on the Floor.</p>
        <p>Thomas Thomas gave evidence as to being on the platform from which the 11.20 mail train was about to depart. In reply to a question, prisoner said that he was seeing a friend off to Glasgow. When witness told him it was the wrong train for Scotland, prisoner said his friend was not going to Scotland, but to New- castle. Witness was about to step into the carriage to examine the ticket of prisoner's friend, when he was struck by prisoner from behind, and knocked senseless on the floor of the carriage. Witness gave prisoner no pro- vocation whatever for the assault. In the assault, witness had a pair of gold-rimmed spec- tacles, of the value of 55., broken.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>Took Three Men to Hold Him.</head>
        <p>Took Three Men to Hold Him.</p>
        <p>P.C. Coombes told how he was called to the scene and was assaulted by prisoner, when he attempted to arrest him. Prisoner was drunk, and witness had to obtain the assistance of a sailor and Foreman Hill before he could get his man in hand. The Other Side.</p>
        <p>Prisoner stated that he was seeing a friend off to Newcastle and asked the ticket collector which was the best --part of the train for his friend to travel in, as he had just come out of hospital, and did not want to change if it could be avoided. - The collector replied: "Further up; further up," and then proceeded to allege that he (prisoner) had said his friend was going to Scotland. He denied that he had said .Scotland. lie said Newcastle. The collec- tor called him a liar threettimes. "I won't be called a liar by anybody when I'm not," prisoner added. "That was the reason why he was struck. If he had kept a civil tongue in his head there would have been nothing wrong." The Clerk: How about the constable? Prisoner: Oh, well, one thing led to another. The Chairman, in announcing that the Bench had decided to inflict a sentence .of two months' hard labour, said that they considered it a serious matter.</p>
        <p>During the reign of William and Mary bachelors and widowers over 25 years were taxed one shilling yearly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>letters to the Odor</head>
        <p>letters to the Odor</p>
        <p>PATRIOT has omitted to enclose his name and</p>
        <p>address.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>BANK PARK BAND PERFORMANCES.</head>
        <p>BANK PARK BAND PERFORMANCES.</p>
        <p>• Sir,—l, along with friends, went to hear a pro- gramme of music given by that fine band of Messrs. Crosfield, for a very deserving cause: the welfare of our wounded soldiers. Talk about trying to hear the music! At times it was an impossibility, owing to the behaviour of boys and girls jostling one another. I should like to know why the chairs cannot be used during band performances, instead of being stowed away. Whoever is responsible for the seating accommodation,- I cannot say. The present ar- rangements are anything but satisfactory. The least they could do is to find seats for our wounded soldiers, as I noticed last Sunday dur- ing the evening performance many of them sit- ting down on the damp grass. Referring to the conduct of young people during band perform- ances, one cannot blame the police under the present circumstances, as the remedy lies with the public themselves. _Hoping something will be done to remedy the above. A MUSIC LOVER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>LORD DERBY AND COMMISSIONS.</head>
        <p>LORD DERBY AND COMMISSIONS.</p>
        <p>• Sir,—l receive many applications from men anxious to take commissions in his Majesty's service, but whose names I am unable to put forward as they have no military experience. I have now come to an arrangement with Colonel Errington, commanding the Inns of Court, an Officers' Training Corps duly authorised by the War Office, to take suitable Lancashire men and train them for commis- sions.</p>
        <p>The conditions are as follows :—Men must be under 35 and must have been at a public ar other well-known school. They must be approved of by Colonel Errington, and after approval Undergo a three months' course:They must undertake to give the first offer of their services when their military education is com- pleted to Lancashire regiments; but, if not required, would have liberty to offer their ser- vices elsewhere. The first two or three weeks of the training is in London, after that at Berk- hamstetid ; but in the case of boys joining from school arrangements will be made that they shall go straight to Berkhamstead and have the whole of their training there instead of part of it in London.</p>
        <p>Although I am unable to promise that at the end of their course they shall be given com- missions, I will personally undertake, to the best of my ability, to secure for them com- missions either in Lancashire regiments or, if they are full, in regiments elsewhere. I would ask that any gentleman wishing and qualified to join this corps should write to me. Putting on the envelope of their letter "Officers' Training Corps." Any men now serving in the ranks of Lanca- shire regiments—Kitchener or Territorial— will be eligible for inclusion in the. scheme pro- vided they fulfil the conditions laid down above and receive their commanding officer's approval. Yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>DERBY. Knowsley, Preseot, Lancahje, June 25, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>WELL DONE! WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WELL DONE! WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Whenever I take up "The Examiner nowadays, I am filled with admiration of the military ardour which my younger fellow- townsmen have displayed. Their bravery, their sufferings, and even their deaths, fill me with pride, for the handsome manner in which they responded to the call of their King and country. Everywhere it is the same, and here, in the centre of things, we see very little else, than the military side of the nation. In Lon- don, every square, every park, every spacious open-air place of amusement, has been given over ta be training of men, and Our streets and and trains, are ADO Vdtli• roads, our ve,liCie: khaki-clad men. Even in the country, the same state of things prevails. At almost every village in Surrey men are billeted. On the roads, too, regiments are to be encountered. I recently cycled into the heart of Sussex, to see a sick friend of mine from the North (who. unfortunately, died soon after), and, when got immediately outside East Grinstead, which is just within the Sussex boundary, I overtook a yeomanry squadron, which had been foraging in the town. A little further on, going towards Ashdown Forest, I overtook another company of mounted - men. The road through the forest, which is usually of excellent sur- face, was a foot or more deep in mud, and I had to walk a considerable distance, which I have hitherto been able to ride. Outside of Uckfield I passed an artillery regiment. Leav- ing I,Tckfield, I had a good run to the residence of my -friend, which was beauti- fully situated amongst the Downs. In coming home, I rode through Tunbridge Wells, and had almost got through the town before I re- membered that many of my younger townsmes were quartered in the place. I pulled up at a large house, in which soldiers were billeted. However, it was getting late, and as I was on a 100 miles run, I considered it prudent to push on towards home, but I resolved that would take an early opportunity of revisiting Tunbridge Wells. This I hope to do before long. Recruiting meetings are very frequent. and on Friday last there was in Croydon a grand demonstration, with a procession, composed of various arms of the service. Croydon has made a very 0,0 response to the call to arms, and its townsmen are now in India, Egypt and at the Front. Even in the little place where I live there has been an excellent response, nearly 700 of its residentg baying joined the colours, and in the next town' ship, which is much smaller, nearly everi! available man is under arms. About a we ago a recruiting meeting was held in the place. but there was not a single response, from th's fact already stated, that everybody had joine° the Army who was fit to do so.</p>
        <p>Still, I realise the handsome contribution which Lancashire has made to the call to arnls. and. I feel that no town ha's made a better response or greater sacrifices than my ow° native town. I een only conclude as I began' with the words "Well done! Warrinzton."__</p>
        <p>London, June 29th, 1915.</p>
