      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>PUSH AND GO WITH EGGS.</head>
        <p>PUSH AND GO WITH EGGS.</p>
        <p>In the national stocktaking which has takell place as, a- preliminary to placing our house order a weakness has been revealed in Inipr than one direction. Take the poultry industrY• When we realise the fact that, according to 1,1 leading expert, there is only about one hen Pe' acre in Great Britain, and that in 1913 01 national bill for imported eggs •total le( £9,590,602, it will be conceded thet we llas-e lacked " pugh and go" in this important sectior: of food production. Unless we mend our the outlook is serious, for hitherto we largely depended upon Russia, ,Germany. r gium, France, Italy and Austria-HungarY our supplies, Russia being by far the large.d contributor.—" Agricultural Economist an 'Horticultural Review."</p>
        <p>Couldn't Do It. Are you raising your baby'seientifie.allY "No. That would be impossible: His gran ' mother lives with us."</p>
        <p>(fljurrij $/eius.</p>
        <p>EVER SO AI V\ I. PRISONERS AS</p>
        <p>NOV.</p>
        <p>Rev. Professor Armitage On The Bitterness Of Captil it 3</p>
        <p>At Wycliffe Congregational Church on Sun- day, the services were conducted by the Rey. Professor Armitage, of Bradford—brother of Mr. Ziba, Armitage, of Preston Brook. In the course of his evening sermon, which was based on the story of St. Paul's imprisonment, the preacher remarked that in all history there had never been such a multitude of prisoners as now. The mind Could not follow the things Which were .out vf sight, and they could not enter into the bitterness and the sorrow of the thousands and thousands of Englishmen, Bel- gians, Frenchmen, Germans and Russians now languishing in captivity, embittered by the scorn of the gaoler and darkened by the menalfes of the future. Paul's chains, however, did not prevent his being the freest man in - Europe. So the Church must be faithful and sing in the night time, and they the agents of the providence of God. Guided by His Spirit and His infinite love they had to be ministers of healing and mercy and interpreters of love. He was seeking to bring in his kingdom. People said: "It will come in God's time." It Was .the preacher's duty to say "God's time is now."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>TIII: IMPOTENT CHURCH.</head>
        <p>TIII: IMPOTENT CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Some Itea,,ims "%Vliy The People Are</p>
        <p>Aid Attracted.</p>
        <p>They lacked for nothing, so far as this world's goods were concerned; and yet John reminded them that they were poor. They needed to possess the riches that never corrode. And had the Church always been faithful to that Purpose and ideal, maybe—nay, very prob- ably—we should not have been in this world deluge we are experiencing to-day. . . The world gives more colour to the Church than the Church gives to the world.</p>
        <p>The above is an extract, from -it sermon preached by the Rev. 11. Ogden, at GoMonte- street Baptist Church on Sunday. The preacher's text was taken from the 3rd Chapter of Revelations, 18th verse:—" I counsel thee to buy of me gold tried in the :fire, that thou mayeSt be rich; and white. raiment, that thou mayest be clothed, and that the shame of thy nakedness do not appear; and anoint thine eyes with eye salve, that thou mayest see."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>Well-bred Repose.</head>
        <p>A Well-bred Repose.</p>
        <p>The subject of the letter (the Rev. H. Ogden said) was the Church of Laodicea and the Laodicean Christians. John complained that they were neither hot nor cold. They were so polite that they became icy-towards the work of God. They were .accustomed to a well-bred repose, and shunned as a plague anything but the orthodox and respectable. John could have understood them if they had been either passionately for or passionately against; but they had passions neither way, and John had to contend with a Church that sat on the fence. What was true of the Laodiceans was, unfor- tunately, true of the Church to-day. If John 'we ere ,olive, with his virile methods, he would co-retain that the Church was neither hot nor he • 'or example, most Christians would not 4e en at an outdoor meeting. it their pastors were • conducting outdoor Services, the people 11-°llld Pass them • by, as if they were total ranges or else, with a quizzical appearance, xeLairn: "What's, their purpose and what's their Work?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>If the Church Had Been Faithful.</head>
        <p>If the Church Had Been Faithful.</p>
        <p>_ The Church of Laodieca failed because it was Without passion, and if the Church failed t0(41.Y (41.Y it was for exactly the same reason. John eounselled those people to buy , gold that was tried in the fire. His advice was all the more Pertinent because the Church was a wealthy Church. They had no poor members of their congregation. - - They •attended Church in the height of fashion, and their minister—if a minister they had—would be a well-paid mini!;- ter. They, lacked for nothing, so far' as this World's goods were concerned; and yet John reminded them that they were poor. They needed to possess the riches ,that never cor- roded. And had the Church always been faith- Sul to that purpose and ideal, maybe—nay, very probably—we should not have been in the world deluge we were experiencing to-day. aohii.further advised the Laodieeans to clothe themselves in white raiment to cover their nakedness. There was no insult to a well- dressed woman like that of being called a dude. 'FO tell individuals dressed in the height of fashion that all their tastes were crude was -1-0 invite life-long enmity. The Laodicean Christians were elegantly attired. Cashmeres. delaines and silks were at their disposal: and Probably the Church on mornings when there 'were special services, were a sight to be seen. And yet John told the people that they were baked, and that they must cover their naked- es s with white raiment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>Eye That Does Not See.</head>
        <p>The Eye That Does Not See.</p>
        <p>In all the pictures of angelic life, the artist robod the angels in White. Any colour would clash with the idea of the ankelic; and one felt that if raiment were worn' by the'ongels it 'would be raiment of white. Johh would have the Church an emblem of spotless purity. There Iras• little doubt that the world had invaded the Church and that the world gave more rolour to the Church than the Church gave to the world. Its colours were garish• and often loud; and if the modern Church of Europe had ,been content with the white and seamless robe, ilovr mightily she would have influenced the People! The apostle's final word to the taodiceans was that they should anoint their eves that they might be opened. John believed that that Church had eyes and saw not. Prob- as the Church. boasted of its wealth .and nk and status, its members also were, proud "d vain of *their beauty—brown eyes, blue c'Yes, dark eyes, light eyes; with featares that 1% luxury luxury and time could chisel. Of all hat, John said they Were vain. Their eyes Were open and they saw</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>Mind Determines the Vision</head>
        <p>The Mind Determines the Vision</p>
        <p>MO. trUe was that in all walks of life! The llntwird eye was the mere retina; it was the eye thi°ll:itid that mattered. A man might yawn in - most select picture gallery and 'regard it 4111 as a waste of good time. A woman adored LIP.. Brussels lace and Mechlin lace, not ;1)1(701 3' for what it cost, but because .of its ant,v• Probably a man would reckon it up cash value. The military expert ,gbed Men !in with a view to an d armY—life- ,-nar s eTen d' artillery. It was the mind . iers, und the eye that determined what a man ''aw• The slave owner looked on • the slave and</p>
        <p>measured his points as a farmer would measure a horse at a fair. But the more penetrating mind could see in the soft light of the negro's eye the possibilities of a saint. A Great Opportunity.</p>
        <p>John desired the Church never to be content with less than realities. Vain show, empty pomp, extravagant custom all marred and maimed the work of the Church. To be in accord with respectable opinion, to associate merely for association's sake, with society to- day, even to re-echo the military gospel, this was to lead the Church to be impotent and futile, and it had been done so. When—and even before—the war came to a close, the Church must do some readjusting. Men who return from the Front would demand the heroic and unselfish bravery. and if, ,when they returned, they found the Church still content to array herself in colours, to be vain about her beauty, he would be disappointed, and a great oppor- tunity would be for ever lost.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>WESLEYAN BELGIAN HOME.</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN BELGIAN HOME.</p>
        <p>Enjo3able Garden Par Iv At The</p>
        <p>Priory.</p>
        <p>One of the many organisations which are the outcome of Wesleyan Methodist sympathies for those suffering because of this war is a Belgian Home, situated in Palmyra-square.</p>
        <p>This institution is contrlled by the Wesleyan Bible Classes, and contains fourteen refugees, each of whom has lost all owing to Germany's violation of international treaties.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon, in response to the kind invitation of Miss.. Dring, the energetic secretary of the.liome Committee, a number of friends joined the Belgians at a garden party in the grounds of the Priory. Despite the great difficulty which differences of language set up, the company was very soon, "at home," and .even •those whose French was worse than a minus quantity were able to carry on a conversation with their Belgian friends. Tea wal.i served in the large schoolroom over- looking the tennis lawn, after Which- the com- pany indulged in games. A musical programme consisting of solos and patriotic choruses bad been arranged by Miss Dring. Miss M. Whit- more sang. " Land- of Hope and Glory," and Miss Emily Taylor rendered "There's a Land." Miss Collin kindly accompanied. The Belgian national anthem was sung in Flemish by the Belgian friends, and the com- pany joined in singing "La Marseillaise." Not the least interesting item Was the singing of " Tipperary" by four little Belgian girls. The Rev. W. T. Derry proposed a .vote of thanks to Mr. and Miss Dring for providing so pleasant an afternoon, and he also expressed his pleasure at meeting his Belgian friends. M. 'Etienne, speaking on behalf of the Bel- gians, thanked the English people, and espe- cially the people Of Warrington, for their great kindness to the homeless refugees. He also felt deeply grateful ,to Miss Dring for her sym- pathy and far inviting them to such an enjoy- able gathering. Photographs of the company were taken by Mr. Burrell, and after a very enjoyable after- noon the party broke up. The practical sym- pathies of the Wesleyan friends were much strengthened by the actual association with their unfortunate Belgian friends, and all were impressed with their worthiness and apprecia- tion of what is done for them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>SOLDIERS' HOME.</head>
        <p>SOLDIERS' HOME.</p>
        <p>A Comfortable Meeting Place</p>
        <p>For Our Warriors.</p>
        <p>On Monday the Bold-street School will be opened (by kind permission of the Trustees) as a place of meeting for all soldiers, particularly the disabled ones, for rest, recreation and social intercourse. It will be Practically a club free of charge for the brave men who have fought our battles, and for those who are going to fight for us in the future.</p>
        <p>Papers, magazines, billiards, bagatelle, and other games, together with writing materials, will be provided. Occasionally, musical items will be rendered, and everything possible will be done to make this " home " a place where the men may spend their time comfortably. The " Soldiers' Home" is for soldiers only, and will be open every weekday from 2 p.m. to 9 p.m., and on Sunday eveningsfrom 7.45 to 9</p>
        <p>• The committee are assured by the 'men them- selves that this :will be a real boon to them,. especially the disabled ones, who often feel the need of a place in town where they may spend half an hour, or a few hours, with their "pals" in their own way.</p>
        <p>The scheme is expected to cost, roughly, about ot3oo .a year, and the committee, knowing that the townspeople are fully conscious of their indebtedness to the soldiers, believe they will gladly meet this cost. The home will be entirely unsectarian. Any funds in hand when the time comes to close the home will be handed over to the Infirmary.</p>
        <p>. Mr. Wm. Isaac, 72, Bewsey-street, Warring- ton, is .the bon. treasurer, and he will be glad to receive weekly, monthly, or quaiTerly con- tributions. Mr. A. Lomb, 302, Manchester- road, is the lion. secretary. The committee is composed of the following: The Revs. W. Thompson Derry, A. J. Morton and Hugh Wil- liamson, Messrs. J. G. Withinshaw, F. Hick- man, G. Weaver, J. W. Sherlock; J. C. Holland, Alderman Bennett, F. Harris, F. Pendlebury, George E. Guest, A. Lumb and Wm. Isaac. They will be assisted by a committee of ladies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>• • ,„ , Vhddren 8 evorner.l</head>
        <p>• • ,„ , Vhddren 8 evorner.l</p>
        <p>to .:. .:. 4. Conducted by UNCLE NED. 4. + .:. .0. .:. 4..:.,.:. .:. .:. 4. .:. .:.+ + + 4.4. .:. 4! .:. .:. .:. .:. 4,</p>
        <p>CHILDREN Or ALL AGES are cordially invited to join this Corner if they read it regularly. Application for membership, stating name, address and age, should be made to "UNCLE NED, 'Examiner' Office, Bewsey- street, Warrington." Every girl or boy, whose name is in the Big Book, is entitled to enter the competitions held every week, to the winners of which useful book-prizes are sent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>THIS WEEK'S ESSAY: THE SCHOOL I</head>
        <p>THIS WEEK'S ESSAY: THE SCHOOL I</p>
        <p>ATTEND.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR BOYS APD GIRLS,— • I am- quite sure that this week's competition will be one to your liking, and one in which you will enter just as enthusiastically as you did in last week's: 1 want you each to pen me a line or two upon:. " THE SCHOOL I Go To," and send them.. along to me before. Tuesday. Either day school .or Sunday school will do; I don't mind which in. the least. So don't for- get! Monday is. the .closidg day, and "Every- body In" is the motto!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>NUMBER, PLEASE?</head>
        <p>NUMBER, PLEASE?</p>
        <p>My faithful nevhew, JOSEPH KITCHEN, of 41, Bewsey-street, writes to ask what his number is. It is 356, Joseph. Nephew ERNEST. POTTER writes:— " 72, Scott-street, Warrington, June 27th, 1915,—Dear Uncle Ned,—l beg to tell you how pleased I am for 'enlisting' my new 'recruit,' Thomas Ashton, in your, regi- ment of nephews. You have told him his number; so please do not forget mine in our • little corner of "rhe Examiner.' next week." Your number, ERNEST, is 447. Thanks very much for the letter..</p>
        <p>A nicely-worded letter comes from 456, Nephew Thomas Ashton :--</p>
        <p>"76, Scott-street, WarringtOn, .27th June, 1915,—Dear Uncle Ned,—Just a line thank- ing you for giving me the privilege of entering your happy corner. I have read 'The Examiner' for several weeks, and I am much interested in the Children's</p>
        <p>Corner; I promise that I will enter your competitions 'and try to win prizes.—From your Nephew, THOMAg ASHTON.—P.S.—I enclose this week's competition, with many thanks."</p>
        <p>That's all right, Tom. I shall watch your progress with great interest, and wish you capital luck! ECHOES OF THE SPORTS.</p>
        <p>Two letters which reached me later ,on last week mention the _sports. I think you would be interested to read them, so here they are : "School Brow Dairy, 'Warrington, June 21st, 1915,—Dear Uncle Ned,—l wish to thank you for the .handsome book which you so kindly sent me this afternoon. I was greatly surprised on Saturday iyhen.:l found that I was the prize winner in my first competition. . "I went to the school sports on Monday afternoon with my friend, who was com- peting in two races. But she did not suc- ceed in winning a prize. Many girls from Fairfield Girls' School (which I attend) won prizes. We spent a most enjoyable time and returned home about seven o'clock. "Again thanking you for your kind pre- sent, rfremain, your loving • Niece, • 450, 'FRA'NCES ROBERTS." The second letter—which has also pleased .me awfully—comes from my 365th member:— " 68, Steel-street, Warrington,—Dear Uncle Ned,—l have joined the Children's Corner some time back, • but I have entered but one competition, . but I cannot resist writing you a letter on the sports. I did go to the grounds, but I was not in any races. We were low in points at first, but gradually gained. At the last, when the prizes were distributed, you could not imagine my joy when the judge called out Beamont Council School girls have won the shield.' Unfortunately our boys were .one point off; but then the had it last year. and if they had have won it they could not have had it, but there is the honour of winning it. I was glad to hear last Friday morning that Lily Goodwin (a girl Out of my class) along with another girl, was the champion. I must close now. —I am, your affectionate niece; LUCY CARET."</p>
        <p>A capital letter, Lucy! I am sorry it came too late to be judged. I notice that your hand- writing is just as good as it was when first you joined. This I am pleased to see, and it applies also to every one of my other letters received this week.</p>
        <p>A pleasing note comes from 457, Nephew ALFRED LONG, to say : "79, Orford-avenue, Warrington, 26th June, 1915,—Dear Uncle Ned,—Many thanks for the cheery manner in which you have received me in your 'Corner.' The two prizes which I won at the sports are: A fountain pen ,and a writing case. The first named was for the Flag Squadron,' and the, latter for the Tug-of-War."</p>
        <p>Heartily, Alfred, I congratulate you on your success. You carry credit to your -school as well as to our Cotner.</p>
        <p>WHO THEY WOULD BE.</p>
        <p>And now for the result of last week's com- petition. I have been delighted by the number of entries, and of course you have all chosen to do something in connection with the war. Now that is a good choice, because it is a patriotic choice. But. you must remember that patriotism is not the greatest thing in the world—not anywhere near the greatest. .And I should have liked so much to see many of you thinking of and hankering after those greater things. However, this is a war as much ,for religion as for patriotism; and there is nothing greater than religion. So I must not and won't be downhearted. The prize goes to 380, NIECE JENNIE M. DUNLOP,</p>
        <p>who sent the following leiter, beautifully written:—</p>
        <p>"52, Chester-road, Warrington, 26th June, 1915,—Dear Uncle Ned,—You have asked us to write a composition on Who I should like to be if I were not myself.' Well, if I had to be somebody different I should like to be the illustrious lady, Florence Nightingale. My delight would be in tending to the wounded soldiers in the hospitals. This famous nurse was born at Florence, in Italy, in 1823. Her parents were wealthy and well connected, having residences at .Embly Park, Hampshire, and Leigh Hurst, Derbyshire. She first devoted her attention to hospitals, visiting and examining the civil and military hospitals of the different countries of Europe. On . returning to London she founded -a sana- torium or hospital. Hoping you are well. With love from your loving neice, JENNIE DUNLOP. age 11." Nephew ERNEST POTTER wrote: "You ask me ',Who would I like to be if I weren't myself?' If you had asked, me who I would not like to be' I would have quickly answered The Kaiser!' But my reply to the former would be I The</p>
        <p>An Interesting Scotch Proverb. "Bread ii;the staff of life, but the pudding makes a good crutch "!—that is if made with ATORA Beef Suet. More digestible and economical than if you use raw suet. Hid. per 11b. carton, whether block or shredded, and Md. per ilb. carton. Ask your grocer for it; refuse substitutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>•:••:••:.:••:••:.•:•.:-:-:••:-:••:-:.•:,.:•.:.•:-:-:.•:-:-:-:-:••:-:-:-:••:••:-:••:-:-:-:,•:-:••:-:-.:-:..:••:-:••:••:,•:-:•.:4•:-:-:••:-:••:-:-:••:••:••:••:••:-:- .:4•:••:-:-:-:-:-:••:••:..).:-:-:-:•.:-:-:.•:-:•,:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:•.:-:••:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:••:•.&gt;•:••:-:••:-:-:-:-:-:-:••:-:-:••:-:-:-:•&lt;:•.:-:,•:-:-:••:-:. .:••:. •:-:. •:••:. •:••:. •:••:. •:-:. •:••:- •:••:. •:. 4:- •:••:. •:••:- •:-:. •:••:- •••••:. •:••:- ..-:- •:.</head>
        <p>•:••:••:.:••:••:.•:•.:-:-:••:-:••:-:.•:,.:•.:.•:-:-:.•:-:-:-:-:••:-:-:-:••:••:-:••:-:-:-:,•:-:••:-:-.:-:..:••:-:••:••:,•:-:•.:4•:-:-:••:-:••:-:-:••:••:••:••:••:-:- .:4•:••:-:-:-:-:-:••:••:..).:-:-:-:•.:-:-:.•:-:•,:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:•.:-:••:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:••:•.&gt;•:••:-:••:-:-:-:-:-:-:••:-:-:••:-:-:-:•&lt;:•.:-:,•:-:-:••:-:. .:••:. •:-:. •:••:. •:••:. •:••:. •:-:. •:••:- •:••:. •:. 4:- •:••:. •:••:- •:-:. •:••:- •••••:. •:••:- ..-:- •:.</p>
        <p>,A. .ii ... , t'4,,, , . . ! . ; . •., • ",,W4-; f I..ie '''," Jr, .. W , ~,- ..„..„ 4: , '• •• •;;I: ''.. il; . 3 " ~. .-iit, • • . 7...q.,-,... ..N. . ~c . • it • •`••,.. .. ~ I- I . . .. .. 47 " RI, ! .</p>
        <p>near future women with engineering training and'skill will readily find employment in many, if not all branches of this great industry. But training is essential. As mechanical and electrical assistants, trained women will take the place of men, whilst in munition works and factories, there are good positions opening up for women. The chauffeuse is already a reality, and women are in request- on farms for running threshing machines and oil engines. All repairs connected with motors,- and the light- ing and heating plants of factories and private residerices, come well within the sphere of the trained woman electrician. In fact, the Board of Trade has lately signified its approval of the employment of women as trained engineers. Fire Drill for Ladies.</p>
        <p>There are few things now unattempted women, and one of the latest developments .is the Ladies' Home Fire Drill, introduced by Messrs. Merryweather and taught by them. By the aid of a few simple forms of fire extinguish- ing appliances, and a small amount of drill instruction as to their use, the women of the household may render valuable assistance in an emergency. In these days, when the atten- tions of an unscrupulous enemy have led to the aerial bombardment of undefended towns, sueb an emergency may at any .time arise. The system of drill referred to is fully explained and illustrated in a little book issued by Messrs. Merryweather (Crieenwich), which no doubt will be sent on request to suitable appli- cants.</p>
        <p>Holiday Suits in Stripes. Many are the coats and skirts designed for this season's holiday wear, in linens, cottons and woollens of the lightest makes. The latter are dearer than in former seasons, but in the end will prove more economical, since no laundry expenses need be incurred. Then chilly days have to be provided for, when thin wash- ing (hresses, however charming, are utterly un- suitable. The holiday suit is most successful in the ever popular stripe, even when it is very broad, if carefully manipulated, and is shown not only in woollen fabrics, but in muslins, voiles and piques. A model on the simplest lines had a full skirt with two graduated floun- ces,. about six. and four iikches in depth, the wider one at the hem, the other just below the knee. On the (toupees the stripes ran diagon- ally, but on the skirt, coatee and sleeves, they were I)erpendicular. The rather wide sleeves were trimmed at the elbows with two frills,</p>
        <p>to match those on the skirt. The smart coatee had a waistcoat of satin that opened just enough to show a muslin chemiBette, to which was attached a high turnover collar. Fur Sets and Feather Neckwear.</p>
        <p>Sets of furs are being sold very cheaply at the summer Sales, and in this connection it must be borne in mind that owing to the diffi- culties of transport, and of securing the usual consignments, peltry of all kinds will be dearer as the autumn advances. Skunk is to be fashionable again, and very lovely necklets of black silky-looking fur, with charming muffs to match, are offered at reductions that may be truthfully characterised as drastic. Dark and blue-grey wolf are most becoming furs, and sets are to be had that are most attractive and low in price. Racoon is a fur that finds many admirers, and the contrast of black and white in the natural skin is certainly striking; sets of this pelt may be 'secured now very low in price. Every description of feather necklets, and boas, in all lengths and widths, are shown too, at the sales, at most remarkable prices. Esp(kially tempting is the ostrich and mara- bout neckwear, either alone co. combined, in black, black and white, mole and grey, and in grey and white. Tasteful Millinery.</p>
        <p>The millinery worn by the really well-dressed women to-day, is severe in its simplicity, and yet graceful and distinctive. Flowers *and feathers used to be considered in very bad taste when worn together, but lately, many good milliners have used the combination rather profusely, probably because their customers desired it rather than from their own convic- tion of the suitahle. Although simplicity is the order of the day, where dress is concerned, it must be confessed that in the case of hats, the trimming is often overdone; masses of feathers and flowers appearing togethet. When a severe style is adopted the materials must be of the best if the result is to be .successful, and only in exceptional cases can an pmateur turn out a good model of this description. If of straw, the best Tagel or Leghorn is the cheapest in the end, because the best, and most becom- ing shapes are to be had, only in the highest •priced articles. The trimming, however, costs but little, and may consist of a few good flowers and some narrow velvet ribbon, deftly arranged on and under the brim, and around the crown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Colonial Generosity. Princess Patricia' has lately purchased a small estate in Canada, upon which lier Royal Highness intends to build a house. The Princess's love for the Dominion is well known, and her popularity among all classes in Canada is unbounded. The help given so spontaneously by all the Dominions is one of the most gratify- ing features of this terrible war. Not only have they provided and equipped their contin- gents, but they have helped all sorts of societies with money and gifts. A short time age, an appeal was made in New Zealand for subscrip- tions-towards the Emergency Antesthetic Fund, and within a few weeks the sum of one thousand pounds was cabled to the Secretary. This enabled the treasurer to send- much needed drugs to British, French. Belgian, Alexandrian and Maltese hospitals. In the two l latter are many New Zealand wounded. • 4. Fewer Servants.</p>
        <p>The war is reducing many families to -a servantles6 condition. Young women who, under ordinary circumstances, would have gone into service, prefer to marry soldiers and draw allowances, with the comfortable assurance of a. pension if anything happens to the husband at the Front. In many districts there are now twice as ninny Registry Office marriages as be- fore the war, and Birmingham jewellers are experiencing ,an unprecedented demand. for wedding rings. Then numbers of girls who have been engaged in, or intended to go into servict, are finding employment in one or other of the ninny new Occupations which the war has opened up to women, and there is so much war money about that it lessens the need for daughters of working-class families to leave home. It is true that well-to-do families are economising by doing with fewer servants, but this has little or no effect on the shortage, as it touches the middle class housewife.</p>
        <p>Engineering for Women. The war has caused almost a complete.revolu- lion in the outlook for women in the industrial worla. Engineering has hitherto been regarded as purely a masculine department, but in the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>HOME NOTES.</head>
        <p>HOME NOTES.</p>
        <p>Profitable Recipes and Useful Suggestions.</p>
        <p>Macaroni Cheese.</p>
        <p>Boil 4oz. macaroni in fast boiling water till it is soft (usually in about twenty minutes). -Strain off the water, put the macaroni into a quart pie-dish, pour over enough of the cheese sauce to cover. Bake till a nice .pale-brown.</p>
        <p>Sheep's Head Pie. Clean the head and put into enough cold water to cover it, with vegetables if you have any. Simmer gently till the meat will leave the bones. Chop up the meat and mix it with bread-crumbs or soaked crusts some chopped onions, and some of the broth. Put it into a pie-dish, cover it with bread-crumbs and some bits of dripping, and brown it in the oven. Rhubarb Wine.</p>
        <p>To - make this get some well-developed rhubarb sticks and cut into small pieces. To every 41b. add 1 gallon of cold water, stand for eight days, stirring every day, then strain, and to each gallon of liquor add 31b. of brown sugar and a sliced lemon. Allow this to stand for three days, then put into a cask, and add a little isinglass. Bottle in three months. Now is the time to make it, as later the rhubarb becomes bitter. -Wheatmeal.</p>
        <p>In its natural state wheat contains every ele- ment of nutrition necessary for the support of the human body, and in gluten it is very rich, containing 12 per cent. of that valuable matter. The virtue of eggs, milk and beef may, indeed, be said to be combined in wheat; this is, of course, speaking of natural wheat, not the wheaten flour from which our white bread is made. By the time that wheat has gone through all .the various and ingenious processes which are necessary to bring it to the required degree of refinement, its character as the "king of grains" is gone. - it is not in its whiteness that the value of a loaf consists; brown bread made from genuine whole wheatmeal is far more healthful and forms a, more perfect food than does white bread. . Although it would not be wise to adopt brown bread as the only form of. loaf, even in war -time, the establishment of its use should be retained in every household.</p>
        <p>Four Ways of Boiling Eggs. It may be news to some people that there are at least four ways of boiling eggs, to say noth- ing of roasting them, concerning which readers of Sir Walter Scott's " Waverley " will remem- ber the description of poor Davm (.lellatiy lire- paring thus for the old Laird of liradwardine. (1) Put the eggs (which must be, fresh, and should be neiw laid) on in plenty of absolutely boiling water, and from the time the water re- boils after immersion, keep them boiling for three minutes if small, or three and a half</p>
        <p>minutes if large, it is well, however, to observe that some people might like a trifle longer time for the cooking. (2) Steam the eggs for four minutes Or so in a steamer, as ii they were potatoes. (3) Put the eggs on in a stewpan with plenty of boiling water, cover the pan down at once, draw it off the fire to the side of the stove, and let the eggs cook in this way for six minutes. Eggs elOoked thus can be left uncovered for ten minutes or so in the water • without harden- ing. • This our grandmothers called " coddling " eggs. (4) Put the eggs into a pan of cold water, on a sharp fire, and when the water reaches full boiling point, the eggs will be sufficiently cooked for most people. Done in this way the white is more . creamy, and mixes better with the yolk than when cooked by the ordinary process. Nurses, however, and other authorities pronounce eggs cooked by the third or " coddling " process to be the lightest and most digestible. for children and invalids.— "Vegetarian Cookery."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>HERE AND THERE.</head>
        <p>HERE AND THERE.</p>
        <p>A woman's imagination is strong enough to make an unreal thing seem more real to her than the real thing to a man.</p>
        <p>More Than Quiet Humour. Something more than quiet humour is in this paragraph, 'printed at the end of the Edmon- ton Opera House regulations by Manager Brandon: "Any old ladies afraid of taking cold may keep on their hats or bonnets."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Dad Gets Sarcastic.</head>
        <p>Dad Gets Sarcastic.</p>
        <p>" Physical culture, father, is 'perfectly lovely. To develop the arms I grasp this rod by one end and move it slowly from right to left." "Well, well !" exclaimed her father, "what won't science discover. If that rod had straws at the, other end you'd be sweeping."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Hard Place for Bachelors.</head>
        <p>Hard Place for Bachelors.</p>
        <p>• "Korea's the wrong place for bachelors," said a traveller. " Bachelors in Korea are consid- ered as • children, and have may children's privileges. . You, a Korean bachelor, get thirsty. You enter a rest house and call for palm wine. The pretty little amber-coloured waitress says: "'Married?' "'No,' says you. Hems, then,' says she; and out you go, unslaked.</p>
        <p>"You want to vote, but they won't let you if you are not married.</p>
        <p>'You apply for a job somewhere. 'How many children have you?' is the first question you're asked.</p>
        <p>"And as soon as you say you're unmarried, they laugh in your face to think that you should presume to apply for work anywhere."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Useful luformatlon In Popular</p>
        <p>Language.</p>
        <p>Thc Fly .Menace. Now is the time to guard the home, the food, the children, and particularly the babies from the deadly fly. Thousands and tens of thousands of children die every summer be- cause of the ignorance of their parents with regard to the terrible mischief that the com- mon -house-fly is capable of doing. Remember that this insect is covered everywhere with hairs anti is capable of carrying millions of germs. Call to mind the filthy habits of this most common of all insects, how it breeds in the manure-pile, and is prone to visit the midden, the garbage-bin, and all sorts of refuse and filth, and then gaily flies in by the window for a drink of milk perhaps, or to sample the golden syrup, or the pudding, or porridge, or bread, or any food. The fly has a special fond- ness for the nipple of the milk-bottle or the pacifier. Have you never felt the sticky feel- ing of those filthy feet as the fly • crawled over your forehead, or face, or hand? Every fly must be looked upon as a potential source of in- fection and disease, and there is only one treat- ment for the fly, and that is to get rid of it.— "Good 'Health.l'</p>
        <p>Nursing Cases of Infectious Disease. The room chosen for an infectious case should be in the most secluded part of fife house; if possible the top floor should be isolated, and he nurse with the patient shut off from the other inmates of the house. • All superflous rugs, hangings and furniture should be removed from the isolated rooms, cupboards and drawers should be emptied, and only things necessary for the nurse and patient kept in the rooms. Upholstered furniture should be avoided, as it is more difficult to disinfect. Throughout the course of an infectious disease a Aheet wrung out in carbolic 1 in 10 solution should be hung outside the patient's door, and kept wet continually. All soiled linen should be soaked in a bath of 1 in 40 carbolic solution or in a very strong lysol for some hours. They should then be wkshed and boiled before they can be considered free. from infec- tion. Blankets and woollen garments which cannot be soaked and washed should be fumi- gated with sulphur or formaline. The nurse should wear an overall in the sick room, and take it off when she leaves the room. She should always have a basin bf kwater with lysol ready to disinfect her hands. Each time she attends to the patient she must wash and disinfect her hands and arms, and always be- fore she leaves the room. The most common infectious diseases which can be nursed at home are chicken-pox, measles, - German measles. mumps, whooping cough, diphtheria, typhoid fever, scarlet fever, pbthisis, and influenza.— "Jack's Household Medicine." •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <p>Prince of Wales,' because my conscience would be satisfied by the way I was doing my duty. Although I would be the least in the world to expect hero-worship, I would be setting a good example to the 'slackers' of Great Britain, and so gain the popu- larity of the people.—From your loving nephew, ERNEST POTTER." Splendid, Ernest! Perserefe. Thomas Ashton says: " I choose to be Admiral Sir John Jellicoe, as I would be commander of the home fleet, which is at present in the North 'Sea. I would be recognised as a second Nelson, and be obeying my country's call as he did in many battles." Of course our splendid Minister of Munitions was not forgotten:—</p>
        <p>"If I was asked who I should like to be if I was not myself, I should say Mr. Lloyd George, the Munitions Minister, because his position is of the. utmost importance, and because he has, by his skill in dealing with money matters; become very popular with the nation to which he belongs. I should to be him because he has the con- fidence of 'nearly every Briton, and you could be walking along a street and hear one person say fo Another: 'He got the money, and he'll get the munitions too.' —From your sincere nephew, ALFRED LONG." A very good letter! and a very true one. Many thanks to 43,1, Niece Hilda Hassan, for an original and first-rate attempt : "36, Factory-lane, Warrington, June 27th, 1915. Dear Uncle Ned,—lf I were not myself I would like to be a person who had travelled a great deal. Then I could tell people all . about different things which I had seen. I could also tell them about the Great Barrier Reef of Australia, which is a coral rock.— I remain, your loving niece, HILDA HASSALL." And so: Good-bye for the present.</p>
        <p>Your loving UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>THOUGHT FOR THE WEEK. The great nii,take iii life is to•give up wanting anything.—W.tuwicx DEEPING.</p>
      </div>
