        <p>. „.; ~ WPIRRING,- ON ~,,,..,z4.:..,/ihT„, ~‘. - ~,, t • • i limy -ink iN A •• eine N . . 4t. o• .:. j V: , al k , 4, il . ..!% kti'''.:t fi ‘1:;„4. ..4';-' I. Ls .... . ,*, ~,„.... 4,, .....,. ~....,,,, . ~, fy ;( . - . u. ; 1 t, . 4.- •o 4 IN THE .. . ... ~,, „. .. .., . . .</p>
        <p>SERGEANT ARTHUR SKELHORNE, 219, Knutsford-road, the Warrington Rugby forward, wounded in back and Shoulder by five. shell fragments. He is 28 years of age, and is "doing °fine.v</p>
        <p>11, llardy-street, was dangerously .wounded ni the right shoulder. lie is 27 years of ages, works at Whitecross, and plays football for the</p>
        <p>Whitecross team</p>
        <p>PRIVATE T. 11. CORBETT.</p>
        <p>SERGE.INT J... 11. SOLLORY.</p>
        <p>• • "Followed By Shells" Whilst Going Nurse Writes Of His Wounds And To The Dressing Tent. Bravery. At 21, Orchard-street, Mr. and Mrs. T.. U. Sollory received a letter from their son:— " I have been wounded. I was hit in the hand and -in the left leg; but tt is not' serious. I hope to be home to see you in the near future. Have had a good tea to-day—tomato soup, egg, bread and butter and tomatoes." This was written fromNhe Quebec Ward, No. Canadian Stationary Hospital, Le Touquet. France. Writing- on June 23rd—with the help of the nurse—the Sergeant says: "I have been under an operation with my leg, and have had a hit of shrapnel taken out 'of my left arm. Now I am going on splendidly. my right hand .is also hurt, which accounts for the bad writing. [Here the nurse takes it up.] Haven't had ani temperature since the second day after I came in, which is a very good sign. They all say I am a brick. (The sister is writing now, and he -certainly is. He never says one word while his leg is being dressed. We hope to get him home to you very soon. Anyone who has gene through what he has should cerminly get back to England—and I am sure Major Bell Will see to that. Don't worry about him. Will let you know again how he is getting along). And now this is all we ,can think ,of to let you know. Do write him soon." On Monday afternoon .word ran: "We have a tip-top nurse, and everything we could wish for. She is a champion. I am feeling too tired to write. The weather here is all right,' flct my only regret is that I have to stay in bed." Scouts and C.L.B. ? . •</p>
        <p>Private Thomas Herbert Corbett, .of B Cora- pany, was, wounded early on in the charge on the enemy's trenches. His injuries consist of shrap- nel wounds on the right foot and under the left arm. Writing home be says : " Our artillery started a. tremendous bombardment 'at about 2.30 in the morning, and a little later the Ger- mans started and kept it up all day. 'We rushed up to the second line trench and we were under very heavy fire.</p>
        <p>Took Five Lines of German Trenches.</p>
        <p>We managed to take five lines of German trenches, although the battalion suffered ter- ribly. While, going along to the dressing sta- tion I was over the knees in vrater, and shells were following me all the way. You should be grateful to 'God, as I am, for bringing me through such an awful battle. It was terrible to see first one and then another getting bowled over. . . . Do not worry about my wound as it is not serious."</p>
        <p>Private Corbett, who is a single man of 22 years of age, resided with hi S parents at Orford, near the Tannery. He was educated at the People's College and Orford Schools, and before enlisting in September was employed at the Orford Tannery. He worshipped at the Parish Church, and attended the St. Ann's Bible Class.</p>
        <p>Aged 22, Sergeant Sollory was educated at Ileathside School, and later went to work as a fin-box maker for Messrs. Crosfield's. Here he remained until August—when he was called tin. haying been in the " Terriers " for five yen rs. Tie also beloncred to the Crosfield's Scouts and Church Lads' Brigade, in which he had been a drummer. He attended St Paul's Church and qunday School.</p>
