      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>STORIES OF THE CHARGE</head>
        <p>STORIES OF THE CHARGE</p>
        <p>"SAT IN THE TRENCHES CRYING."</p>
        <p>"Quite Young German Lads" Who Dreaded The "Terriers" Charge.</p>
        <p>Private Harold Bailey, of the Warrington Territorials, writing to his parents at 116, Battersby-lane, says:— " We went to the trenches last Tuesday night and we were told that there would be a charge, and that I've would take part in it. . . Well, I thought that I had had some rough times, but I never want to see anything like that again. It was awful to see the dead lying in the trenahes, and to hear the wounded moan- ing and Acing for drinks. . . . I have God to thank for being alive! . . . "We Rushed the Germans Out of It."</p>
        <p>"We rushed the Germans out of it, and took four, lines of trenches. Half of the German soldiers surrendered at once. They cannot face the bayonet. Those who were taken prisoners were glad to get out of it all. . . . Some of them were quite young lads, who, according to what a soldier told me, sat in the trenches crying, because they knew we were going ,to charge after the bombardment finished. . . . There were hundreds of shells flying in all directions, and I cannot wonder at anyone coming home with shattered nerves."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>fIIIAJOR CROSFIELD.</head>
        <p>fIIIAJOR CROSFIELD.</p>
        <p>Private Says That Officer Saved A Trench.</p>
        <p>Private C. C. Taylor, machine gun sectim L:eet, has 'writ-, throughwa , "</p>
        <p>he sa.7 s,</p>
        <p>des.:rved anything 1, rld. I was in the fi: In the morning SOP ' ;imei,ts came running from the secor fl•cnch. Who should come along In the Major. lie shouted out 'Follow me . Conniany.', All the ligys rushed to him an( they went across a field to the second Germs, trench. I do not know how they got across— it, was hell. He remained there all day. lar certain he saved that trench. The Gallant Dead.</p>
        <p>I see Charles Knowles' photograph in 'Tb' Etaminer." He was in the same class as myself at Trinity School. I believe he has been men- tioned in dispatches. The last time I saw him he went over with Major Crosfield."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>STORIES FROM 7HE TRENCHES. 1</head>
        <p>STORIES FROM 7HE TRENCHES. 1</p>
        <p>REAL BRITISH GRIT.</p>
        <p>The following letter has been received by Mr. P. Phillips of Mount-street, from a friend, Trooper E. IL ilunstowe, who is serving at the Front with the Royal Horse Guards:— "It is an -awful experience" he wrote. "going under fire for the first time, but we soon got used to it, and now we take practi- eo.ily no notice. We were in the front line, and we saw•and heard one of .the heaviest bits oi artillery firing during the war. The kierintins started early one morning and kept on tnioughout the day. From three points they concentrated their fire on one point of our line. As it happened the 3rd Dragoon Guards were at. that point, and we were not many yards away, for shrapnel fell on several of our chaps. . . . We had few casualties, but the 3ra Dragoon Guards lost heavily. They were shellea out of their trenches time after time, only to come up again smiling, and more determined. They possessed the real British grit, and the Germans got more than they gave. . . .</p>
        <p>"One of the worst difficulties with which we have to contend are snipers. It is said that they go about in khaki. Fifty men and an officer were seen to enter a chateau dre4sed in khaki, but they were discovered and shelled out, a machine gun adding to their inglorious exit. Not many escaped. The brutes are up to all low down tricks, even to sending a mes- sage down the line. . We are on the winning side. Our chaps cannot be touched—they are the right stuff."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>TWICE WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>TWICE WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>And NoW Back Again In Thc Fighting</p>
        <p>Line.</p>
        <p>Rifleman William Davies, who is at the Front with the Ist Battalion of the Rifle Brigade, and whose home is at 65, Howley-lane, Warrington, refers in a recent letter to the fact that he first entered the trenches on New Year's Day, and he got along all right until February 21st, when he was wounded by shrapnel whilst out sniping. He next went into the trehches on April 16th, and the quarter in which his regiment were fighting at that time was, he says, "a very hot one." He adds: "I wasn't surprised when I again got wounded on May 13th. The Germans shelled us all day long, right up to dusk. After I got over the second wound—l was down at the base for a month—l went back to the trenches —in much the same quarter as they, were when I left them. At the moment of writing I am along with the Warrington boxer—Joe McDonald. All the boys from Warrington that I have come across are quite cheery." Rifleman Davies adds that he gets "The Examiner" regularly, and assures the editOr that he is " a faithful reader."</p>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL WM. PINSON, 83, Chorley-street, was hit in shoulder, leg and thigh. He is nineteen years of age, and a clerk at the Education Offices: A Colour-Sergeant in St. Paul's Church Lads' 13rigade.</p>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAI. F. J. PERCIVAL. Lance-Corporal Francis Joseph Percival, 103, Mersey-street, was one of six hit by the same shell. Wounded in the back. ,Works- for Messrs. Crosfield and attends St. Mary's Romau Catholic Church.</p>
        <p>_ . - .. .-; ..m., .. • , i -4-'4,,,,. : . ''' _ *.r. ~.... , - , i,,,..t/:........,-..„, ' '''''-',•sii:.:',4';'‘.,;.,-. ' . s * •':,,,,i4;;.,,„'5':k•::0'1--:', , ."`..,•,,5.17-..;:,'':,1.:5% ::&lt;'''.?C`,:i'lS,,:'';''',;'' ' z&gt;. 11e,4;t4,: :3.i,-'' •:,-,'''.:s,'::=—,l,-,,::'.:....7 -i-,,,,...,-4-'l* ',l .: itkz,„-al; ww-,.--1,7,,,,,-,.. ~„,. A .‘+',4,- • • ''°4'. `1••• -**0.4"".',, , ' ~,,fa;.'44:.,:' 4-'4*,' ' ). . ~,' ..•'._,- `,,,,a0s- ~ i • Al* ,s,s4kii. Avtit„,- - ~' , ~. , .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>"THINGS 601\G SPLENDIDLY WITH THE ALLIES."</head>
        <p>"THINGS 601\G SPLENDIDLY WITH THE ALLIES."</p>
        <p>Liberal Clubite AVho Hopes To Be Home In August.</p>
        <p>A popular member of the Warrington Liberal Club—Private T. Mullin, who is now serving his country in the R.A.M.C.—writing to Mr. E. A. Sanders, the Liberal Agent, a few days ago, says:—" I can assure you that we are all doing our best for our country, and I only hope that when this is over we shall be treated as we ought to be. We have had it very ' thick ' just lately, but we have stood to our posts like men. I only hope that at the next General Election we Liberals may might as manfully for Sir A. I. Crosfield as our soldiers are fighting out here, for then' there can be no doubt as to who will be returned as M.P. for good old Warring- ton. . . . Remember me fo all at the club, and tell them that I am in the best of health and spirits, and that I hope to be with them some- time in August, for things are going splendidly with the Allies."</p>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL S. MONKS</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Simeon Monks, 17, • Kean- street, was buried by fall of earth in the trench, but was rescued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>• ROUSING CALL.</head>
        <p>• ROUSING CALL.</p>
        <p>A Few A% ords From One Alho Is Serving To The Many Who Are Not!</p>
        <p>In the course of a letter to Mr. E. A. Sanders, the Liberal agent, Private 11. Brandwood, a member of the Warrington Liberal Club, who is serving with the M.T. section of the A.S.C. at the Front, says: "I see that a good number of my old school chums have 'gone under' whilst doing their duty, but how many young, able- bodied men whom I know are there in Warring- ton who are not doing their. duty? I saw thousands in the streets during the short time I was on furlough. It is very disheartening to us out here to think that there are so many young men. who don't; or won't, realise the danger that our beloved old country is in. Ido not suppose that anybody wants conscription, and if it does come to pass, then the young men a Britain will only have themselves to thank for it. I do not see any young men in the French towns as NA-e pass through them, for every one of military .age has hitd fo 'face the music, whether he liked it or net I would rather see our fellows volunteer titan lao forced into it. But if our young men will not volun- teer !" The writer adds that he gets " The Examiner" regularly, aml remarks, -uXou pay bet that it interests</p>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL WM. GRUNDY, 87, Laira-street, shot by a bullet which was partly stopped by his pocket books. He is 28 years of age, and works at Croslield's A vcirks competition footballer.</p>
        <p>k,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>PRIVATE ROBERT ROBINSON;</head>
        <p>PRIVATE ROBERT ROBINSON;</p>
        <p>who is an orphan, and lives with Mrs. Wrench, 103, School Brow, and works at Rylands' Bros. as ,a labourer, is wounded in the right hand. Parish Church worshipper.</p>
