      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>PRIVATE F. W. LEA,</head>
        <p>PRIVATE F. W. LEA,</p>
        <p>46, West-street, whose father died a few days before, is a moulder at Richmond's, and a Longford White Star footballer.- lie wati .0 Ile of seven to be buried by a ellen,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>LANCE-CORPL. W. J. HIGHAM.</head>
        <p>LANCE-CORPL. W. J. HIGHAM.</p>
        <p>One Of Four Soldier-Brothers hit In</p>
        <p>The Head By Shrapnel.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal William 'John Higham, who lives with his parents at Laburnum Villa, Orford, has been wounded with shrapnel in the forehead, and has been removed to the base. .LanceXorporal Higham, who is 27 years of age, formerly belonged to the old Volunteers, and joined the Territorials in August. He was educated. at Orford School, and at the outbreak of war he was employed at the Orford Tannery. Re worshipped, at Orford Church and was a member of the Young Men's Bible Class. He is a member of the Junior Conservative Club in Orford-lane, and takes an interest in football and bowls. Corporal George Higham,. who was _ .</p>
        <p>killed recently, was his brother, and two other of his brothers are serving with the colours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>PRIVATE ARTHUR WALKER.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE ARTHUR WALKER.</p>
        <p>Adult School Member Shrapnelled In</p>
        <p>He:id.</p>
        <p>As we stated last week, Private Arthur Walker, of 29, Fennel-street; has been wounded by shrapnel in the head. His wife received the news on, a postcard from him, and he now 'writes from hospital at Tonbridge, Kent, to say he is progressing well and hopes to be home soon: Unfortunately he also sprained his ankle on leaving the battlefield. Aged 33, Private Walker i 4 married and has three children. As a boy he attended St. Ann's School, and now works as a range-fitter for Messrs. .Fletcher, Aussell. He belongs _to the Academy-street Adult School.. The ." Terriers" he joined fourteen years ago, and was thus called up in August.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>PRIVATE JOSEPH SAVAGE.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JOSEPH SAVAGE.</p>
        <p>liWands' Cranedriver Doing Well At</p>
        <p>Oxford.</p>
        <p>Private Joseph Savage, of 151, Stephen-street, makes good progress at the 3rd Southern General Hospital, Oxford—after a serious period with concussion and a broken hand. •lie was wounded by the trench in which he was being blown up, and a piece Of shrapnel catching him as stated. Last week he arrived in England, going to Oxford immediately. The Private, who is 36 years of age, attended as a boy St.. James' School, and first went to work as an engineer at St. Helens. Later he joined Messrs. Rylatids", Dalton Bank, as, a eranedriver, He has served on the Fairfield Ward Conservative Committee. He is married, and has a son and daughter. The Territorials he joined on the outbieak of war, .leaving for the Front at the end of Feb- ruarY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>PRIVATE I. WILLIAM SMILEY.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE I. WILLIAM SMILEY.</p>
        <p>Progressing Well 111 Bromborough Hospital.</p>
        <p>Private John William Smiley, of 30, Glaze- brook-street, is progressing nicely, at Oakfield Military Hospital, Bromborough, near Birken- head.</p>
        <p>The news of his woUnding was received last week by his wife: "I am slightly wounded by shrapnel in the left, shoulder-blade, but I expect to be all right again shortly. . . . I shall never forget our great battle as long as I live; it is one of the greatest ever known. There were dead and dying lying all over the place; it Was jut like a slaughter-house. I don't think there are many of our battalion left. I am very lucky to escape so well. There was a great slaughter of Germans also." On Monday, word from hospital ran: "We have a lovely home here, and ladies and gentle- men come to see us every day." Aged 38, Private Smiley • was educated at the National School, and works as a labourer at Dallam Forge—where he has been for about fifteen years. He took considetable interest 'in 'football.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>PRIVATE T. MASKELL.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE T. MASKELL.</p>
        <p>Blinb V.C. As Amanuensis For Wounded "Terrier."</p>
        <p>Private T. Maskell, whose name appeared in last . week's heavy cagualty list as one of the men wounded in the gallant charge made by the local " Terriers," writes to his fiancee, Miss Ethel Shaw, of 10, Norris-street, Warrington, to say that he is "going on fine" and has "nearly got over" his wounds. "I have been in hos- pital nearly a week" he adds—his letter is post-marked June 21st—" and I . . . have lived like a lord. . . . I can use my right arm in which he was wounded] now. . . . • The con- valescent camp in • which. I am quartered is situated in a lovely place, and I am within sight of the sea, which looks grand." The writer goes on to add that he had caused two pre- vious letters to be forwarded—one of which was written by a "bed chum" and the other by a Captain in .the Gordon Highlanders, of whom he says He is blind, but he has won the V.C. and he goes visiting the wounded t and writes for those who cannot write for themselves."</p>
        <p>Germans Ran Into British Lines and Surrendered.</p>
        <p>Describing how he rectived his wound, the gallant " Terrier " writes:—" We were 'attack- ing the Germans, when a shell burst close to me. . . . . I was more burned than anything else. MY head was ,a bit hurt and some shrapnel got into my hand I thank God that I got off so luckily The sights I saw I shall never forget! .. . I was sorry for the Germans, for our guns gave them an awful time. Our lads had no trouble in taking the trenches. There were dozens of Germans running into our lines' and giving - themselves up! Some of them did look sights ';-they looked to me as if they were fed up with the war. We I took a lot of prisoners, but we had a good lot 'put out'! Still the Gernians lost three times as many as we did."</p>
        <p>The letter from the blind officer of the High- landers—Captain E. B. B. •Towse—referred to in the plucky " Terrier's " letter, states that Private Maskell's wound was, on June 20th. going on very well indeed. "You May rest assured" adds the Captain "that he is in very capable hands, and that he will receive, every possible care and attention, both for his health and comfort."</p>
        <p>Private Masicell celebrated his 25th birthday in tb.e trenthe about a fortuialat ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>PRIVATE PETER VALENTE.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE PETER VALENTE.</p>
        <p>Crosfield's Labourer GOillff On Well</p>
        <p>At Woolwich.</p>
        <p>Private Peter Valente, of 4, -Pilling's-court. off Dial-street, joined the local Territorials ill September, and now sends home to his brother and sister a field card to say he is going on well from his injuries—the nature of which i 5 not known at Warrington. He is in the Royal Herbert Hospital, Woolwich, under the care of the Red Cross Society. .</p>
        <p>In peace times a labourer at Messrs. Cros- field's works, Private Valente is 20 years of age. His brother Dominic recently enlisted in the 3rd/4th South Lancashire Battalion, n°ll being raised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>PRIVATE JAMES POOLE.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JAMES POOLE.</p>
        <p>Metal Broker Now Recovered 01 Ills</p>
        <p>‘‘ound.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Arthur Lunt, of 113, Algernon-street, received a field card from her brother, Private James Poole—who lives with her—saying that he was slightly wounded in the left arm bY shrapnel. • - _ „</p>
        <p>He now writes very briefly to say he is better and ready to leave his hospital at Rouen for the firing line again. The Private had also been at Boulogne Hospital. Aged 30, Private Poole, as a lad, attended St. James' School, and later went to work at Mr. Howard's, metal broker, Hall-street. If! enlisted at the beginning a the war, and well' out with the first draft of local "Terriers."</p>
        <p>PRIVATE J. MAN.</p>
        <p>Hit By Shrapnel, But Writes Letter 01 Sympathy To 'Chum's Widow.</p>
        <p>Writing on Sunday from the Borough Hos- pital, Birkenhead, to Mrs. Hollowell, of Jockey' street, whose husband, Private Hollowell, Wag killed.</p>
        <p>Private J. Dunn, whose home is at 46, War- burton-street, says:— • ' " I hear that your husband, Private Hollo- well, has been killed, and I offer you my deepest sympathy. " Terriers" In the Thick of the Fight. .</p>
        <p>"Our battalion was in reserve, and jl bad t° go through the hardest of the .fighting, as the' German shells were dropping all round us, and burying some of our fellows alive, while other were blown to pieces. I got wounded through the right thigh with a piece of shrapnel. A Close Shave. "The shell burst at the other side of the trench, and the fragments came through thf: sandbags.. If. it had come another yard would have blown us all to pieces. My officer got hit in four places. lamin a very nice hospital. They give us anything we want,' or do anything for us."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>PRIVATE I. UREV.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE I. UREV.</p>
        <p>"Shells Came On Us In Thousands.'</p>
        <p>Private Isaac Urey is a married man, whose home address is The Cottages, Orford. He Act° suffering from the effects of the German but according to a letter which ho sent to WA° wife, he has now recovered, and has returne", to his battalion. Writing last week to his Peal- ents, Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Urey, of 62, Lyt,"' goes-lane, be said: "After pulling through t.Phe' shell fire, the dirty Germans put me out Not t. their poisonous gas, but I am glad to say I Vi over it all right. I shall never forget tilge Wednesday as: long as I live. The shells ce, on us in thousands. They say that there 118' been nothing like it yet.</p>
        <p>. Kept Their Adrantage_Despite the Shells. "I am sorry to say that we suffered ve,q heavily through it, but the lads stuek to 0' trenches we captured, in spite of the Gern3eit shells." Private trey had served in the Territorial: for some time before war broke out; indeed. had completed his term of service, but he t1,1e',., proniptly rejoined, and is at present a nierauhev. of A Company. He was formerly employed Messrs. Wm. Mortimer and Co., Ltd., 01'01.1, Tannery. Private Urey was educated at James' School, the family having formerly live' at Latchford. His brother, Private Ernest 111'6' is also in the Territorials.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>SERGEANT PETER MORRIS</head>
        <p>SERGEANT PETER MORRIS</p>
        <p>"Terriers'" Chaplain In Tears .49</p>
        <p>Those Left Marched Home,</p>
        <p>• to A .letter written by Sergeant Peter Morris e his home at 60, Priestley-street, conveyed "t. news that another brother, Company,S2rgefolo., Major Henry Morris, had been crushed I))fgther ing sandbags. In the course of the letter die, writer said; "We went into a charge oe 16th June, at a place called , and. me, I would not care to take part in another• ct it for anything, for it was borribh, !1 have landed back, and there are not so many 0!,11 left. I was very lucky to come out living, lc); was buried alive. . . .</p>
        <p>Singing as They Made the Charge. re "When we went to make the charge we vielg all singing, and Mr. Bracecamp was sh°llt,liere "Cheer up," but when we got hack there jed tears in the Chaplain's eye-s, as we. wart. past, weary and footsore."</p>
        <p>A Previous Narrow Escape.' n in • Company-Sergeant-Mafor Morris has bee- 1 the TerritorMs many years, and served throilgo: the Boer War os a Volunteer.' A few weeks ag,,, whilst ia the trenches, a bullet struck a bag e°- • f the taining some chloride of lime, and some e-ncaY lime entered his eye, necessitating his rerlai b3r to,bospital. He is, in peace times, 03101(30'o. Messrs. Bylands Bros., Ltd., and he is a known member of their Rugby football teil,A, having varloi*- won nverul medals with thew ia coLopetitjua.</p>
        <p>- • e‘</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>PRIVATE S. MILLET,</head>
        <p>PRIVATE S. MILLET,</p>
        <p>92, Orford-lane, who is single and 26 years of age, worked at the Bewsey Wire RoperY. He attended Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church, and was a member of the Junior Conservative Club.</p>
        <p>Shrapnel wound in the head</p>
        <p>erweitim i</p>
        <p>13, 'Marsh Flouse-iane,‘ was wounded in the right arm and shoulder. He works for Messrs. Richmond, and has three brothers serving with</p>
        <p>the Colours.</p>
