      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>PRIVATE JOHN DILLON,</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN DILLON,</p>
        <p>whose wife is living with her mother, Mrs. Houghton, 101, Algernon-street, has been wounded in the throat. He is employed at Randall's Tannery.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE ERNEST, LITTLE WOOD, adopted son of Mr. Prank Jolley, 14, Tijley- street, market porter. He had "wiped off an old debt" by accounting fop fil/Q cxermanq.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>PRIVATE JOHN MULCAHY,</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN MULCAHY,</p>
        <p>on of Mr.-,Maurice Mulcahy, 37, o*ford-strect , was wounded by a shell which buist over his head, a bullet from which went through hi neck. He is 22 years of age. Works in the boiler yard at Dallam Forge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>PRIVATE THOS. NICHOLSON,</head>
        <p>PRIVATE THOS. NICHOLSON,</p>
        <p>10, Vere-street, was wounded below the right knee by shrapnel, on his 28th birthday. He is employed at Crosfield's, and is conneeted with St. Peter's Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>PRIVATE TOM WORSLEY,</head>
        <p>PRIVATE TOM WORSLEY,</p>
        <p>Private Tom Worsley, 56, Priestley-Street, was wounded in the jaw. He is 30 years of age and married. Employed at Walker's Brewery as office boy and then as labourer.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE ISAIAH HEATH,</p>
        <p>49, Tilley-stitegt, who was Wounded in the right shoulder, is 28 years of age, and works at Messrs. Randall's Tannery. He is a married man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>LANCE-CORPORAL FRANK SHAKESHA.F'</head>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL FRANK SHAKESHA.F'</p>
        <p>Parish. He has been in the Territorials for four years. His father is a warehouseman at the Bridgewater Canal, Latchford. Lance-Corporal Shakeshaft, writing on June 18th, said:—" I am on the wounded list—two wounds in the right leg, one above knee and One in calf. Not very serious. Hit by piece of shell and at present in hospital. Hit Wednes- clay morning, 16th." Lady Superintendent P. F. Watt, writing from the Rawal Pindi British General Hospital, Boulogne, June 22nd, to Lance-Corporal Shake- Shaft's mother, said the leg was so badly in- jured that it had had to be taken off that morning. "Your son," ran the letter, "bore .the operation well and seems a little better this afternoon, though he is of course seriously</p>
        <p>Wanted News From Warrington. TWO days later the Lady Superintendent Wrote to say the patient was a little better. " Ile asked me to tell you that he was anxious to have a Warrington newspaper, and if you will send one addressed to me for him I will see that he gets it at once. We hope very Much that your son will do well now and be able to go home before very long."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Escorting Prisoners.</head>
        <p>Escorting Prisoners.</p>
        <p>Writing later to his sister, Mrs. Irons, 14, Reynolds-street, Lance-Corporal Shakeshaft, says:--" I am just gathering myself together. • • . . and I shall soon be on the move again, but minus my right leg. lam now my old self again. What a bombshell when the casualty list was issued. Warrington men have kept the tradition. . . . I left the scene of action during the charge.. We had just left the sup- Port trenches and were, waiting for the word to advance when I was detailed in charge of escort over prisoners."</p>
        <p>i. .400</p>
        <p>Splendid Selection OF</p>
        <p>NEWEST S TYLES</p>
        <p>BOYs', yOUTHS' AND</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>MEN'S CLOTHING.</head>
        <p>MEN'S CLOTHING.</p>
        <p>Perfect Pitting.</p>
        <p>Weasonable Prices.</p>
        <p>iviAiNcHF.sTER Clothing Depot</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <p>say that her son's condition is slightly better. The son, it will be remembered, was originally reported killed, but subsequently a Red Cross nurse wrote to his mother to inform her that he was in hospital seriously wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
