1.'1:- A: / 4---,,,7 ;,. -: :;,.litAgg.iiiiiii,e, ...ii;X•-",j47---,:-1. oi' \\,', ./;,..4,-*:._.,-,,,A,ijihri-ifilii .: 1 tf'ifr?; 11 itiiirllo,-; r.* r ' --.' ' fll4llll 11111'C ril lifial . t 1\ . , Hiqi. , ~ '...) :. ~,,..4; 7 ,1.T.:1/4t.;,!,..`4 ~ , / ' ,f•l'ti-l41;11i111.:1:i:::u ' 3''.711' '. Al 4' I. .i 11 E- 44 0 Wilk ' ,i, I if/ P:.:.:, -.; . • --60,.0,..;;;!;;„., ,/ . ~ a, ' I ‘kt(rii''''' I. ' - //.. ,J*••,.:-,_ "i ~ifiii:iiq ,/, rairalre!..::::vii ii• # .or, ._ iI , ~--.. , ,1 1 :II •• 11., .41: '1 .sitl,llll// l'i: 17/1;1111::1:1\m:': Olii;:7111-11;111611;1:11:1111111,/'//.7'1/./-111!!!!!111111111111r 'l' 1 .. 4"1\1 ‘\\- ' I _Ail i I 1!.. :,.,„, 'I it , "TI.:-.---.-- • _._..._ _. , 4,,,...- I ' i'lln"lP 111; 11. IPA -ri . •••'•lii . \\ I 6:: ..: _ —l_l7</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>* '0 S. •.. ~ 'S; Is ..,.... to-s--•-• 4-,S----- ,-.._.:: . . ssl , ---. —.'-...•-•es.:' 5',...--- .' L s • – s. ;Si , 4 • .-. . • • – ". ' . ~': :-.-, ....7 '' . '-....7.--;:::-.7.7:77-7,-.7.--....--- '' -7 ; -..- -' – -- --t-'----.' – --- ----""---"-- ----'•---.' ' ,' – ''..- ... • – -....., -; .- • . ... . .</head>
        <p>* '0 S. •.. ~ 'S; Is ..,.... to-s--•-• 4-,S----- ,-.._.:: . . ssl , ---. —.'-...•-•es.:' 5',...--- .' L s • - s. ;Si , 4 • .-. . • • - ". ' . ~': :-.-, ....7 '' . '-....7.--;:::-.7.7:77-7,-.7.--....--- '' -7 ; -..- -' - -- --t-'----.' - --- ----""---"-- ----'•---.' ' ,' - ''..- ... • - -....., -; .- • . ... . .</p>
        <p>10, Horsemarket St., WA RRiNGTON.</p>
        <p>(By permission of the proprietors of "Punch.")</p>
        <p>Sergt. (to recruit wandering about at the will of his horse): "Ere, you What are you doin' them ridin' up axe down like a General?".</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <p>Lieutenant Hatton, w.ho had been wounded in the leg.</p>
        <p>The gallant Sergeant is a native of Bridg- water, Somerset, but has worked for the War- rington Corporation as a gas-fitter for twelve years, having served his apprenticeship at the Gas Works.</p>
        <p>Writing from the Voluntary A.D. Hospital, Cheapstead, near Sevenoaks, Kent, to his aunt, he says the shell irPhis back and neck has been removed without an operation, and he feels much better now. .. . • "I shall have a chance of seeing • the new bridge yet," he adds. "Our casualties were heavy, but the German casualties were more. It was a treat to see them surrender."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>PRIVATE JAMES TAYLOR.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JAMES TAYLOR.</p>
        <p>" When I Got Wounded I killed Six</p>
        <p>Germans."</p>
        <p>Private Sames Taylor writes home to his wife at 4.5, Hume-street, to say he is getting on well at .Wildernesse Hospital, Seal, Sevenoaks. He was wounded in the right shoulder—how is not known yet. He is very cheery, and says : "I shall soon be all right again. When I got wounded I killed six Germans. The shells were flying all over the place." Aged 38, Private Taylor received his educa- tion in London—where he was born—and came to Lymm 23 years ago. At Lymm he worked as a bricksetter's labourer for Mr. •Thomason. Four years ago he obtained employment at Messrs. Rylands's, Dalton Bank, and came to live in Warrington,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>PRIVATE JAMES TAYLOR.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JAMES TAYLOR.</p>
        <p>The Royal Engineers he served with through the Boer War, but joined the "Terriers" early in October. He attends Brick-street Sunday school, and is a Liberal in politics. He has two little sons- and two girls.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>PRIVATE ARTHUR OXLEY.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE ARTHUR OXLEY.</p>
        <p>Writes That His Hurts Are Nothing 'l'o</p>
        <p>Be Alarmed About.</p>
        <p>Private Arthur Oxley, who will be twenty years of age in about a week's time, and who made his home with his parents in Knutsford- road, was among those wounded in the "Ter- riers'" big battle. He worked as a dresser at Messrs. Richmond's, and had been there about a couple of years before the war broke out. He only joined the Territorials about two months before war, was declared, and the camp which was so dramatically ,broken. up ,was, of _course, the first he had attended : He was• educated at St. James' School, and attended the Kent-street Mission, and he is spoken of by his friends as being of quiet disposition. He had been acting as orderly to Company-Sergeant-Major Morris whilst at the Front. Private. Oxley was wounded in the head, shoulder and leg, and wrote to his parents to assure them that his hurts were nothing to be alarmed about, and added that he was in hos- pital and ' was being well looked after. His father is serving in the National Reserve at Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>PRIVATE ERNEST KENT.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE ERNEST KENT.</p>
        <p>A Fighting Family: Four Sons And A</p>
        <p>Son-in-Law In The Army.</p>
        <p>Private Ernest Kent, one of those who was wounded in action in the great charge of the 16th inst., is the son of Mr.' and Mrs. Harry Kent, of 42, Hoyle-street, Warrington. He was wounded em n the arm, and is now at Tun- bridge Wells, where he is making good progress. Throwing up his occupation on Messrs. Cros- field's wharf, Private Kent joined. the 4th South Lancs. in August. He is a Warrington laa and attended the Wycliffe School. Private Kent, who was 20 years of age, was connected with St. Paul's Mission.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Harry Kent have three other sons with the Colours: Lance-Corporal Joseph Henry Kent (who up to the time of his brother Ernest being woundeQ, was with him in the Territorials from the first); and Harry and John Joseph Kent (both Privates in the Gordon Highlanders). There is one other son, and although he is only eighteen, he has had a shot at enlisting. The only other member of the family is a married daughter, and she has given a husband to the Rifle Brigade. Lance-Corporal Joseph Henry Kent is married. Mr. Harry 'Coatis a Liberal in polities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <p>PRIVATE THOMAS CHAMPION. Wounded.</p>
        <p>tinued, "is just above the left knee and was caused by shrapnel, and it is going' on all right." Private Champion is 22 years of age and: he was educated at the National School in Church-street, and is a clogger by trade. He worshipped at the Friars' Green Independent Methodist Church, and is very popular with a large circle of acquaintances, being spoken of as an industrious, "straight" young man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>PRIX ATE RICHARD STEPHENS.</head>
        <p>PRIX ATE RICHARD STEPHENS.</p>
        <p>Former Crosfield's Employe Wounded In The Arm.</p>
        <p>• Amongst those who were wounded in the charge of the Warrington Territorials on June 16th was Private Richard Stephens, who has a wife and three children at 64, Hoyle-street. He is now at Tunbridge Wells, recovering from the effects of a wound in the arm.</p>
        <p>Prior to the outbreak of war, Private Stephens was employed at Messrs. Crosfield's, in the glycerine department. As a lid, he at- tended Heathside School. He had been in the Territorials since the early part of last year. He was connected with St. Paul's Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>PRIVATE 11. KITCHEN.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE 11. KITCHEN.</p>
        <p>Was In The Latchford Cadet Corps.</p>
        <p>We learn that Private H. Kitchen was for- merly a Sergeant-Major in the Latchford cadet Corps, and not the Church Lads' BrigAdp, tot we stated last week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>NOT MISSING.</head>
        <p>NOT MISSING.</p>
        <p>PRIV E THOMAS KIMBLIN.</p>
        <p>Reported Missing, But Now Found To</p>
        <p>Be Wounded.</p>
        <p>Private Thomas Kimblin, of Stamford-street, who was reported as " missing " in the official list of casualties received at the Drill Hall last week, is now in hospital. On Monday the following letter was received from him by his wife :—" My back is awful, and I don't think it will ever be right again, for I cannot hold my- self up. I am getting well looked after, but I cannot eat any solid food. I will never forget that big battle if I live to be a hundred. Poor Fellows Blown Up in the Air.</p>
        <p>"I gave myself up that day when I saw poor fellows get blown up in the air, and I have only God to thank that I am here to-day. I know there were six killed when I was buried, and when they 'got me out`they put me down against an officer's dug-out. I had not been there a minute when another German shell came and buried me again, while one poor fellow got his head blown off. It was awful, but it must have been three times as bad on the German side."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>WITH THE SOLDIER BOYS.</head>
        <p>WITH THE SOLDIER BOYS.</p>
        <p>WarringtOn,llVleil :Who Read And Enjoy "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Bombardier Peter Broadhurst, 33, Oldham- street, Latchford, and a member of Emmanuel Church, who has joined the Royal Field Artillery, writes on the eve of setting sail this week for the Front, to say he had had "The Examiner at his place of training every week, and he enjoyed it too. It passed through a great many hands.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Goes With a Good Heart.</head>
        <p>Goes With a Good Heart.</p>
        <p>"I go with a good heart to do my bit, and I appeal to the men of ' Warrington to come for- ward to help our comrades out of the fire. 'Don't say to-morrow will do," do it now, for to-morrow never comes.</p>
        <p>"I now speak on behalf of my comrades and myself, we are all going this night with brave hearts to do our best and to do it right well too. Men of Warrington, rally round, join in with us. Don the khaki and be men, for the sake of the Land of the Vree."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>NEWS AT THE FRONT. Mr. Crosfield's Honour Received With</head>
        <p>NEWS AT THE FRONT. Mr. Crosfield's Honour Received With</p>
        <p>Satisfaction.</p>
        <p>"Just a line," writes Sergeant J. Burton, of 25, Tanner's-lane, who has been in the King's Liverpool Regiment for fifteen years, and fought in the Boer War, and more recently at Neuve Chapelle, "to your valuable paper, which I have the pleasure of reading every. week: Sometimes I read it in the billets, soncietimes in the trenches, but wherever I read it it always seems a great pleasure. I take special interest in letters from 'Somewhere in France.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Delighted With the Baronetcy.</head>
        <p>Delighted With the Baronetcy.</p>
        <p>"In the issue dated the sth, the first item I noticed was that Mr. Crosfield had been honoured by the King. I was really delighted, and I was pleased, to think he will be able to say, that he only got what he well earned. But what would have pleased me more would be ta</p>
        <p>have seen him included in the Coalition Cabinet,' assisting that wonderful head,' Mr. Lloyd George, who, all the boys out here think a great and worthy Minister to take over the difficult Wit 9f Mini*tcr 9f Munitions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Let All Pull Together.</head>
        <p>Let All Pull Together.</p>
        <p>"I am pleased to see that none of the War- rington boys have lowered themselves so much as to strike. I think it 'is out of all reason for men who are left at home to throw back and hinder the valiant work that our boys have done out here. It is no use me stating the diffi9lties and experiences that I have gone through since I came to France, it would take too long, and then I think mat enryone goes through the same. We all face death, as every- one at home understands; but let us all pull the same way. Let the people at home supply- us with our needs, and then we shall soon see the results, the overthrow of the Hun. "I would like to thank the Women's Liberal Association for the splendid work they are doing for the boys out here. It is a pleasure to know that someone thinks of them."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>PRIVATE ARTHUR BROOKES.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE ARTHUR BROOKES.</p>
        <p>suffered heavily, were in no wise dismayed, especially as they had achieved their object; the dislodging of the Germans from a position of some strategic value. "My word," said one of the gallant fellows, "that charge was no picnic. I saw my mates 'drop round me, and once I thought I was 'done in,' for only a few yards ahead of me the ground was suddenly 'all ploughed up. and there was a cloud of dust. instinctively I knew that a shell had 'come to 'town,' but luckily it didn't burst—quite a lot of the German shells don't. 'Made in Ger- many' was never so good a trade description as 'Made in Britain,' was it F" NOT ALLOWED TO TALK.</p>
        <p>One of the quintet who arrived early Wed- nesday morning was Private Arthur Brookes, who is married, and whose home is at 21, Catherine-street, where his wife and four chil- dren anxiously awaited his coming, and where he got a rousing reception from friends and neighbours, with whom he is extremely popular. Private Brookes was seen by an "Examiner" man on Thursday, but when asked to talk about the charge, or about the " Terriers' " ex- periences in the trenches he asked to be "ex- cued," saying that the strictest injunctions had been issued to him and to his comrades who had been given "over-the-water passes," as they are called, to keep their mouths as close as might-be, especially in the presence of newspaper men. . "They made it quite plain to us," he said, "that we must say even less than we are permitted to wrkite in our letters home." Private Brookes was a member of the old Volunteers, and continued on after that force was translated into the efficient and well- equipped Territorial Army, which has been winning such high encomiums from those who in the old days ,were its unkindest critics, and which, indeed, has covered itself with glory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>CLOSE SHAVE-AND A MASCOT.</head>
        <p>A CLOSE SHAVE-AND A MASCOT.</p>
        <p>Private Brookes had a close call in the recent heroic charge. A piece of "shrapnel caught him on the head and dazed him, and it was only his cap that saved his life. Private Brookes cherishes n. interesting little mascot—a small figure of the Saviour evidently broken front a small .crucifix. This was picked up in one of the trenches after it had been vacated by the enemy, following upon a heavy artillery duel. As to the charge ,itself, Private Brookes is extremely reticent. "We did our best—our level best—and that is all I shall tell you about it." he said to our reporter, with a smile. "He was always craving to go into a bayonet charge," interpolated his wife, "and now he has had his wish. And do you know," added the good lady, "that now he has been through one he does not fear the next. He intends to go throngh with his job and to do his duty like a man."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>WOUNDED WARRIORS.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED WARRIORS.</p>
        <p>Grosfield's Band Raise Funds</p>
        <p>For A Treat.</p>
        <p>Crosfield9s splendid band gave their services in a good cause on Sunday evening, when, in Bank Park, they rendered an excellent pro- gramme of music with the object of aiding a fund for providing a treat for wounded soldiers brought to local hospitals. As the large crowd entered at the various gates they were met by eagle-eyed Boy Scouts armed with collection boxes and few, indeed, were those who failed to "dub up" a small subscription, the net result being that a sum -of about £22 was realised. To this the members of the band have added their fees, so that the total proceeds were about £25. Thus, financially, the concert was a great suc- cess, and it goes without saying that it was also a 'success musically, for without doubt Cros- field's is the doyen of the local bands; and may always be relied Upon for a concert of unusual excellence. The band was under the conductor- ship of Mr. J. CI Taylor, and the programme was a decidedly interesting and attractive one, introducing numbers calculatea to appeal to all shades of musical tastes, ranging as the items did from the purely popular to the classical. The " star " item was a pot pourri of selections from the works of kissini, whilst a most popu- lar number was spirited rendition of Handel's immortal "Hallelujah Chorus." Specially fitting to the occasion was Mr. J. Blackburn's cornet solo " Abide With Me," whilst Mr. E. Smith scored heavily with Elgar's "Land of Hope and Glory," arranged as a trombone solo. Mr. F. Rogan's horn solo " L'etoile du Nord" was a very popular item. Other items on the programme were: March, "The Cossack" (Rimmer); overture, " Ray- mond " (Thomas); descriptive selection, "Mili- tary Church Parade," introducing a realistic imitation of church bells; and the "Hallelujah Chorus" (Handel).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>OAKLANDS MILITARY AUXILIARY</head>
        <p>OAKLANDS MILITARY AUXILIARY</p>
        <p>HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>Last -Saturday evening a. very enjoyable con- cert was given to the patients and staff of this hospital. The following kindly gave their ser- vices,: Miss Collin, monologues; Mr. R. 11. Pear- son, violin.; -Mr. J.' Willett, tenor; Mr. T. Humphreys, bass; Mr. Scarisbviek-, humorist; Master Neville Davies and Mr. F. M. Darby, pianoforte. At the close of the performance, on the pro- position of the Rev. T. T. Lee-JoneS, a hearty. vote of thanks was passed to Mrs. E. Aubrey White for having promoted the entertainment, to Mr. F. M. Darby for having arranged the programme, and to the artistes for their ser- vices.. This was acknowledged by. Mr. Darby, who said it had been a great pleasure to all of them to come.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>RYLANDS'S C.C. vr I\ ‘‘ K.</head>
        <p>RYLANDS'S C.C. vr I\ ‘‘ K.</p>
        <p>Soldiers' Meritorious X% in.</p>
        <p>Rylands's Recreation C.C. visited the Lord Derby Military Hospital at Winwick on Wed- nesday, to entertain the wounded soldiers to a game of cricket. Despite their wounds, the soldiers proved far too strong for the visitors, and inflicted upon them their first reverse of the season. - _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Regimental Band of the 2nd South Lancs. was in attendance, and played selections throughout ,the afternoon, which were greatly appreciated, and to Lieutenant R. Richards (R.A.M.C.) and his colleagues all credit is due for a very enjoyable afternoon's sport, d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <p>STANDARD:</p>
        <p>HEIGHT • sft. to sft. 2in. CHEST -331 n.</p>
      </div>
