      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>LATCHFORD ENTERTAIVNG SOCIETY AT TIIELNVALL HEYS.</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD ENTERTAIVNG SOCIETY AT TIIELNVALL HEYS.</p>
        <p>A very successful entertainment was given at the Thelwall Heys Hospital on Saturday aftei- noon last by the Latchford Orchestral arid Entertaining Society, under the conductorship of Mr. Alf. Grounds. Numerous selections were given by the orchestra in a finished man- ner. Songs were rendered with good effect by Miss N. Welsby and Mr. W. Ward, who were heartily applauded. A novel turn was given by Miss Grace Vernon, who danced the Scotch EcTsl in full Highland costume. The humorons element was ably sustained by Mr. Stan. Harrison, the Hebrew comedian, and Mr. Joe Hands, the Lancashire humorist. ,The concert concluded with the rendering of the Antos National Anthems by the orchestra.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>RADDON COURT.</head>
        <p>RADDON COURT.</p>
        <p>The following is a list of the newly-arrived patients at Raddon Court Red Cross Rocpi- tal</p>
        <p>Corporal William Webster,. 2nd Norfolks.- gun-shot wound abdomen; from the Persian Gulf.</p>
        <p>Private Curry, Ist Royal Inniskillen Fui- hers; gun-shot wound right shoulder. ,Private W. J. Ackroyd, Ist Australian 1. perial Force; shrapnel wounds in the lei 7 from the Dardanelles.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Charles. Smith, 2nd Rol Fusiliers; right leg amputated. Private H. Walch, 3rd Australian Imper.T.:d Force; gun-shot wound right right and shrap- nel wound chest; from the Dardanelles. Private Peter Buchanan, 9th Argyle and Sutherland Highlanders; shrapnel wound iu knee.</p>
        <p>Private Charles Mould, sth Manchester nient; scalded feet.</p>
        <p>Private George Fairy, 14th Australian Impe- rial Force; bomb wound left knee and thigh; from the Dardanelles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>ON ST. JAMES' SCROLL OF HONOUR.</head>
        <p>ON ST. JAMES' SCROLL OF HONOUR.</p>
        <p>Writing in this inonth's issue of his Parijii Magazine, the Vicar of St. dames', Latcly:(.3 (the Rev Herbert B. Firth) says:—" It would be interesting to 1-now how many men in or parish are now serring ith the Colours. The, names of 322 parishioners appear on the list in Church, and most of these are connected by bonds of affection either loose or strong with: the Church of England. But there must be many more whose names have not yet been received, and we should be glad to have them. The list is already a long one, and, it will form a noble- record in the future, and will be p: - -served among the Church documents with co and affection."-</p>
      </div>
