        <p>:,... ~ ~,.....2 ~.„.,...,.: ~,,_, , D, ,: , . ... sTock</p>
        <p>CONIMENCIES</p>
        <p>MONDAY NEXT, JULY sth.</p>
        <p>NO CATALOGUES ISSUED.</p>
        <p>DEANSGATE, MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>oe„z-q ° *za e. .0. A", *</p>
        <p>columns show, have had the honour of speak- ing to some of the heroes of the charge in a general kind of way.</p>
        <p>It will be seen that the Battalion has suffered somewhat heavily since the day of the charge, two casualty lists having been issued within the last two. day. The losses are not so heavy as Dame Rumour, who was again busy, would hart had us believe, but they are, netertheless, sufficiently serious. Some day the country ,will know the size of the debt it owes to the Territorials, amongst whom the Warrington Battalion will take high rank. They will hold up their heads as well as the best, and, as we said last week, the country will do its utmost to repay them for all the sacrifices they have made, as a slight recognition of all the gallant deeds they have done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Marriage.</head>
        <p>Marriage.</p>
        <p>,OMAX—GOULDEN.—On the Ist inst.. at Grap- penb.3ll, by the Rev. H. V. Pigot, William John Lomax, son of Mr. and Mrs. Ipmax,_of Botany.</p>
