      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, JULY 3. 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>OUR GALLANT TERRITORIALS.</head>
        <p>OUR GALLANT TERRITORIALS.</p>
        <p>The news that has come through during the week has gone to confirm all the informa- tion. that came to hand last week, which clearly indicated that the onslaught the Ter- ritorials assisted to make upon the German trenches a fortnight ago, will live in history. Oui splendid boys, as the Rev. W. BRACECAMP says in a letter addressed to us to-day, were heroes all. They went on desperate work and they did it in desperate fashion. It was do or dio with them. They accomplished their task and paid the price, and the more we know of them the prouder do we become. They had twenty-four hours of the most exacting work even this war will demand of any of the combatants, and some of them have been allowed a few days' leave of absence, which they have spent in coming home. They are, •verforce, reticent respecting the -doings on, that great day, though our reporters, ss-9ur</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>OUR MOTOR AMBULANCE.</head>
        <p>OUR MOTOR AMBULANCE.</p>
        <p>Warrington has been an unconscionable time in raising £6OO in answer to the MAYORESS'S appeal for that amount to enable the town to place a motor ambulance at the disposal of the mediCal authorities in France and Belgium. But the £6OO has been raised and the QUEEN has commanded all the Mayors and Mayoresses of towns who have supplied an ambulance to meet her at Buckingham Palace next Monday, when HER MAJESTY will review the fleet of motor ambulances she has been the means of raising in the towns a the country. Our MAYOR and MAYORESS will be present, and Warrington will be worthily represented among the visitors as well as in the ambulance arena. After the ceremony the visitors will be entertained to luncheon by the</p>
        <p>LORD MAYOR at the Mansion House.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>ANOTHER WORTHY OBJECT.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER WORTHY OBJECT.</p>
        <p>Now that the ambulance is comfortably out of the way, may we commend to the notice of our readers the claims of the Church _Lads' Brigade? If the success or • failure of this war had depended upon the will of Young England, there would have been an end to German militarism long ere this. The Church Lads' Brigade has furnished many of the officers and men in our magnificent Volunteer Army. From this battalion alone nine officers and 96 lads, all present members, have volun- teered for active service, and it will furnish many more. The battalion,, of which the Warrington lads form a very active partl has been officially recognised as a cadet battalion. It follows that the lads need equipping in full khaki uniform, and that means that £6O will be required, in addition to the official grant. Many claims are being made upon the public, but they are not so many that so small an amount as this, for so great a cause, cannot be collected. A circular, asking fcr dona- tions, has been issued and signed by the RECTOR as chaplain; Major J. A. BROOKES, officer commanding; and Capt. C. FORD, C.L.8., " Eversleigh," Fairfield-road, to whom all replies must be sent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Cheaper Bread.</head>
        <p>Cheaper Bread.</p>
        <p>Bread is to go down a halfpenny per 41b. loaf. The Warrington E.l.' Co-operative Society and the Master Bakers' Association announce in our advertisement columns that on and after Mon- day the price will be 71-d. and Sid. (for "seconds" and best " respectively) for loaves of the size named.</p>
        <p>Cottage Homes. A very enjoyable entertainment was given at the Padgate Cottage Homes on Thursday, arranged by Mr. W. C. Brooks. The artistes were the Misses Kilduff, Mr. Kilduff, Professor Baxteau, and Mr. J. Baines, who gave their services. Songs and solos were rendered- by the Misses Kilduff, and recitations and songs were given by Mr. Kilduff. There was an ex= ceßent exhibition of conjuring by the Pro- fessor. Mr. J. Baines gave violin solos. The Guardians present were Miss Broadbent (in the chair), Mrs. Hall and the Rev. A. M. Mitchell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 3, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 3, 1915.</p>
        <p>IMPERISHABLE</p>
        <p>GLORY.</p>
        <p>Honouring The Charge Made By Territorials.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>GLOWING TRIBUTES.</head>
        <p>GLOWING TRIBUTES.</p>
        <p>Sir Arthur and Lady Crosfield's Sympathy.</p>
        <p>Sir Arthur H. Crosfield, Bart., writing to the Mayor, says : My dear Mr. Mayor, May I avail myself of your ever kind and helpful agency in begging you to convey •to all the.relatives of those fellow townsmen of ours who have laid down their lives for the old country and for the Empire, as well as to those who have suffered in body or mind while fighting for the same great causes, the sincere and truly heartfelt sympathy of Lady Crosfield and myself? Not one of us can ever think without an acute pang of all the poignant sorrow and misery, of the terrible addition to human Suffering inflicted on so terrible a scale by this war. And it is some- thing more than the deepest sympathy one feels. The thought that a set of Militarist Junkers of Prussia, and at their head an all- powerful Emperor' whose insane egotism and crithinal ambition haVe made him their tool— the thought that with cold-blooded delibera- tion they should have made themselves re- sponsible for the most colossal crime against humanity and its well-being recorded in his- tory—that thought fills one with deep and intense indignation, and the sternest and most inflexible resolve that these reckless and un- scrupulously and unspeakably cruel malefac- tors shall be punished, and forced to at least make partial reparation. Complete repara- tion for all the sorrow and anguish they have I caused can never be made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>PRICELESS RAY.</head>
        <p>A PRICELESS RAY.</p>
        <p>We mourn with those that mourn, but even in the hour of sorrow a ray of priceless con- solation may help to dispel our gloom—the consciousness that our heroes in the field-who have voluntarily sacrificed themselves in life, or limb, or health, have devoted themselves to as great, as noble, and as sacred a cause as throughout all future time it can ever be the privilege of the men of our race to serve and defend.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>WILL RESOUND DOWN THE AGM</head>
        <p>WILL RESOUND DOWN THE AGM</p>
        <p>Last week we celebrated the centenary of Waterloo, and no prouder traditions are cher- ished in British families to-day than the claims to kinship with those who a hundred years ago withstood the tyrant and finally overthrew him. A thousand years hence Britain and Old Ireland, mighty Canada, and far-off Australia and New Zealand, our won- derful Indian Empire, and how large a p9r- tion of Africa—yes, and how many of our other smaller Colonies and dependencies?— will ring with the story of the deeds of Mons, Cambrai, Ypres, Hooge, and Neuve Chapelle, of the heroism of our Sailors on the high seas, and our soldiers in Gallipoli—while mothers tell the children at their knee of the immor- tal courage and achievements which have made the name and fame of Britain and the Empire, and which in our day saved the civilisation of Europe and the freedom of the world. It is considerations like these which may not only fire the spirit and ambition of our fellow townsmen, but which in the .hour of supreme trial at the Front may Well help to steady the nerve and strengthen the will of those who fight for the priceless traditions of Britain and principles of liberty, and carry them, as Mr. Lloyd George put it, "into the very jaws of Never in all history have men had to face a sterner, a more terrific test of courage and endurance; splendidly, nobry have they stood the ordeal, while earning for themselves, .their kith and kin and their country a heritage of imperishable glory. I remain, dear Mr. Mayor. Ever faithfully yours, ARTHUR CIIOSFIELD. 41, West Hill,'Highgate, N.. June 26th, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>MR HAROLD SMITH, M.P.</head>
        <p>MR HAROLD SMITH, M.P.</p>
        <p>Warrington's Sorrow' And Pride.</p>
        <p>Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., has forwarded the following telegram ti:o the Mayor, Alderman Peter Peacock :</p>
        <p>" Learn with much grief of awful toll paid by the South Lancashires for their undying gallantry. Desire to associate myself with Warrington's sorrow and pride.</p>
        <p>"HAROLD SMITH."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>CHAPLAIN SAYS "THEY ARE HEROES ALL"</head>
        <p>CHAPLAIN SAYS "THEY ARE HEROES ALL"</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Somewhere in France, June 29th, 1915.</p>
        <p>My dear sir,—Will you very kindly allow me through the medium of your valuable paper, to express my sympathy with those who are mourning the loss of loved ones in connection with my dear old 4th South Lancashire Regi- ment F I was privileged to be present when they marched out of camp to take their stand against the Germans in that memorable charge, when there were so many casualties, as you have already reported in your paper. I thought as. they passed me, and shouted "Good cheer," I had never seen a finer set of men in my life, ana I knew that, whatever action they were undertaking, they would do it with credit to themselves and honour to the good old town of Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>" Pride in Their Part."</head>
        <p>" Pride in Their Part."</p>
        <p>I was also present when they came back again, and I wish all your readers could have seen them, it would have stirred the heart of a stone, and it would have made any young man, who had any patriotic feelings, to have joined the colours at once. Although abso- lutely dead tired in body, and covered with mud, they went past me, headed by their brave Colonel and courageous Major, with steady but tired step, and as they passed they seemed to say, with a smile upon their faces, "Well, Padre, .we have none our bit." And they have done their bit, I can assure you, if they are never called upon to do anything more in this war. Brave, dear fellows—they are heroes, every one of them. And as I lis- tened to the Brigadier-General's words of com- mendation, don't think me unmanly when I say I couldn't restrain the tears trickling down my cheeks as I tried to say "Hear, hear," as I knew every word he said was atso- lutely true.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>When They Come Home.</head>
        <p>When They Come Home.</p>
        <p>When they come home I hope all your readers will honour them as they deserve to be honoured—and those who have "departed this life" we commend their souls to the safe keeping of Him Who is all merciful and lov- ing. I can only pray that God will comfort those who have lost their loved ones, and that those who have been wounded may soon be restored to health again. Unfortunately, I am now several miles from my own boys, and I did not know the names of all who had been killed until I got your paper last night. Thanking you in anticipation, Believe me, Mr. Editor, Yours faithfully, W. BRACECAMP, Army Chaplain,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>WAR LOAN.</head>
        <p>THE WAR LOAN.</p>
        <p>Our Duty In Providing The</p>
        <p>Silver Bullets.</p>
        <p>THE ONLY COURSE.</p>
        <p>Details of the method by which we can all lend to the Government in most advantageous conditions and so do. our share .towards beating Germany, are advertised in another column. The Prime Minister, at a meeting called this week to advertise the war loan, and initiate a conceted national movement for what might be called war economy, said waste in these times is nothing short of a. national danger.</p>
        <p>The annual in-come of this country was from 2,250 to 2,400 millions, and the annual expendi- ture of all classes was estimated at something like 2,000 millions. _ _ _ •</p>
        <p>The balance annually saved and invested was nominally between 300 and 400 millions.</p>
        <p>THE PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>How were we, who normally had only 300 to 400 millions to spare in a year, to meet this huge, unprecedented, extraordinary draft up(..n our resources? .</p>
        <p>There-were four possible courses. The first and ' most obvious was the sale of investments and property. We in the United Kingdom had inv.sted abroad something like 4,000 millions sterling. Could we draw upon that to finance the war? If we were to try to pay for the war in that way we should end it so much poorer. He dis- missed that, therefore, as out of the question. • Similar considerations seemed to him to show the impracticability of any considerable scale of a second possible expedient, namely, borrow- ing abroad. If that were possible on any considerable scale, 'We Should have to face the prospect of ending the war a debtor country. There was a third course—payment out of our gold reserves. That, however, must be dis- credited because we could not imperil the basis of the great system of credit which had made the City of London the financial centre and capital of the world. There remained only one course—to diminish our expenditure and increase our savings. If individuals saved more, they could lend the State more, and the nation would be propor- tionately enabled to, pay for the war out of its own pocket. If we consumed less we should leave a large volume of commodities available for export. For the first five months of the present year our imports had increased by 321- millions, while our exports • and re-exports had decreased by 73i millions.</p>
        <p>That meant that for twelve months our indebtedness to other countries would amount. to 260 millions.</p>
        <p>That was a serious_ prospect, and could be counteracted by reducing all unnecessary per- sonal expenditure on imported articles such as tea, sugar, wine, petrol, and also on goods which were made in this country, such as beer, so that a large quantity would be left for export.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>OUR SACRED DUTY.</head>
        <p>OUR SACRED DUTY.</p>
        <p>It is our bounden duty to save and to lend to the Government</p>
        <p>Because I am helping my country! Because it is a sound investment! Because it is the only way I can fight! Because it is my duty!</p>
        <p>The purpose of the loan is to shorten the war. The loan as an investment is perfectly safe, because it has behind it the greatest security in the world. It is a weapon in the war just as much as a rifle or a big gun. And, finally, support for--"the loan is the nation's collective act of patriotism.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>WHAT THRIFT WILL DO.</head>
        <p>WHAT THRIFT WILL DO.</p>
        <p>The Parliamentary War Savings Committee have issued leaflets dealing in simple language with the need -for thrift, how to save and how to subscribe amounts from ss. .upwards, to the War loan.</p>
        <p>"Why You Should Save" is the heading )f one of the leaflets, which set § out eight, points as follows:</p>
        <p>1. Save in every way possible for your country's sake and for your own good. 2. Save for your country's sake, because it is spending now £3,000,000 a day, and must find most of the money out of the savings of its citizens. 3. Save for your own sake, because while work and Wages are good now, hard times may come after. the war. 4. Save so that we need buy as little as pos- sible from abroad. We are buying from foreign countries £1,000,000 a day more than they are buying from us. 5. Save, because whenever we import things that are not absolutely necessary it be- comes more difficult to import and pay for all the things that are absolutely necessary. 6. Save, because the more we import from abroad the more difficult it is to prevent gold leaving the country in payment. Save, then, because our store of gold is vital, to our credit and to our financial power. • 7. Save, because by so doing you will help your country to give financial help to our Allies who are less rich than we are. 8. No saving is too small to count. If 45,000,000 people save an average of even half a crown a week-, the total is huge— it means nearly 000,000,000 a year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>UNION BANK.</head>
        <p>UNION BANK.</p>
        <p>Encouragement For Small Investors.</p>
        <p>Prospectuses for the new war loan may be, obtained, applications received and inquiries attended to at all branches of the Union Bank of Manchester, Limited.</p>
        <p>To encourage small investors, the Bank will lend to applicants for amounts not exceeding £lOO the necessary cash up to £95 per £lOO paid up, at V, per cent. interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>MINERS GIVE SUPPORT.</head>
        <p>MINERS GIVE SUPPORT.</p>
        <p>Permanent Relief Sciely ii vest £17,000.</p>
        <p>The Treasurer of the Lancashire and Cheshire Miners' Permanent Relief Society (Mr. D. Golds- worth, manager of P,atr's Bank, Ltd.,Wigan) in- vited a special attention of a meeting of the Board held last week-end to the very favour- able terms offered to investors by the Govern- ment War Loan. The members of the Society —both miners and colliery owners and their sons—had nobly responded to the urgent needs of the country by enlisting in the Army. The numbers of ordinary members of the Society who had enlisted up fo the 31st March, 1915, was 11712, and there had been further enlist- ments since.</p>
        <p>As regarded their further duty of finding , money, Mr. Goldsworth stated that he was for- tunately in the position of being able to inform the Board that there was a considerable sum available for investment. In the first place, the Society had four loans to the amount ot £12,000 falling due for repayment, and he recommended to the Board that the whole of this sum should be invested in the new War Loan. He was glad tc.• see that notwithstand- ing the large enlistment of members with the consequent loss of income, there was available for investment the sum of £5,000, being the present excess of income over expenditure, and he recommended that this sum also be invested in the War Loam This made a total of £17,000 forinvestment in the manner indictited, and he felt sure that the general body of members, and also the colliery owners associated with the Society, as well as the Board of Management, would feel a special pride that the Society had been so far enabled to fulfil its duty in the grave national position which demands so urgently, the best services of all patriotic citizens.</p>
        <p>It was moved by the Chairman (Mr. J. Dean), seconded by the Vice-Chairman (Mr. William Ingram) and resolved: That the report and recommendation of the treasurer be adopted, and the sum of £17,000 be invested in the Government War Loan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>SILVER WEDDING.</head>
        <p>SILVER WEDDING.</p>
        <p>Councillor J. T. &amp; Mrs. Locker Receive Rose Bowl.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL CLUB RECOGNITION.</p>
        <p>Councillor 'J. T. Locker, the hon. secretary of the Liberal Association, and Mrs. Locker, who joins her husband in much of his work for the party, , were the centre personages in a highly interesting gathering in the first-class billiard room at the Liberal Club on Saturday night. They had recently celebrated their silver wedding, and the fact having leaked out, a presentation, upon quiet lines, owing to the nature of the time through which we are un- fortunately passing, was inaugurated. Coun- cillor John Wilkinson, who was at the head of the movement, occupied the Chair, and the ' -attendance, in addition to Councillor and Mrs. Locker, included Alderman and Mrs. Tinnion, Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Pemberton, Councillors Firth Lindley and James Halliwell, Messrs. W. Bibby, J. Critchley, Jos. Wilkinson, J. T. Smith, F. Walker, Aaron Israel, P. H. Brown, R. Wilson, H. Johnson, and 0. Marshall. The Chairman said the presentation was tak- ing place rather late in the day, the 25th anni- versary of the wedding day having passed a month ago; but 'circumstances had made the delay inevitable. He and all hoped Mr. and Mrs. Locker would be spared for many years to perpetuate and enjoy the pleasarit associa- tions that existed among their many friends. (Applause.) ALDERMAN TINNION'S GRACEFUL TRIBUTE. -•</p>
        <p>Alderman Tinnion, in making the presenta- tion, said: I never, in any circumstances, per- , formed a duty with more genuine pleasure than I do this night. It is because of the very great respect, almost, ia fact, amounting to affection, in which we hold our friend Councillor Locker, that we have taken up this matter with genuine enthusiasm. (Hear, hear.) The presentation was contributed to by his personal friends in the isubscription room of the club, who most heartily responded. Every man here highly respects him for his sterling worth and straightforwardness. (Hear, hear.) He has been a member of this club for a great number of years, and he has' done his share of work on both the political and the social side. Mrs. Locker has often Come among us—(applause)—and we have learnt to respect her in the same degree as we respect her husband ;' and, in presenting this silver rose bowl to them on the occasion of their silver wedding, we feel that though we are not repay- ing them for their services, we are making them some slight acknowledgment of the debt we owe them. I am particularly pleased that the form the presentation has taken is that of a rose bowl. I rather hike associating roses with a function of this 'kind. Roses are the sweetest and brightest flowers in the garden, and on this, the 25th anniversary* of their marriage, it •i pleas- ant to associate roses with the garden of life. May their gardea continue to bloom with roses of contentment and happiness; may all their wishes and desires bloom and come to perfec- tion; and may they, 'when they gather those roses and look upon this rose bowl, remember the many happy years spent here doing a use- ful work amid the great esteem and affection in which \they were held. (Loud applause) Several friends, including some who were pre- sent at' the wedding, added words of congratu- lation- and gave many reminikences of the occasion. Councillor Locker, replying on his own and his wife's behalf, said the present was quite unexpected, but it would be all the more highly appreciated on that account. He thought in the present national crisis the mat- ter might easily have been overlooked, but their friends had not seen fit to overlook it. For their kindly remembrances of the wed- ding day he thanked them most sincerely. (Applause.) During the evening songs were given by Councillor Wilkinson and Messrs. P. H. Brown and G. Marshall (the Scotch come, dian).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>PRESENT.</head>
        <p>THE PRESENT.</p>
        <p>The present was a solid silver rose bowl on an ebony stand. It, bore the following in-</p>
        <p>scription :</p>
        <p>Presented to Councillcr J. T. Locker, LP., and Mrs. Locker, on the occasion of their silver wedding, May 29th. 1915, by their friends at the Liberal Club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>SALE! A SALEM</head>
        <p>A SALE! A SALEM</p>
        <p>New Scheme Afoot For Helping</p>
        <p>Wounded Soldiers.</p>
        <p>" CHRISTIE'S " AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>There is a sale on the horizon. And, like the sail on the horizon to the drifting mariner, much is expected of it. The idea is to raise funds to help wounded soldiers by holding a sale of articles of value given "for the good of the cause" by people with articles of value to give. Jewellery, pictures, ornaments and nick- nacks from the mantelpiece and the drawing- room tables, weird-headed walking sticks and quaint curiosities of all kinds (so long as they seem likely to "fetch a price ")—these articles, and many others, come in the scheme of things, and everyone who is in a position to help is earnestly asked to do so. We would make it quite plain that kitchen chairs and deal tables, discarded raiment and. second-hand perambulators are not required. This is not a "rummage sale." Live stock is invited; but there is live stock and live stock, and the latter kind is not wanted; by which we mean to convey that stray cats and genial mongrels are not looked on as "desirables." If you have a thousand pound Persian puss, .a blue-blooded bulldog or a tortoise with six legs and Several heads—bring them along, and they'll be knocked down (quite gently) to the highest bidder. The, Parr Hall has been fixed on as the "dumping ground" for the goods, but in the case of live stock of any kind, donors are 'asked to keep their gifts by them till the day of sale. Messrs. Herbert Johnson (auctioneer) and T. Hewitt, sen., of Bridge-street, are the promul- gators of the admirable scheme. Two 'meetings have been held during the week, the first on Monday night and the second on Thursday after noon. Both meetings were held in the Town Hall, and the Mayor presided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>THE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At Tuesday's meeting, the following com- mittee was elected, with power to add to its number: The Mayor and Mayoress, Mr. W. Peter Rylands, Mr. Roger C. Parr and Mr. J. H. Smethurst (representing the Infirmary), Miss Sylvia Broadbent (Raddon Court), Mrs. F. A. Frost (Thelwall Heys), Mrs. Henry Greenall (the Oaklands), the Town Clerk (the Tobacco Fund), Lieutenant-Colonel A. Simpson (Lord Derby War Hospital; on behalf of the w,</p>
        <p>soldiers), Mr. H. Johnson and Mr. T. Hewitt, Alderman James Evans (representing the com- mittee of the Lord Derby Hospital), Mr. A. Bottomley (Whitecross Hospital), Lady Greenall Lady Crosfield, Mrs. Lyon, Mrs. C. E. Parker, Mrs. Dodds, Mrs. Ralph Johnson, Mrs. Lindley, Mrs. T. Locker, Mrs. Fox, Councillor Wilson, the Rev. Irvine. Lister and Mr. H. S. Page. Mr. Hewitt, jun., was elected honorary secre- tary, and the Borough Treasurer (Mr. Fair- hurst), hon. treasurer.</p>
        <p>The aim is to support the Warrington In- firmary and other local hospitals, including Raddon Court, Thelwall Heys and Oaklands (at Daresbury), also to provide tobacco, cigarettes and other comforts for wounded soldiers in the neighbourhood. Mr. T. H. Spinks acted as secretary pro tern., but at Thursday's meeting the Rev. Irvine Lister and Mr. J. Hewitt were appointed joint hon. secs.</p>
        <p>Amongst those present at Thursday's meeting, in addition to the. Mayor and Mayoress, were: Mks. Lyon, Mrs. F. A. Frost, Mrs. Lindley, Mrs. T. Locker, Miss Broadbent, Alderman Jas. Evans, Mr. H. S. Page, and Mr. Herbert John- son (hon. auctioneer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>School Holiday.</head>
        <p>The School Holiday.</p>
        <p>With eager expectancy the school children of the town are looking forward to " breaking- up " day. The summer recess will commence on Friday, July 16th, the re-opening date being Monday, August 16th. The Parish Church Sunday Schools will be closed for three Sundays —July 25th, August Ist and August Bth.</p>
        <p>'ZwF.</p>
        <p>Taal Jintritigentt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>0144,&gt;14.14.14,14014014,1014 014.101444014,14,144444 44</head>
        <p>0144,&gt;14.14.14,14014014,1014 014.101444014,14,144444 44</p>
        <p>4-4 41 ÷ THE WARRINGTON 44 44 + 44 A .I 1 .C. ÷ 44 ÷ ÷ RAILWAY GUIDE. 44 44 44 ÷ 44 44 ÷ The June Railway Guides, for the + 014 44 most part, remain in force 44 44 414 .14 until July 12th, when the 4. + changes for the season come 014 Pr 44 into operation. 44 814 44 144 _ + 44- 44 The Warrington A.B.t. Guide for 4. 44 July will be published next 4, + Friday, the 9th inst. Pk Pk 4. + .14,14.1444•1440144444.1.4.1444.14014,14.1444•14+,44</p>
        <p>A Warrington Will. Mrs. Ann Elizabeth Jones, 51, Wilson Patten- street, widow of the late Mr. Daniel Jones, left £1,663 net personalty, and £1,756 gross.</p>
        <p>Catholic Friendly Socielleg. The annual general meeting of the Catholic Friendly Societies' Association (Catholic Philanthropic Branch, No. 7) will be held in st. Alban's School, King-street, at 7.30 on the even- ing of Tuesday, July 19th.</p>
        <p>This Year's Agricultural Show. The twenty-sixth annual show in connection with the Warrington and District Agricultural Society will be held in the Brewery Field, Wilderspool, Warrington, on Thursday, August sth. The last day for receiving entries is Mon- day, July 26th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>Park Festival.</head>
        <p>Park Festival.</p>
        <p>A well-attended rehearsal of the " Messiah " by the combined choirs, who will render it in the Park on August 22nd, took place on Tues- day evening. The next rehearsal will be ,at Cairo-street School on Tuesday, the 13th July, at 8 p.m.</p>
        <p>Bewsey Wesleyans' Special Services. Special services will be held to-morrow at the Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church, when the preacher will be the Rev. Campbell Jeffries. Special anthems will bp rendered by the choir, and in the morning the soloist will be Mr. Turner, and in the , evening Madame Sara Silcock.</p>
        <p>Pledge-Signing Campaign. . In pursuance of their pledge-signing campaign, the Warrington and District Citizens', Temper- ance League will 'hold another open-air public meeting in Bewsey-street on Friday next, com- mencing at 7.30. The meeting will be under the control of the local branch of the 8.W.T.A., and Mrs. Owen will preside, whilst Mrs. Moores will be thc speaker. •</p>
        <p>Music In the Park. The Warrington Borough Brass Band will play the following programme of music in the Park to-morrow, conductor, Mr. T. Griffin:— Hymn, "Lead, Kindly Light"; overture, "La Ewandiere (E. De Luc) ; selection, " Zar and Zimmerman (Lortzing) ; selection, "La Illustri Rival" (Mereadante) ; selection, "Sweet Eng- lish Song" (W. Seddon).; "God Save the King."</p>
        <p>Warrington Market Prices. Wednesday.</p>
        <p>Wheat a little firmer on to-day's market prices, is. to 7s. 4d. per bushel, 751 b.; oats firm at late rates, 4s. to 4s. id. per 451 b.; oat straw, 355.; wheat straw, 355. to 365. per ton; hay, clover (old), 24 10s. per ton; XX flour, 41s. per 2801 b.; bran, Is. 21-(1. per 20th.</p>
        <p>The Union Bank Savings Department.</p>
        <p>_ The Union Bank of Manchester give notice that all deposits made in their savings depart- ment will be lent to the Government for the prosecution of the war, there being an under- taking to repay on demand whenever required. All the branches of the bank are open for savings business each day duting the usual banking hours, and on Friday evenings from 7 to S. "All cannot enlist or make munitions" we are told, "but all can save a little."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>Their Last• Resting-place.</head>
        <p>Their Last• Resting-place.</p>
        <p>It has been proposed by the Cemetery Com- mittee of the Town Council to set apart a por- tion of the ground at the Cemetery for the burial of soldiers who have been _killed or who have died during the war, to divide this por- tion into three parts—for the Churchmen, the Roman' Catholics and the Nonconformists—and to erect, later on, a memorial in the centre. The Lord Bishop of the •Diocese (the Right Rev. Dr. Chavasse) has arranged to consecrate the Church portion of the new addition on Saturday, July .17th.</p>
        <p>Parish Church Sale Of N't urk " As Usual" This Year.</p>
        <p>The customary sale of work in connection with the Parish Church—which as given up last year on account of the war—will be held as usual this year. in the first week in,November, in the Fairfield Schools, during the day-school holiday. "Our parochial needs," says the Rector in the current issue of the Warrington</p>
        <p>Parish Church Magazine, "are great and-the supply of our wants cannot be postponed for another year. The various ladies' parties are hard at work, and though the calls for money are many and pressing, we are sure our people will all try to contribute to the utmost of their power."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>Belgian Street Day.</head>
        <p>Belgian Street Day.</p>
        <p>A meeting was held on Thursday evening, at the Church House, Bridge-street, the Rev. Canon Willis in the chair, to arrange for a King Albert Day, when photographs will be sold on behalf of the Belgians, as roses are on Alexandra Day and flags on Flag Day. The Rev. W. Thompson Derry, Mrs. Bracecamp, Mrs. Percival, Mr. Jos. Clarkson (of Prestwich Park), &amp;c., were also present. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Clarkson said that 2,000,000 people in Belgitun were destitute, that the number was increasing, and the funds for their support decreasing. Considerable sums of money had been raised in other towns by the method pro- posed, and the Mayor of Warrington (Alder- man Peter Peacock) had expressed his appro- val of the effort, and appointed Saturday, July 17th, as the best day for it. It was decided to hold a larger meeting for making the arrangements on Tuesday, July 6th, at the Church House, at three o'clock, and that every minister and clergyman in the town and suburbs should be asked to send to it two representatives from his church or parish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>Publicans and Their Assessments.</head>
        <p>Publicans and Their Assessments.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Warrington Union Assessment Committee, held at the Workhouse yesterday morning, Mr. D. P. Davis, the secretary of the Manchester and District Wine, Spirit and Beer Retailers' Association, appeared to offer opposition on be- half of thirty-three Warrington licence holders, and one at Padgate and another at Sankey, to projected increases in the assessment of the premises in which they were interested. It will be recalled that the matter was previously before the committee and was adjourned in order that more complete information might be gathered. At the outset Mr. Davis asked the committee to make one case the test of the whole, and selected the Greyhound Inn, War- rington, of which Mr. W. J. Shaw is the licensee. The committee decided to hear the appeal in this case, and after going,into the matter fully came to the conclusion that further considera- tion of all the appeals should be postponed for another three months. During the meeting a letter was read from Alderman Bolton, who, at a previous meeting had entered an appeal against the assessment of the licensed premises in which he is interested, and who no wrote that he found that trade .was fluctuating so much owing to the war .and to the uzicettainty of legislation as affecting the licensed trade, that he thought his appeal had better be (eft in abeyance for the presett.</p>
        <p>I Should you find any i difficulty in obtaining the EXAMINER consult</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <p>'-I</p>
        <p>That Toy Puppy! The draw for a toy puppy given by Mrs. T- Pemberton in aid of the Women's Liberia 80v; ing Fund resulted in ..t6 13s. 4d. being raise. The winner is Mrs. A. Hough, 126, Wellfield" street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>AA'eather.</head>
        <p>The AA'eather.</p>
        <p>Last week was cooler than the one which pre- ceded it, the mean temperature being one degree lower. The warmest day in the period under review was the 21st ult., when a maximum tem- perature of 73 was recorded. Last week's rain- fall, gardeners—amateurs and otherwise—should be pleased to learn was heavier than we have had locally for some time, the meterological in- struments kept by the Public Health depart- ment having recorded .86 of an inch.</p>
        <p>Contagious Diseases. During the week ended June 26th, 18 fresh cases of scarlet fever were reported to the Medical Officer of Health for the borough, there being on Saturday of last week a total of 115 patients suffering from that disease in the Borough Isolation Hospital, a fact which will suggest that there had been a recrudescence of the epidemic, which has been with the town for close upon twelve months past, and which, lt was thought, was dying out. There are at pres- ent twenty phthisis patients in the same hos- pital, and during the past week seven fresh ones were registered.</p>
        <p>Shunter's Accidental Death. A verdict of "Accidental death "was returnea</p>
        <p>at an in-CI-tie-8i at Warrington on Thursday con- cerning the death of Arthur Harrison, aged 34, of 88, Orford-lane, Warrington, a shunter in tile employ of the London and North-Western Rail- way Company, who was knocked down and killed at Bank Quay goods siding on Monday aiter- noon. The evidence showed that he was watching some waggons which had been shunted, when he stepped back on another set of rails and was knocked down by a vehicle attached to an engine which was being shunted. He died in the JW firmary a few hours later.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>Vital Statistics.</head>
        <p>Vital Statistics.</p>
        <p>.- --- The estimated population of Warrington last week-end was 75,532. During the week endeq June 26, 30 births and 26 deaths were recordeci in the borough, as against 45 births and 2•5 deaths in the corresponding period of last year: The death-rate ivas, therefore, 17.8, as againsv 17.3 in the corresponding period of last Year' and an average of 11.0 for the previous 01:( weeks, whilst the, birth-rate was 20.6, as against 31.2 last year, and an average of 26.9 in the vious six weeks. Of the 26 deaths last week, were those of persons between the ages of 25 an 65. No deaths were last week due to zymotic diseases, whilst in the same week ef last year tile, deaths from such diseases were equal to 4.1 0} the population.</p>
        <p>Leather Trade Strong. Trade continued very strong last weitlf, (writes the Warrington correspondent of I's "Leather Trades Review "), the deraanii running on most lines and prices advancing a" round. Several merchants paid a visit t° this centre and bought as freely as oppertunitY offered, the general advance in prices intlyL ing very little check. Business continued r be confined to goods for prompt or early' ce' livery, and only in a few cases Were deliveries beyond early September entertained. Taoll'r!, much prefer to sell sole leather as it COD through, or from stock in the warehouse: lConditions are so abnormal, with a continua risk of sudden changes, that the most care% estimate is little more than guesswork. ap,„ therefore, both sides are generally found to in accord in confining transactions to eari-fe delivery. The output of Army leather 10 large, much of the old arrears have heft, cleared off and, with the production now maximum, fewer complaints than formerly' 11,ft heard about short delivery. At the raot°,llt- more inquiry is being made for accoutreluelc leathers than for sole, and during tho fee.ce considerable orders for dressing lines "re been placed in the district. Light bends "fer, For- also in good demand for similar purposes. tunately, it has been possible to deal falrhe well with the former, but I am afraid, '6,0 supply of the latter is inadequate. BUSintip from the home trade has been generally' sa factory; all the usual lines were taken. readily, and were followed by a steady illcllll`i</p>
        <p>, / _AIA-- -....--..</p>
      </div>
