      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>PRIVATE ERNEST CLARE.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE ERNEST CLARE.</p>
        <p>4‘ VOL] Should Have Seen How Gliid The</p>
        <p>Captured Germans Were." Private Ernest Clare, of 23, Nestou-street,, Warrington, who took pat in the local Terri- torials big charge on June 16th, has been killed in action in a subsequent engagement. Writing to his mother he said, describing the grea -e• t charg " Our Battalion has been in a •</p>
        <p>battle. It was hell' upon earth. We have a lot of men, but I managed to pull through light. I gave ,up hope of getting out of it tel3', but God was good. " 'One of the Fiercest Bombardments of the War. cc We were in one of the fiercest bombard- „eve aof the war. There was somebody hit 14.1.3 r second. The wounded lay all over the "e 6. Some had shattered arms and legs;</p>
        <p>.Y Were buried alive. We captured a lot 'th —erinans, and you should have seen how glad et looked to have been captured. They -tholl, they will win, but we will see whether ao or not. Our lads have plenty of • thi nirs—helmets and rifles and all sorts of -begs. Our artillery knocked their trenches to -th 8.. But didn't we have a warm time with reVr artillery afterwards. When we had Nve:red and got away from the trenches, we 'e t</p>
        <p>Buried By An Old Friend. 1) • th 'i eri,...vate Clare had been about three years in ?:erritorials. He worked, after leaving 0 s :31.4111t0n-street School, for the Whitecros ti' and was employed by that firm up to the waT`e of his volunteering for active service. He 18, 4,esoiated with St. Peter's Bible Class. Fit twat() Alfred Daglash, writing to his wife lat 22) Neston-street, tells how he buried the e Private Clam, an old friend 46 will, as a '"nlrade-in-arins.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>UNOFFICIAL</head>
        <p>UNOFFICIAL</p>
        <p>Besides the above the 'following casualties have been unofficially reported to relatives :--</p>
        <p>KILLED. Corporal John Rose. Private Wm. Gibson.</p>
        <p>CASUALTIES TO DATE. Killed, 74; Wounded, 362; Missing, 4; Total 440.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>PRIVATE GEORGE HART.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE GEORGE HART.</p>
        <p>Sixteen-Year-Old "Terrier" Gives His Life For His Country.</p>
        <p>Only just turned sixteen years of age, Private George Hart has laid down his life in the 'ser- vice of his country. The news reached his home at 4, Eustace-street, through a letter written by a companion, who said that the mournful task of burying his old chum fell to his lot. _</p>
        <p>Private Hart joined the Territorials after the</p>
        <p>PRIVATE GEORGE HART. Killed in Action.</p>
        <p>outbreak of war—in September last, as a matter of fact. Whilst in training in Scotland and in the South of England he 'wrote many letters home, all of them breathing enthusiasm for the duties he had undertaken, and more than one containing an appeal to other lads of his age to come and help the old country in her time of trial. He received his education at St. Paul's day school and worshipped at St. Paul's Church: indeed for some time he was a member of the choir. After leaving school he went to work in the wire department at the Whitecross works.</p>
        <p>Vivid Description of the Big Battle. Writing home after he had safely weathered the memorable fight in which the " Terriers " covered themselves with glory and in which, along with other heroes, he played a part, the brave lad, now In his grave, said "The engagement in which we took part that day was one of the biggest in the war. Our artillery bombarded their trenches early in the morning and we rushed them, captured them and fairly cut down the enemy who occupied them, It was 'hell upon earth.' Many a time on that stirring day I said my prayers and gave myself tin for lost, but I got through all right, although I hope I shall never be called upon to go through such an ordeal again. After we took the Ger- mans' lines they made a desPerate attack. They used gas on us first and I was nearly. suffocated; in fact; I was staggering about the trench .for nearly three hours with a respirator on. Then I was knocked senseless by a bullet which hit me on the back of the head. About an hour later, after I hatl got over the stunning, I had my rifle blown out of my hand during a bout of machine gun fire to which the enemy sub- jected us. German Who Stabbed the Man 'Who Succoured Him.</p>
        <p>"The Germans are nothing but a lot of cowards. One of our fellows came across one of their men lying wounded and bandaged him up. When our fellow turned round the Ger- man stabbed him in the back with a dagger. . . . . The Germans screamed like little girls when we charged them aeross the fields, which were riddled with shell holes. We came • out top dog. I'll bet the Huns will never forget Wednesday, June 16th." The letter concludes with a reference to Private Arthur Daglish, an old friend, who, the writer says, "got through it all right." Pathetically enough, Private Daglish subsequently wrote to his wife, who resides at 21. Neston-street, Warrington, to tell bnw he assisted to bury his chum, Private Hart.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>PRIVATE WALTER HINDS.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE WALTER HINDS.</p>
        <p>Killed The Day After The Great Charge.</p>
        <p>Private Walter Hinds, whose name figures in one of the latest lists of the honoured dead, formerly resided at 55, Brick-street, and, be- fore offering himself for active service, was employed in the barb-room at Messrs. Rylands Bros.' wire works. Prior to that he was en- gaged in the fish trade, which he quitted about four years ago. He has a brother—Mr. Edward Hinds, of 24, Elaine-street—working at Messrs. Rylands's.</p>
        <p>-The dead Private received his education at St. Mary's Roman Catholic School, in Butter- market-street, and was a devout worshipper at the church from which the school takes its name. He was a member of the League of the Cross, whose headquarters are in Bewsey-street, and also of the Sacred Heart Club. His fav- ourite form of recreation was billiards, and he was a good exponent of "the art of the cue." The news of his death was conTeFed to his relatives by Sergeant Mercy. cif the Terri- torials, who had been on a flying visit to his home at Stockton Heath and who called upon the dead Inan's aunt, Mrs. Gleeson, of Church- street.</p>
        <p>Experiences in the Reserve Trenches During</p>
        <p>the Charge. Last week we published a lengthy extract</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>REPORTED DIED OF</head>
        <p>REPORTED DIED OF</p>
        <p>WOUNDS.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE WM. GIBSON.</p>
        <p>Contrade Writes That He Helped To</p>
        <p>• Bury Him. . It has been reported that Private William Gibson, son of Mr. James Gibson, plumber and painter, of Mill-street, Warrington, has died of wounds, but hopes are entertained that the report is unfounded, inasmuch as Quartermaster Ingram has informed the gallant Private's relatives that he has grounds for believing that he is still alive. "We Never Know When Our Turn Will Come."</p>
        <p>Private Gibson joined the Territorials in September, and was' allocated to B Company. Before offering his services he worked with his father as a painter and plumber. A few days ago his parents received a letter from Private</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>' At , • , , , .._ ..... . _ , ..</head>
        <p>' At , • , , , .._ ..... . _ , ..</p>
        <p>el</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <p>PRIVATE WALTER HINDS. Killed in Action.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <p>My lot were put in the support trenches. The regiment in the front trenches charged, and, as quickly as possible, we took their places in the trench they had left The bom- bardment went on continuously; I looked over the parapet and saw that the boys' were in the German trenches. Again I looked over and this time I saw them charge for the second line of the enemy's trenches. Again they were successful! Still the bombardment went on! It was like being in hell! Shells ,were drop- ping around our trench just like rain ! What a terrible sight it was for one to see his com- rades being buried .under the falls—buried sometimes alive, sometimes after being blown to pieces Enemy " Blew the Trench to Atoms."</p>
        <p>"We had to leave the trench, for they .blew it to atoms. . . . I saw our boys bring some German prisoners in We subse- quently went into yet another trench, and it was horrible to feel the earth tremble under your feet as a result of the bombardment. By this time the first line of our lads were in the captured trenches! . . . . We suffered, but the enemy were repulsed with heavier loss. We got relieved at about midnight. A piece of shrapnel hit my valise and made twelve holes in my towel and eight holes in my oil sheet and buried itself in a tin of bully-beef. I have got the piece of shrapnel." Private Hinds was killed the day following the "Terriers." historic charge. In one of his pockets, after he was killed, was found a letter from his cousin, Miss May Gleeson, as well as a tobacco pouch, which had been sent to him, and which he probably received the day before he so gallantly gave his life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>DIED OF WOUNDS.</head>
        <p>DIED OF WOUNDS.</p>
        <p>CORPORAL JOHN ROSE.</p>
        <p>A Gun-Shot Wound In The Head</p>
        <p>Which Proved Fatal.</p>
        <p>• The name of Corporal John Rose has to be added to the list of the gallant Territorials who have sacriAced their all for their King and country. News was received a week ago on Tuesday that he had been wounded, and some days later came a telegram to say that his injury had proved fatal. A gun-shot wound in the head is stated to have been the cause of his death.</p>
        <p>Said at First to Have Been Slightly Wounded. Although only 23 years of age, Corporal Rose had been in the Territorials a number of years and was married, having one child—a son, aged two years. His home was at 66, Forster-street. 'lke first that was known of his injury was the receipt of a letter from his brother-in-law, Private Albert Shepherd, of 12, Clegg-street. In that letter Private Shepherd wrote that " Jack " had been slightly wounded. "We were in the big charge," the letter continued, "and we lost heavily, but Jack is not so bad; so tell his wife to cheer up! I suppose I shall be home shortly. We came back after losing well over two hundred men."</p>
        <p>Three Brothers Serving King and Country.</p>
        <p>Three brothers of the dead Corporal are</p>
        <p>formed _officially before now.</p>
        <p>know when our turn will come."</p>
        <p>We never</p>
        <p>An Old Church Choir Boy.</p>
        <p>Private Gibson received his education at the People's College, Arpley-street, and he for- merly belonged to the choir of St. Paul's Church, being also greatly interested in the company of the Church Lads' Brigade associated with that parish. He was a member of the Borough Band, and also of the Cheshire Lines Band, and attended the Young Men's • Bible Class at St. Paul's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <p>.44#441040.161ii: ' IP • 'm•:,.'::i,!:-...,,.,:..,, .•',.;.• ' .. - . "...;::::, . ' ' -"'•-• ::"E'''....Hi!.':!;,-.ZI-..'..7:::::::i.i,...::":','9.mi ' '''',..' • "., ',•14.%•:at0r0,...,•,,,-.-........,,.,4,, . . 4,„toiliOti:,:!,- •.,,..-:-.,ti11ir1t?):"..,:,',---,:-:4.011,44.ti... --'.:,..4...,...':•*t•V4.45r.'..5' 11:,......-:-.40.04,,,,45,4%.. i ,'Q...W..- 5..*.tw,,5m0...,..,...:. ~. .....- '. •••: :.t...:::,:*:..w.,.V.A:::.i "',41t:,•j...,CA1i,;.:.. 4ir:!l '.. 1'....:•:,„:„,.,•••e.,:•,...A,..;:5• ..:-&amp;•,,,..1.::;,,:';!...:;;.!:. . iii!..': • ..,' :. 't: ."::::,,,::;::,..,..A.-...-...,....,",, 1'AiV•:!:;,..::1;,*;:n.:.4,ii ''•,•••,,,tj••.;,,,,!it... ~,,.i ,:,..;•••,,,i,:.•:::.•',.:-.1..!•,::9•:;,.,,.: ....„ . . ii7.N.:.... :,.„:-.'.:' ....i...4,F": .!;::::....?,•:'";-'..544).; 10:f.',4:2i,..4.,.,,5ii:T.:1,,;:.:::::"...i.4k . ~.•.:.;il:444:lfitillofirViltrelk ..:4........:um1,!%R.ifa'e.:.:111r.:...:,••••40k.;`,':r.. .i..,',411im0.0.* .. , . ...liiitiOliiiiiii*ltZTlMPltikiEltilligil'3o. ?..,...•.:., ..„,•:-.A.p04:?..51.,:::'..!:47,.?•"....•'..i.1:Y..5. *AtigittitMankii4ftilf!.:.. . 1 • . . ~ . • .41k1=';',.t4:: :4,V.tiiitilti.ttttl ..ii, • • ...: „...,.....,......„....„..,.!..!,:.iiim0ggRa,m,,:,....h,,,,,,,... - - ...' . ~: ... „</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE C. E. LITTLER.</p>
        <p>Says " Terriers " Were Under Shell- Fire Nineteen Hours.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Littler, 45, Birchall-street, received on Thursday morning a letter from her husband, Private Charles Edward Littler, to say he had been wounded in the head by a fragment .of a shell. "Don't upset yourself," continued the letter,-" for I shall soon •he all right. It is a wonder I have not been blown to pieces. The shell dropped right in my trench, and I don't know where my mates are. I got wounded on Saturday, June 26th."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>Went Through the B;g Fight.</head>
        <p>Went Through the B;g Fight.</p>
        <p>Private Littler came through the big fight a fortnight ago with slight injury, his back being hurt through being buried in a shell eiplosion. "We were under shell fire for nineteen hours," he said in a letter to his wife, "and only had time to get a. drink of water. The Germans are the biggest lot of cowards you ever saw. They put the white flag up when we got up to them." Private Littler rejoined the Territorials on the outbreak of war, having served in them seven years previously. He is at present a mem- ber of B Company. He formerly worked for Messrs. IL Fairclough, builders.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>PRIVATE PERCY DAMN.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE PERCY DAMN.</p>
        <p>Writes To Tell His Wile That His</p>
        <p>Wound "Is Not Very Bad."</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning the wife of Private ,Percy Dakin, who lives at 15a, Nora-street, Warrington, received a field card to say that he had been wounded, a postcard following shortly afterwards. "I am going on all right" said I the postcard. "My wound is not very bad. I am in hospital in France, and I expect to go on leave before very long. I got hit on Satur- day, a niece of shell catching me in the shoulder."</p>
        <p>Private Dakin, who was employed at the Hey.. dock-street Tannery, has been in the Terri- torials about two years, and he is 23 years of age. He received his education at the National School in Church-street, and went to the Front in February. He is well-known in local athletic circles as a good runner. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>PRIVATE R. J. BLEASDALE.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE R. J. BLEASDALE.</p>
        <p>Thought The Top Of His Head Was</p>
        <p>Blocvn Off.</p>
        <p>Private Richard James nleasdale, a married Warringtoft Territorial, whose home is at 33, Andley-street, is amongst those whose names ,ippear in the latest casualty list of the 4th South Lancs. He is now in hospital at Leeds .and is doing well. In a letter to his wife, he ,ays : "I am sorry to let you know I am wounded, but don't upset yourself as I am doing ~s well as can be expected. I was on duty in the trenches on Friday morning (June 25th), It about 6 o'clock, when all at once I felt such d thud. I thought the top of ray head was ;one, but, thank God, it wasn't so bad as that. The bullet went straight across the back of my head. I am lucky to be here and able to write and tell you about it.</p>
        <p>Old Volunteer Who Joined Up When the Call Came.</p>
        <p>Private Bleasdale is an old Volunteer, and he joined the Territorials in August. He had previously held the rank of Corporal in the Battalion. He served in the South African War with the Volunteeirs, and has one medal. Prior to the present war, he was employed by Messrs. Thewlis, Griffiths and Edelste.n, Ltd., of Mersey-street. He is 32 years of age. Private Bleasdale is. a member of the Order of Shepherds. He attends Emmanuel Church, in Bold-street. As a lad he was educated at St. James' School, Latchford. His brother William is also with the Colours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>PRIVATE F. HUNT.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE F. HUNT.</p>
        <p>The Nature Of His Hurts Not Yet</p>
        <p>Known.</p>
        <p>Private F. Hunt, 'whose name appears in the list of Territorials wounded in the most recent fight in' which, so far as is known, they par- ticipated, makes his borne with Mrs. Filling- ham, at 103, Sharp-street. He received his in- jury, the precise nature of which is not yet known, as recently as Monday of the present week, news to that effect being conveyed on a field card, which reached Warrington on Wed- nesday morning. Interested in Football.</p>
        <p>As a boy, Private Hunt attended the Silver- street School. He was interested in football, and he has been in the Territorials a matter of about twelve months, being among the first to volunteer his services when the call came for men for the Front. He is attached to D Com- pany. He was employed by Messrs. Rylands' Bros., in the wet shop. He worshipped at 'St. Peter's Church. A brother of his is serving in the Army Service Corps.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>VERY BEST FELLOWS GOING.</head>
        <p>VERY BEST FELLOWS GOING.</p>
        <p>Major Crosfield's Tribute to Lieu- tenants Holden and Frost.</p>
        <p>Major George R. Crosfield, writing from the trenches to a friend in Lymm. says: "We are all here very sad at the loss of Legh Holden and Lionel Frost—two of the very best fellows going. We had a stiff time the other day, but the Warrington lads did right well, and are justly proud of themselvts."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>IN THE CHARGE.</head>
        <p>IN THE CHARGE.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN O'BRIEN.</p>
        <p>Slightly Crushed "When A Trench Exploded.</p>
        <p>Private John O'Brien, of 12, Catherine-street, who, prior to the war, was engaged by the L. and N.-W. Railway Co. as an engine driver, was injured on the day of the big • charge by the Warrington Territorials. .</p>
        <p>He w-rites to his wife from Chichester as follows: "I am not wounded; so never mind what they. say. I never felt better in my life. ,I was slightly crushed by an exploded trench. There is no mark about me. I am not going to France again." , He adds "Please send me The Examiner' on Saturday."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>PRIVATE RICHARD LEIGH.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE RICHARD LEIGH.</p>
        <p>Received His Wound In The Big Bayonet Charge.</p>
        <p>Private Richard Leigh wrote to his home, 43, Fomter-street, Warrington, on June 20th, to say "I got wounded in the bayonet chatge the previous Wednesday.. The wound is in the right thigh. lam now in hospital. The sisters are very good, and will do anything for our comfort."</p>
        <p>Private Leigh rejoined the Territorials on the outbreak of war. He had previously been in them, but left when his Iree years expired. When the call came, however, he felt that he could not be out of it, and so he again offered his services, which were accepted. He re- ceived his education at the Silver-street School and afterwards went to work at the Whitecross Works, where he was employed at the time of offering. A football enthusiast, he had played for his works club in many of their matches. He is 34 years ot age.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>PRIVATE ENOCH HOULSTON.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE ENOCH HOULSTON.</p>
        <p>Thought He Had Been Cut In Two"</p>
        <p>Aviien A Shell Burst Near Him.</p>
        <p>"I got hit Min the arm and shoulder," writes Private Enoch Houlston, of the Territorials, to his mother, who resides at 3, Bond-street, Sankey Bridges, his letter being dated from the Portland Ward of the Second Eastern General Hospital, Portland-road, Hove, near Brighton. Sussex. "The hit in the arm is not much," he Continues, "but I don't know what to say about the wound in the shuarder. I think it is hurt badly. I have got the piece of shell which struck me; it weighs two or three ounees. I was in. that big. battle the other 'week. . . I had a lot of visitors on Sunday. Being here is like entering a heaven after com- ing from a hell. I do not know whether I am to go back again, but, anyway, it will be a long time before I do. Paid the Gerrrrarrs Back In Their Own Coin. "The 'Germans didn't half 'give it us,' but, by frive. We paid them back in their own coin. I got hit when one of the enemy's 'Jack John- burst—one of those shells which, .when they exprode, maken hole so big that von could put a wagon into it. The force of the eX- Tilosion brew my straps and pouches off, my ba,.k, and lifted me. about three feet in the air. I thought it had cut me in two."•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>Played Football For The Whitecross Team.</head>
        <p>Played Football For The Whitecross Team.</p>
        <p>Private Houlston is a single man, 21 years of age, and he has been in the Territorials about four years. Prior to volunteering fqr active, service he was engaged in the wire ropery at the Whitecross Works. At one time he played football in the works team. lie has a brializ.r, Donald, serving as a rifleman in the ltifle Brigade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>RIFLEMAN A. R. FORSHAW.</head>
        <p>RIFLEMAN A. R. FORSHAW.</p>
        <p>Suffering From A Gun-Shot Wound</p>
        <p>In The Leg.</p>
        <p>Rifleman Alexander Robert Forshaw, of the 3rd Battalion of the Rifle Brigade, lies in hos- pital suffering from a gunshot wound in the leg received in recent fighting. This infprmation was forwarded to his father, Mr. Thos. rorshaw, who resides at, 59, Hamilton-street, and who is a carter in the employ of the Corporation. This is the second time the gallant rifleman has been wounded.</p>
        <p>Rifleman Forshaw, who was only 19 'years of age in December last, joined the Rifle Brigade on September 2nd. He has been wounded twine, the last time being on June 16th. 'Prior to offering his services to his country he was employed at the Whitecross Works.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>WARRINGTON D.C.M.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON D.C.M.</p>
        <p>Local Territorial Gains Coveted Distinction At The Front.</p>
        <p>Congratulations to Sergeant Tom Widd and his parents. Sergeant Widd, whose father is the licensee of the Roan Cow Inn, Bridge Foot, has won the Distinguished Conduct 'Medal for bravery in the .field. He is one of the old Volunteers, and went out in February with the</p>
        <p>ber of the Ancient Order of Foresters, whose Court meetings he regularly attended. A Roman Catholic, he was a member of the congrega- tion of St. Mary's Church, Buttermarket-street. He passed away in a military hospital at Boulogne, leaving his wife with an eleven- months-old child to. mourn their loss. A brother-in-law of his—Carporal C. Wooldridge, Of the Ist Battalion of the Lancashire Fusi- liers, and a son of Mr. George Wooldridge, of Brooklands-street—lost his life in his country's service during a fierce engagement in the vicinity of the Dardanelles on May 27th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>SERGEANT TOM WIDD, b.c.k</head>
        <p>SERGEANT TOM WIDD, b.c.k</p>
        <p>Warrington Territorials. He is twenty-five years of age, and has been associated with the Volunteers and Territorials for a matter of nearly ten years. Well-known Footballer and Athlete.</p>
        <p>Well-known locally as a footballer and athlete, Sergeant Widd's chief line is rearing and selling poultry. He has managed the affairs of various poultry firms with great skill and splendid results, both from the prize-winning and general .business point of view. The D.C.M. was awarded him for his gallantry on June 16th, the day of the big charge by the Warrington Territorials. He anticipates being granted leave shortly, as part of the reward for his distinguished service. A Brother Serving With The Colours.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Albert Widd, the parents of the gallant Sergeant, have another son with the Colours, Walter Widd, who is in the King's Royal Rifles. Sergeant Widd has played for Newton-le- Willows P.C., and also for Warrington Second- ary School. At the time of enlistment for war, he was employed at the London and North- Western Railway Works at Earlestown, as a wheelwright.</p>
        <p>- s , , ^ , ~."-• 'i.i.'k4k, , „ ~,,*‹..% ......,, z u ,av -.,, /4.1 - ' • - -''' '.,t,,,,,,..„4 , •41„ , , s , 1.5" •k,.. . -, . - , ' t 4 , , ...0 , -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>WAR PRISONERS' INGENUITY.</head>
        <p>WAR PRISONERS' INGENUITY.</p>
        <p>Warrington war prisoners in Germany wrote home to say they were very short of food. Their remarks were censored. Two of them, how- ever, again tried—and this time succeeded—in sending home the news thus:— "We are in a beautiful spot, but-would be much happier if Mr. Turog or Mr. Ifovis were with us."</p>
        <p>Another letter was addressed: "Mrs. I. A. M. Clenuain. cio Mrs. Holbrook, Brick-etteet.".</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>PRIVATE J. C. MADAM.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE J. C. MADAM.</p>
        <p>Brother Of Warrington Sub-Librarian</p>
        <p>Gives His Life.</p>
        <p>Users of the Free Library and Museum in Bold-street - will learn with regret that the courteous sub-librarian, Mr. John McAdam, has lost his brother in the wat. Private James Cameron McAdam was killed in action on the 16th of last month. It was at Hooge that he gave his life for his country, falling in the memorable charge made by the Liver- pool Scottish, who in this historic engagement lost 43 men killed and had no fewer than 211 wounded. Young McAdam—he was only 20 years of age—was not widely known in War- rington, although he had paid many visits to the town. He was the youngest son of , Mr. and Mrs. James Cameron McAdam, of Bootle, and he only joined the Liverpool Scottish on the outbreak of war. He had a bright future, for after finishing his educatich at the Bed- ford School, Bootle, he too* fifth place in a competitive examination, open to the whole country, for marine inspectors, and had obtained a good berth with the British and Foreign Marine Insurance Company, whose head offices are in Liverpool. A brother of the dead soldier is serving with a motor ambulance con- voy in France.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>FIVE SOLDIER BROTHERS.</head>
        <p>FIVE SOLDIER BROTHERS.</p>
        <p>Proud Warrington Mother's Letter Of</p>
        <p>Appreciation From king.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Lucy Bluck, of 53, liowleY-lane, War- rington:, is the proud possessor of a letter of congratulation, written on behalf of His Majesty, King George the Fifth, by the Keeper of the Privy Purse, Sir F. W. Ponsonby. The letter conveys to the gratified recipient "on ex- pression of His Majesty's appreciation of the patriotic spirit" which has prompted her five sons to give their services at the present time to the Army. "The King," it proceeds, "was much gratified to hear of the manner in which they have so readily responded to the call of their Sovereign and their country, and I am to express to you and to them His Majesty's con- gratulations on having contributed in so fall a measure to the great cause for which all the people of the British Empire are so bravely fighting."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>Four Sons Actuallj in the Fighting Line.</head>
        <p>Four Sons Actuallj in the Fighting Line.</p>
        <p>Four of the five sons referred to are actually in the fighting line "somewhere in France," or Belgium, and the fifth is in the Royal Garri- son Artillery—if necessity a home defence fc rce —in the South of England. The three eldest sons have the proud distinction of starving vdth</p>
        <p>the Warrington Territorials. They are Private Edward Bluck, Private Wm. Bluck, and Private John Arthur Bluck. Private Edward is the eldest of the gallant family, being 3i. Before he .volunteered for active service he worked for the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co.. Ltd. Private William is married, and prior to c.ffer- ing for the Front he was employed on HoFic...y Quay. He is 31 years of age. Private John. is 29, and he was in the 'employ of Messrs. It. A. Naylor, Ltd., and is a member of the Con- servative Club. Of the other two brothers, Private Charles Dunn Bluck, aged 27, is ;t mem- ber of the sth (Territorial) Battalion of the King's Own Royal Lancaster Regiment, whilst Gunner Percy, aged 25, is the one who is serv- ing his country by helping to defend her shores from the invader. Before he "joined up" he I was employed by the Warrington C,orporatiol, in their highways department. Private Charles was at one time a member of the Junior Con- servative Club in Orford-lane, and prior to enlisting he was working at Lancaster as a cabinet-maker for Messrs. Waring and Gill9r.r. Tie served his apprenticeship to his trade with Mr. Ralph Johnson, of Warrington. All Old National School Boys.</p>
        <p>He received the first part of his schooling ' at the National School, but later attended the ' St. James' Day School in Latchford, together with the three younger of his brothers, the eldest, Private Edward, having completed 'his' education in the school at which the whole quintet started their scholastic careers. The four unmarried of the gallant five lived at home with their mother, who. is the widow of a grocer—Mr. William Bluck—who formerly carried on business in the town and who died in March of this year. He was brother to Mr. John Bluck—who therefore is uncle to the patriotic quintet—who is likewise a grocer, trading in Mersey-street. All the soldier- brothers were keenly interested in football.</p>
        <p>The Governor and Company of the Bank of Eng- land are authorized to receive applications for this Loan, which will take the form either of Inscribed Stock, or Bonds to Bearer, at the option of the Subscribers.</p>
        <p>If not previously redeemed the Loan will be re- paid at par on the let December, 1945, but His Majesty's Government reserve to themselves the right to redeem the Loan at par at any time on. or after, the Ist December, 1925, on giving three calendar months' notice in the "London Gazette." Both Capital and Interest will be a charge on the Consolidated Fund of the United Kingdom. The books of the Loan will be kept at the Bank of England and at the Bank of Ireland. Dividends will be paid half-yearly on the Ist June and Ist December. Dividends on Stock will be paid by Warrant which will be sent by post. Dividends on Bonds will be paid by Coupon. Inscribed Stock will be convertible into Bonds to bearer at any time without payment of any fee: and Bonds to bearer will be exchangeable for In- scribed Stock on payment of a fee of one shilling per Bond.</p>
        <p>The instalments may be paid in full on or after the 20th July, 1915, under discount at the rate of 41 per cent, per annum. In case of default in the payment of any instalment by its proper date, the deposit and the instalments previously paid will be liable to forfeiture.</p>
        <p>Scrip Certificates to bearer, with Coupon attached for the dividend payable on the Ist December, 1915, will be issued in exchange for the provisional re- ceipts. As son as these Scrip Certificates have been paid in full they can be inscribed (i.e., can be converted into Stock); or, they can be exchanged for Bonds to bearer (as soon as these can be pre- pared) in denominations of £lOO. £2OO, £5OO. .21,000, 5,000, and £lO,OOO. Inscribed Stock will be transferable in any sums which are multiples of a penny. •</p>
        <p>CONVERSION OF</p>
        <p>£3 10s. per cent. War Loan, 1925-1928. £2 10s. per cent. Consols. £2 15s. per cent. Annuities.</p>
        <p>.22 10s. Per cent. Annuities</p>
        <p>Holders of £4 10s. per cent. War Loan. 1923-1945. will have the additional right, IN RESPECT OF EACH £lOO STOCK (OR BONDS) HELD BY THEM, and fully-paid in cash, to exercise one or other of the four following options of conversion. PROVIDED APPLICATION FOR CONVERSION IS MADE NOT LATER THAN THE 30th, OCTOBER, 1915. Option 1. Conversion of .-E3 10s. per cent. War Loan, 1925-1928. To exchange Stock (or Bonds) of £3 10s. per cent. War Loan, 1925-1928, to an amount not ex- ceeding £lOO nominal, for fully-paid Stock (or Bonds) of £4 10s. per cent. War Loan, 1925-1945. at the rate of .{:100 of the former, with a cash pay- ment of £5 per cent. thereon. for £lOO of the latter. Persons who exercise this option will re- ceive the dividend of £1 10s. 11d, per cent. pay- able on the Ist September, 1915, in respect of the £3 10s. per cent. War Loan, 1925-1928, sur- rendered, and a. full half-year's dividend of £2 ss.</p>
        <p>per cent:, payable on the Ist December, 1915,- in respect of the £4 10s. per cent. War Loan, 1925,- 1945, issued in lieu thereof.</p>
        <p>Option 2. Conversion of £2 10s. per cent. Consols. To exchange Stock (or Stock Certificates) of £2 les. per cent. Consols, to an amount not ex- ceeding £75 nominal, for fully-paid Stock (or Bonds) of £4 10s. per cent. War Loan, 1925-1945, at the rate of £75 of the former for £5O of the latter. Persons who exercise this option will re- ceive the usual quarter's dividend of 12s. 641. per cent.' payable on the sth October, 1915, in re- spect of the £2 10s. per cent. Consols surrendered, and a full h-alf-year's dividend of £2 ss. per cent., payable on the Ist December, 1915, in respect of the £4 10s. per cent. War Lon, 195-1945, issued in lieu thereof.</p>
        <p>Option 3. Conversion of 2 15s. per cent. Annuities. -</p>
        <p>To exchange Stock (or Stock Certificates) of £2 15s. per cent. Annuities, to an amount not exceeding £67 nominal, for fully-paid Stock (or Bonds) of £4 10s. per cent. War Loan. 1925-1945, at the rate of £67 of the former for £5O of the latter. Persons who exercise this option will re- ceive the usual quarter's dividend of 13s. 9d. per cent., payable on the sth October, 1915, in re- spect of the £2 15e. per cent. Annuities surren- dered, and a full half-year's dividend of £2 ss. per cent., payable on the Ist December, 1915, in respect of the £4 10s. per cent. War Loan, 1925- 1945, issued in lieu thereof.</p>
        <p>Option 4. Conversion of £2 10s. per cent. Annuities.</p>
        <p>To exchange Stock (or Stock Certificates) of £2 10s. per cent. Annuities, to an amount not. exceeding £7B nominal, for fully-paid Stock (or Bonds) of £4 10s. per cent. War Loan, 1925-1945, at the rate of £7£3 of the former for £5O of the _ .</p>
        <p>latter. Persons—,Ac:-eX-e-ici—s-e- this option will re- ceive the usual quarter's dividend of 12s. 6d. per cent. payable on the sth October, 1915, in respect of the £2 10s. per cent. Annuities surrendered, and a full half-year's dividend of £2 ss. per cent., payable on the Ist December, 1915, in respect of the 4 10s. per cent. War Loan, 1925-1945, issued in lieu thereof.</p>
        <p>In the event of future issues (other than issues made abroad or issues of Exchequer Bonds, Treasury Bills, or similar short-dated Securities) being made by His Majeiaty's Government, for the purpose of carrying on the War, Stock and Bonds of this issue will be accepted at par, plus accrued interest, as the equivalent of cash for the purpose of sub- scriptions to such issues. A commission of one-eighth per cent, will be allowed to bankers, brokers and financial houses on allotments made in respect of cash applications for this issue bearing their stamp; but no commission will be allowed in respect of applications for con- version.</p>
        <p>Application forms for cash subscriptions may be obtained at the Bank of England and the Bank of Ireland; at any bank or money order office in the United Kingdom; of Messrs. Mullens, Marshall and 3, George-street, Mansion House, E.C.; and of Co..ihe principal stockbrokers. Application forma for conversion will be forwarded with each letter of allotment. THE LIST OF APPLICATIONS WILL BE CLOSED ON OR BEFORE SATURDAY, THE .10th JULY, 1915.</p>
        <p>BANK OF 'ENGLAND, LONDON, 21st June, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
