      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>FIRST " TERRIERS " ON LEAVE.</head>
        <p>FIRST " TERRIERS " ON LEAVE.</p>
        <p>Captain And Sergeant Accorded Rousing Reception.</p>
        <p>As previously announced ni our columns, a new system of leave has been introdueed at the Front, by means of which two men from each company are in turn allowed five day' leave— which will permit the Lyn= men-each to have three days at home as their turns come. Of course it may take some few weeks for the turns of some to come round, but the .difficul- ties of any leave -at all are 'perfectly appreciable; and 'happily the Lymm " lads " do not mind waiting so long as they know their leave is coming. And it is quite certain that. every man will get his leave at the earliast possible moment. In fact, this leave system is now stated, and in their due and proper turn the first two local members of the 4th Cheshire Territorial Battalien have paid such a visit -home; for on Thursday evening Captain G. W. C. Hartley and Sergeant Harold Clare arrived home from the Front—where they had been since Feb- ruary.</p>
        <p>The, occasion was one of great celebration, for the Captain is exceedingly popular not only with his colleagues and men, but also with every- body in Lymm. The News Leals Out.</p>
        <p>The news that they were coming was only received the same night in a message to Mrs. Hartley (the Captain's mother) at Wormil.l Green. ' Therein it was stated that they were due in Warrington at 8.20. Mr. Basil Hartley motored to meet his br3ther at Bank Quay Station, conveying both the visitors home via Thelwall. In the meantime, however, the news of this visit—which only permits of three days at home —had leaked out in the township, reaching the local platoon of the Cheshire Volunteers—who were at the Drill Hall. under Sergeants S. D. Moston and J. Lister Farrar. Also there was present 'Lieutenant Wilde, of Leconby, At present there are a hundred Northwich 'rerritorials billeted in Lvmm, in charge of Lieutenant Milne. Their Sergeant, Allen—who is a commde of Sergeant Clare, and is back home from 'having been wounded—also heard the news, and asked permission of the Lieutenant to call a parade to accompany the Volunteers in iheeting Captain Hartley and his Sergeant. This permission was granted, and in a very short time they were as pleased as sur- prised to be called out of the various church- clubs and assemble. Procession to Thelwall.</p>
        <p>Accordingly the Volunteers and Northwich " Terriers " formed procession and marched to Thelwall—followed by a large crowd of local civilians. On 'arrival at Brook Bridge, Thel- wall, they lined up and awaited the advent. of the motor—with eager eyes cast along the road. At. the same time the Volunteer Sergeant sent out a' party of four to meet the 'motor on the road and warn its occupants of how they were awaited. The party—which was under Sergt. Farrar—duly met the motor and gave the new arrivals warm words of welcome. Along with them, the motor then proceeded slowly to the bridge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>Hundreds Present.</head>
        <p>Hundreds Present.</p>
        <p>Here the bong column stood at attention, and reached from the bridge to the joint road to Thelwall Station. Lieutenant Wilde im- mediately called for three hearty cheers, which were given most enthusiastically by the hundreds present, and followed by "He's a jolly good fellow." The Captain, who was very touched, stepped out of the car and shook hands with several of the men, saying how glad he was to see them wearing His Majesty's uniforms. .</p>
        <p>All then proceeded along the main road to the Drill Hall, the Volunteers branching off at the Nag's Head Hotel to form a guard of honour at Wormald Green. Thp " Terriers " did like- wise at the Drill Hall.</p>
        <p>In that hall both the Captain and Sergeant Clare were earned shoulder high.. amid loud cheers, and Captain Hartley, with character- istic modesty and brevity, acknowledged how deeply he appreciated the motives which had prompted the reception. Territorial Token.</p>
        <p>Yesterday the Territorials at Lymm fully ex- pressed the pleasure they had been afforded by being permitted to attend and participate in the reception.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>STATHAM ARMY RECORD.</head>
        <p>STATHAM ARMY RECORD.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Booth's Sixty Years' Connection</p>
        <p>• With Welsh Regiment. Company-Quartermaster-Sergeant J. Hewitt, of the 84th Brigade Ist Welsh Regiment, writes from France to Councillor F. Lockyer, of the Star Inn, Statham:—</p>
        <p>"I have heard that the rebord of the Booth family I sent you [for The Examiner ') has unfortunately been mislaid, so I have again drawn it up. • "In considering records of Army life, I feel that Lymm and the Welsh Regiment in par- ticular are proud of the record. of Mrs. Booth, Statham, who has been represented in the above regiment by one of her sons or sons-in-law since 1857—nearly sixty years! Altogether father, six sons, one son-in-law, and one grandson have been in the Army; total 109 years. out of which 100 years have been served with the Welsh Regiment alone. I think this is hard to beat— more so as half of it has been abroad. We are indeed very proud.</p>
        <p>RECORD ANALYSED. Deceased.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Booth (father), 22 years, mostly abroad. Private R. Booth (son), 19 years, mostly abroad.</p>
        <p>Serving, Lance-Corporal A. H. Booth (son), 14 years; South African War; now with National Reserve. Private ,L Booth (son), 9 years, South African War; now in France.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal John Booth (son), 10 years; now in France; previously wounded in both camnaigns,</p>
        <p>C.Q.M.S. Hewitt (son-in-law), 25 years, mostly abroad; South African War; now in France. Private S. Booth (grandson), 3 years; wounded twice in present war.</p>
        <p>Total service. Welsh Regiment. 102 years. Private Joe Booth. 7 years, old Volunteers. Private C. Booth, 3 months; now serving with 4th Lancashire Fusiliers.</p>
        <p>To,tel record. 109 years, 3 months.</p>
        <p>_ . , "I have written to recognised authority to get Charlie transferred to .our own regiment. I should say that Lymm has proved itself a detached depot of the Welsh Regiment. I might mention that Mrs. Booth—who is now 76 years of age—has seen a lot of India and Africa. Also her daughter (Mary) has travelled in Egypt and India. returning home with her husband at the outbreak of war."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>FISIIING IN THE LAKE.</head>
        <p>FISIIING IN THE LAKE.</p>
        <p>Council's Decision As to Area</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>Prohibited.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Urban Council on Mon- day evening, was approved me following minute of the Roads Committee: "Resolved that fishing be allowed from all parts of the lake exceptinq the part abutting on the :woods; and that boards be fixed, and lettered: 'Fishing is prohibited from the woods abutting on the lake. 'Trespassers will be prosecuted.'</p>
        <p>. - - . . --- . .. , , v . , . . . , 1.. • . , • . .1.. . . ~ -------- -...........- --- -..-... --. :-.: ~ ' -:- " • ...- ' . -'-'.....t..........:;.. .7.477:-1-.."' -0,...........U. .</p>
        <p>, , 'A' , '4,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>IST RI CT /JEWS</head>
        <p>IST RI CT /JEWS</p>
        <p>: E. ....tr.</p>
        <p>Footpath Notice Boards.—The Council Roads Cammitt;_pe has decided that notice boards shall be fixed to indicate foofpaths, as per the report of the Surveyor (Mr. D. Mort).</p>
        <p>'1 Lam.p to be Removcd.—The Council approves a minute of the Roads Committee, which 7 -decides that the lamp at the junction of , 4 Crouchley-lane and New-road shall be removed, as arranged with Mr. Owen.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hamilton, M.P.—At a meeting of the Council Gas Committee, was read from Mr. Hamilton, M.P., a letter saying he was writing tte support the resolutions passed by the Gas and Electrical Institute re supply of coal.</p>
        <p>Thanks For Gallant Workman's Services.— The Gas Committee of the Urban Council have passed a vote of thanks to Mr. W. T. Moss for services rendered by his son at the Gas Works ,on the 11th inst., when several of the workmen were gassed.</p>
        <p>Team Labour Increased Cost.—At a meeting •of the Council Roads Committee, a letter was read from the Team Owners' Association (per Mr. Allman), giving notice that the team labour would be increased from 8. per day to 95., as and from July lst.—The Council approved.</p>
        <p>"Council Coal Tender.—On the recommenda- tion of the Water Committee, the Council accepted the tender of the Bridgewater Trustees to supply 150 tons (more or less) or steam slack coal for the period ending December 31st—at 17S. per ton, subject to the usual con- tract.</p>
        <p>Private George Appleton.—"l was surprised" writes one of the Territorials, "to see George Appleton's photo in the paper as having been wounded. I am glad it was no worse, for it's an awful time when we are getting shelled; we have to chance our luck. I was also very sorry to hear about. Jim Lawless having that acci- dent."</p>
        <p>Increased Gas Price.—The Council on Mon- day evening adopted the recommendation of the 'Gas Committee that the price of gas be in- creased: For ordinary lighting consumption, from 3s. to 3s. 6d. per 1,000 cubic feet; for cookers and stoves, from 2s. Bd. to 3s. 2d. per 1,000 cubic feet. Also discount on slot meters will not be allowed. The order takes effect from Thursday, June 24th.</p>
        <p>;</p>
        <p>German Trophies at Statham.—Sergeant J. Hewitt, of the Ist Welsh Regiment, 84th Bri- gadp, has sent his wife at Cross Brow, Statham, wale very interesting war relics. • They include two shell-cases of ,the famous 75-inch French shells—which, he says, the Germans mortally detest"; two sets of cartridge-cases on metal bands; and a large metal badge, bearing the motto, "In Treue Fest," taken from a German helmet. The helmet, adds the Sergeant, will follow later.</p>
        <p>Motor Danger Boards.—The Council Roads Committee wrote to the Automobile Association and Motor Union, asking if they would supply boards warning motorists against the clanger of the roads at the junction ot Warrington- road. The reply runs: "The matter of danger- signs has not been dealt with for some time, -owing to the national crisis; but I understand it is possible in the near future that considera- tion will be given to such matters. In that event the inspection will be made, and due notice will be given you of tie result.—Yours faithfully, F. W. Hatton, northern manager."</p>
        <p>1 ' :, i' ‘/</p>
        <p>Terrific Maxim Fire.—" The last time we went into action," writes C.Q.M.S. Hewitt, of the Ist Welsh, " was Whit-Monday. Our losses, with no exaggerating, were roughly eleven officers and over four hundred N.C.O.'s and men. We have been strengthened, and are now fit for the trenches again, to get a bit of our own back. The maxim fire was terrific. We are a long way from finishing it; but I am certain the Runs are having quite the worst of it—and are totally fed up, from what I can gather. I my- -self have not come across any Lymm boys, but should like to."</p>
        <p>There—Present at the ordinary monthly meeting of the Urban District Council' held on. Monday evening, were: Councillors R. 11. Gotobed (in the chair), C. E. Cowper, S. Critchley, W. G. Groves, F. Lockyer, G. :H. Parker and A. Macintyre. Also there were in attendance Messrs. W. Mullard (clerk and col- lector), W.. Timperley and Cecil Mort (assistant clerks), C. I. Barnes (waterworks manager), T. H. West (gas manaver) and D. Mort (surveyor and inspector), and Dr. E. M. De Jong (medical 'officer). At the outset of the meeting the Chairman mentioned that this was the first Council meeting of the year at which Coun- cillor Lockver had been present; and in the name of the members he congratulated Mr. Lockyer on his recovery, and gave him a hearty welcome on his return to the Council Cham- ber. Councillor Lockyer feelingly replied, and thanked the members for their kind ex- / pression of sympathy and welcome.</p>
        <p>lAYMM</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEERS.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>Altrinehana And Lymm Battalions Help On Recruiting. We were realising out at the Front the full horrors of the war; and our people at home had not wakened up yet! Since then, thank Heaven, Britain has wakened up. It has realisecV that this is a war in which the British Army, alone, cannot fights on its behalf.</p>
        <p>So spoke Lieutenant J. D. Thompson at Lymm on Saturday. The occasion was a I splendidly-organised and rousing recruiting parade of the Altrincham and Lymm battalions of the Cheshire Volunteer Regiment. After parading at the Drill Hall early in the after- noon, the Lymm men marched to Broomedge, where they ere met by the Altrincham Bat- talion—who were making an attack on Lymm. The local volunteers defended against the attack, and 4ter what seemed a very long time their scouts reported the advance of the attack- ing party. An exciting period followed, the Lymm men capturing the left flank. Their advance then proceeded, but the "enemy's" numbers told. The Lymm platoon—who were in charge of Captain G. Williamson and Ser- geants Lister Farrar and S. D. Moston—were afterwards complimented by Sir Arthur Haworth on their very fine defence at great odds The whole then proceeded round by Booth's Hill, thence to Heatley and back through Oughtrington. By now it was getting towards evening. On arrival at The Cross the parade was held, and the battalion band played several spirited selections in capital style. Many fine patriotic speeches were delivered to the large assembly of civilians. Mr. Alfred Watkin pre- sided; and there were also present Sir Arthur Haworth, Bart., Vice-Chancellor Dudley Stewart-Smith, Captain George Williamson and Lieutenant Thompson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>"WITHOUT SHIRKING."</head>
        <p>"WITHOUT SHIRKING."</p>
        <p>The Chairman stated that he had recently paid a visit to France, and had stayed eight day: in Boulogne. He had seen soldiers brought straight from the battlefield—he had seen sur- geons, nurses and chaplains (including the Rector of the neighbouring parish of Northen- den,. the Rev. F. C. L. Hamilton) at work. All were doing their duty faithfully and well, and he was more than ever convinced that every- body in England—men and women—should also do their duty in these momentous times. Mr. Watkin, having paid a fine tribute to the work of the Volunteer Regiment—with which he was well acquainted—said that he had come to feel they must., every one of them, do their full share of their duty without shirking if they were to be victorious. The very best— the cream of their country—had already fallen, killed or wounded on the battlefield; and every one must come to help in order to be success- ful. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>MOST SINCERE INVITATION.</head>
        <p>MOST SINCERE INVITATION.</p>
        <p>Sir Arthur Haworth—who was unable 6; speak at length owing to the weakness of his voice, after much similar work in different parts—expressed the hope that every man who had no other reason for staying at home would ' join His Majesty's Regular Forces. But if he had a good reason for not doing so, then he (the speaker) gave him a most sincere invitation to join their Cheshire Volunteer Regiment—when he would be able, if the enemy landed on the shores of this country, to defend "our own homes." In the meantime, surely 'every man not engaged on Government work and who could would arm for the war. and hesitate no longer to join the colours! (Hearty applause.) Lieutenant Thompson—who is back wounded from the Front—said that ore of the things which struck him very forcibly whilst out at the Front was that when shells were flying about and bullets whistling round within a few ,Tards, and when Men were ordered to hide in ditches and streams frgainst the excessive forces of the Germans, th 6 brave Lancashire lads could ask: "Is there a war on?" (Laugh- ter.) That was undoubtedly humorous, but it also expressed a tragic feeling to which they could give expression in no other way. It had made them wonder .why the people at home were not backing them up as they should do: hut thank Heaven they were doing it now. "We have," continued the sneaker. "had wars of great tragedy, but compared with this they all pale into insitznificance. In the present day our Army, magnificent as it is, cannot fight the battle alone; because, allied as we are with other nations that have put forth every ounce of their strength, we as a people must fight the allied German and Austrian people. But I believe that in a very few weeks perhaps no longer will there be any tragedy in that cry 'ls there a war on? ' " There had been serious shortages, but he believed the nation had determined that it should not happen again. Then that work which lay to their hands must be done with the best of their ability., They were all indi-viduals of the national strength; and if any man or, woman was not doing,his or her best. then he or she was a traitor to the cause. Fe hoped the name of Lymm might be written in the annals that must be handed down to future generations: that they would use all their power that right; as represented by the Allies, might triumphantly march over the broken body of wickedness and might. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>OAtk to ouA OWN YOUTH."</head>
        <p>OAtk to ouA OWN YOUTH."</p>
        <p>Vice-Chancellor Smith said the present afforded them a chance to slip back to their own youth, and to take their place, if not in the first line, then in the second line of troops. They might be called upon sooner than they knew to take their nlace in the foremost fight- ing line. He thouelt there was more than a possibility of the Germans taking our shores: there was a danger of it. And, however they were all big enough to Qfop a bullet—if they could not stop two! (Hear, hear, and applause.) After Mr. Watkin had cordially thanked Sir Arthur Haworth and the other speakers, the meeting concluded with the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL A.N.6 WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL A.N.6 WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday and Thursday the usual drills were held at the Drill Hall, and on Wednesday the musketry ,instruction at the miniature range, _</p>
        <p>To-day the Lymm platoon fall in at 2.20 at the Drill Hall and march to GFappenhall, there to meet the Warrington men for the purpose of manceuvres.</p>
        <p>Private James Taylor.—On another page we give details of Private James Taylor, of War- rington, being wounded. For 19 years Private Taylor lived at. Lymm-,:working as a labourer for Mr.. Thomason and Mr. Holt. He attended the Congregational Church, and lived in Rush Green and later Statham. Four years ago he .etilli&amp;eo 'Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <p>,p. , 1 ,i. • I e i.: • :12,i</p>
