        <p>f</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>MAN WHO WAS NOT DROWNED.</head>
        <p>MAN WHO WAS NOT DROWNED.</p>
        <p>Eccentric Escapade.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening Ulm ivei'e Many visitor in Lymm.</p>
        <p>As the saying gm, however, "they departed with 0. pt,Wenker whic was left behind." He :was peatefully 'sleeping on the canal bank be- hind the Congregational Church. Also he had, for some unknown reason, hung his hat and coat upon the railings thefe. Time passed on; and in the small hours he awoke. There was darkness over all the land. He commenced a „ramble around the village, until a friendly constable conducted him to the shelter of the Police Station—wondering what might have become of 'the coat and hat, for the absence of which this Runcorn visitor could not account.</p>
        <p>The sequel took place at nine o'clock on Mon- day morning, when a workman passing along the canal bank was horrified to discover hung on the railings a coat and hat. Hastily he ran round by the Council Offices, where he ex- claimed to the first man he met: "There's a man in t' cut!" The news spread; the alarm was raised: it was stated that a man had been in the neighbourhood all night rending the un- canny atmosphere with his "hideous howls." Visions of grapoling-irons did gruesomeness hovered large in the mind of him who, mercury- like, was hastening to inform the constabulary of the startling event. • •</p>
        <p>On arrival A the Police Station those self- same visions were rudely dispelled; but the wanderer—who declared he had been "lost " pricked up his ears. "My hat and coat !" he was heard to exclaim, as he beat a speedy if undignified retreat to the canal, afterwards proceeding stionwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <p>z • :</p>
        <p>WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>DR. THORP MARRIED.</p>
        <p>At St. George's Church, Llandudno, on Mon- day, the marriage took place by special licence of Dr. Leonard Thorp, of Lymm—eldest son of Mr. Henry Thorp, of Lymm and Man- chester—and Miss Dolena Shaw Mackay, youngest daughter of Mr. Kenneth Mackay, of Dingwall, Rosshire. The Rector ,(the Rev. Ll. B. Hughes) officiated.</p>
        <p>Dr. and Mrs. Thorp will be at home at Foot's Crag,, Lymm, Cheshire, October 12th and following days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>DRINK WATER-COTTON.</head>
        <p>DRINK WATER-COTTON.</p>
        <p>At the St. Mary's Church on Saturday took place a pretty wedding between Mr. James Drinkwater, only son of Mr. H. Drinkwater, of Pepper-street, Lymm, and Miss Mabel Cotton, youngest daughter of Mr. John Cotton, Street Farm, Gissingliam, Suffolk.</p>
        <p>The bride, who was given 'away by Mr. W. Challoner, was attired in a cream coat and skirt, with pale blue hat. She carried a bouquet of pale pink carnations. The duties of best man were discharged by Mr. A. W. Allman.</p>
        <p>The Presents. Mrs. Cotton, sewing machine. Mr. H. Drinkwater, dinner service. Mrs. H. Drinkwater, house linen and clock. Lieut.-Col. and Mrs. C. Martin, case of cutlery. Mr. and Mrs. Milnes White, silver pepper pots. Mrs. R. White, linen bedspread. Miss Taylor, teacloth. Mrs. Paxford, pickle jars on stand. Miss Laura Macdonald, case of silver spoons. Miss E. Coats, jam pots and spoons on stand. Master Rupert and Barbara Martin, toast rack., Miss Maud and Sybil Bold, case of silver spoons and tongs. Mr. and Mrs. Minards, satin cushions. Miss Shellibeer, traycloth. Mrs. Thomas, gold wrist watch, cushion, and travelling trunk. Miss Mary White, white cruet. Mrs. H. .Burkett, • linen 'bedspread. Miss E. Cotton, .house linen. Mr. A. Lefley, eiderdown. Mr. and Mrs. F. A. Morris, jam dish on stand. Mr, and Mrs. Margetts, tray. Mr. P. Griffiths, copper kettle. Mr. and Mrs. S. Reynolds, pictures and flower vases. Mr. J. A. Rhodes, fruit dishes. Mr. and Mrs. W. Challoner, tea services and case of spoons. Master E. Challoner, cheese dish. Mr. C.. Normington, teacloth. • Mr. R. E. Elliott, table plant. Mr. G. Willan, house plant. Messrs. A. W. Allman, T. Fletcher, F. Chesters and J. Margetts, case of knives, spoons and forks. Bride to bridegroom, Burbery. Bridegroom to bride, Burbery and book case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>SOLDIERS' CLURS.</head>
        <p>SOLDIERS' CLURS.</p>
        <p>Churchpeople's Considerate Actiot</p>
        <p>For Billeted Visitors,</p>
        <p>For the entertainment of the aoldiers at present billeted in the village, several of the local churches have opened rooms to be used as clubs. The first to be opened was the Wesleyan Schoolroom on Monday evening. On Tuesday evening the Wesleyans also gave the soldiers a social with refreshments. At the concert, among other artistes was Private William Hope, who gave three comic songs with great gusto and success.</p>
        <p>Other songs and items were given in equally good style by: Private A. Frayue ("Silver Bells "), Private A. Cain (" Boys in Khaki," &amp;c.), Private S. Butterworth (" The Rescue "), Private H. Bostock (" The Alphabet"), and Private W. Rostock (" Sweet Adeline"). - Who was the soldier who fell asleep in a corner?</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening the Primitive Metho- dists also opened their school, giving a social and concert of song and recitation. The officer (Lieutenant Milne) gave a fine address of advice to the men on leaving home—in many cases for the first time. He went on to express his deep appreciation of the churchpeoples generosity in opening these rooms, and of the good spirit and kindness with which, they had been met throughout the village. The Congregationalists intend to, follow suit. These rooms, which are used for Writing, read- ing and games—the Primitive Methodists are to instal a billiard table—are patronised a great deal, for of the hundred men billeted so many as eighty have been in attendance. Also, we understand, the Parish Church offi- cials are rising to the oceasion not only in this respect, but also in the matter of facilitating sports—offering the use of their ground for cricket or football.</p>
        <p>This afternoon the billeted "Terriers" will play the Wesleyans at cricket.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>"A DANGEROUS JOB." Private H. Cadman Speaks Of New Mining Work.</head>
        <p>"A DANGEROUS JOB." Private H. Cadman Speaks Of New Mining Work.</p>
        <p>Yesterday Private Henry Cadman, of the 14th Brigade Mining Section, wrote to his sister, Mrs. G. Rixon, of Pepper-street "I have been away from our battalion, and have joined a, mining section. So I am away from the Lymm lads altogether now. It is a- dangerous job; but, you know, someone has got to do it. And we , have so much a day more, We go down work- ing for eight hours at a time, and we get sixteen hours off. We are billeted in a wood just be- hind the firing line—well, you can tell how far we are behind when we can go to our work in five minutes from where we are! Just fancy being under the ground about two or three , hundred yards, and about twenty feet down. The one which I am working in now has been blown up once, because the Germans were work- ing above our men; so our men blew them up. You see, that is the danger working down there and running in the Germans or alongside of them. Because we never knew where they are! The other night we were working hard, and all at once we came to daylight; and I can tell you that there was a rush for the top. But it v-as only an old one which we had run into.</p>
        <p>Thtee Mines Going.</p>
        <p>To Mrs. S. Owen, Brookfield Cottages, Booth's Hill, Private Cadman writes:— "I am not likely to go hack to our battalion now I have joined. a tunnelling company. We are just this side of , mining again. Well, we have got three mines going where we are. . . . We are in a 'big wood, and the Germato shell it everyday. . . I think the last tli-ree months of this war will be the worst, but it will not look long if you do not worry about it. "The other night some of our chaps IN:are carrying some foot-boards up for the trenches, and it was very dark. . . • When they are on carrying parties and it has been raining, you hear some awful talk."</p>
        <p>Lieutenant W Ide.—Lieutenant Wilde, of Leckonby, Station-road, who has just gained his commission, was formerly a sergeant in the Vol- unteer motor company, and also an ex-member or the Cheshire -Volunteer Regiment, Lymm platoon. He belongs to the 76. Manchesters, now gtationed in Lancashire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>LYMIVI TERR ITORIALS.</head>
        <p>LYMIVI TERR ITORIALS.</p>
        <p>NO FULMER CASUALTIES.</p>
        <p>On Thursday night the township "Terriers" again entered the tiring line—for a fortnight. Among the Lymm Territorials at the Front there have been no further casualties reported this -week.</p>
        <p>Two of the Lymm "Terriers" have been transferred to the Mining Section of the ;14th Infantry Brigade. One is Private Henry Cad- man, whose knowledge of practical building will doubtless prove a valuable asset. The other, we understand, is Private jack Clarke, of Statham. Private Walter Hadden, of the Second Divi- sion of the Lymm "Terriers," Writes to his mother at Pepper-street: "I am at Southamp- ton [June 30]. I had only just sent you that letter before we had orders to pack up, and we left Epping at two o'clock this morning I think we are leaving to-night. . . ." Writing to his nephew, Harry Owen, before he was transferred, Private H. Cadman said: "We ;are - now out of the trenches—on our 'rest' 'as .they call it; but,we. have to go out at nights on digging parties, or on carrying parties to the trenches, and it is not a very nice job at times. It is very dangerous, and we are under rifle fire and shells all the time. We might have a night in, and then a night out while we are on our rest. Last night we went out, and had Willie Jackson hit in the leg; he will get home now with it. Fancy being in a trench for six- teen days at a time! You see, a trench is like a cutting of a drain in the road (like you have seen Council men working at); and we have to stop in one of them sixteen days and sometimes longer. . . . The big guns are going at it all day long over our heads." - Private Wallace McHugh, in a letter received by a friend, Mr. F. Leach, at Grappenhall, says: "We were in the trenches sixteen days, and had a pretty rough time. No doubt you will have heard about the loss of two of my com- rades 'from Lymm. We were inspected to-day [Sunday, June 27th] by a certain General, who praised us highly for the good work we have, done. He said the sth Cheshires would be men- tioned in the next despatches. There is a Run- corn lad in C Company who has.won. the D.C.M. for bringing wounded in under heavy fire." Private Joseph Clare sends word to his mother at Booth's Hill: "Thanks very much for the parcel. . . . I had a proper tea for once.; It's always either jam and bread or bread and jam; but all the same that sort of a' meal has been welcomed above once. Still, a. good change of ` chuck ' goes down well. ' Smack ' Booth found us again to-day [June 281, and also told us that he had seen George Appleton. His regiment is occupying the same trenches as we once occupied, but we are a few miles away from them now, and we don't get days off, or else I would go and have a look for him. . . . Our General paid us his last visit yesterday, and he told us eomething to be proud of. No 'Territorial regiment could have better words said to them. We had, he said—although we had not been through what other regiments had gone through—done full justice to ourselves, and also had kept our billets cleaner than other regi- ments did. But we have always had that name wherever we have been. The time will come, he said. when we shall be tried in a battle. So we shall have, to keep our wits about us, for that is a job one has got to keep cool in: And when that time comes I know we shall do it bravely. "We are just having a drop of rain."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>Big Thunderstorm.</head>
        <p>Big Thunderstorm.</p>
        <p>Private Percy Clare, writing on June 30th to his mother, Mrs. G. Davies, says: "We are still in the fields, • but go in the trenches again to- morrow night. Roll on the time when we are coming home! We saw Walter Massey, from Lymm, yesterday. Harold is leaving us now for home, E 0 I will send this letter by him. "We have all been for -a good bath and a change of clothes, so I can tell you we feel a lot better. We had some grand weather a week ago. We have to go up to the trenches at night. We should have gone on the 25th, digging, but we had a big thunderstorm, so they let us stay ill.. . ."</p>
        <p>Corporal Jack Moston's word runs "We are all going on very nicely, and are having lovely weather."-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>SALVATION STEAK.</head>
        <p>SALVATION STEAK.</p>
        <p>Private Henry Brookes writes 'amp to 1, West _ Hyde, a capital , letter of how steak arrived in the .trenches when the " Terriers " were almost stranded. He says:—_ _ . _</p>
        <p>"You may be somewhat surprised to learn that such a luxury as steak can be had in the trenches. Running well into our seventh day in the firing trench, we were practically on our last legs as regards the supply of footstuffs, and I had begun to think that Messrs. Libby and McNeil (bully beef) and Huntley and Palmer (biscuits) would be the next allies to pull us through. I was busy at the time trying to make a meal up with our scanty few small pieces of bread which we had left, when up came Sergeant Clare; and he happened to notice that I was a, little troubled with the arrangements of pulling together something in the way of a tea. He said Well, what's for tea to-day?' I answered: It's all there! Everything we possess is two jars of water, a few pieces of dry bread and a small pot of. Mr. Tickles (jam). 011:' that's the case is it?' Ile said. Yes,' I replied. that is all we have for tea.' And to make things worse we had invited our friend— Corporal Moston as a guest up to our dug-out. Well, we were. well aware of the fact that Cor- poral Moston would be looking forward to a real good spread, and we intended that he should have ah exceptionally pleasant tithe with us.</p>
        <p>" Well, without a moment's hesitation the Sergeant—to my amazement—left me in the midst of my trouble and made his way up the trench, and before I had made the tea he re- turned with a double handful of Good Old Eng- lish steak. Well, his face was wreathed in smiles—and mine too when I saw It! He had evidently been to see some Good Samaritan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>Jealous Germans Smelt Good Fare.</head>
        <p>Jealous Germans Smelt Good Fare.</p>
        <p>"Needless to say, it did not take me long to cook it. Having everything in readiness, I went to fetch our. appointed guest, whom I found busily engaged preparing himself for the occasion. As you may guess, we are not favoured, over here with the fine parlour 2, lir- roundings and fine furniture. In our sorrt?.- what comfortable dui-out our chairs and table were represented in the form of biscuit tins and ammunition . boxes. I poured out the tea. leaving the party to partake of the good fare set before Orem. The conversation was various, and many remarks we passed of the lull that there was in the battle. During this particallr time the Germans had evidently smelt the extraordinary good meal we were partaking (..f, after which we were entertained by a song, sung by Corporal, Moston—entitled "My Little Dug-out In the Trenches." Then we were in- troduced to the old renowned &amp;nil' of W. D. and H. 0. Wills in the' form of a cigarette—which we also enjoyed. This interesting function eventually came to a Close, and we listened to a few words front our notable guest, in which he expressed his sincere wish that we would in the near future give him another invitation! The important duty of holding the line then made a call upon us, as .the Germans at this time were getting restless, but we soon set on our work and brought their rapid fire to a close. I am glad to say that no casualties in this particular instance occurerd, and that up to the present we are all keeping well and fit."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>COUNCILLOR COWP,ER AND THE BELGIANS.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR COWP,ER AND THE BELGIANS.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your issue of the sth of June reported Councillor Cowper as saying, with reference to the Belgians at Beecliwood : "We see the men there drinking and no doubt enjoying themselves." When challenged by M. Hen- drickx to produce his proofs, he quotes a series of newspaper reports referring to_,mis- behaviour of Belgians in London. What have Deechwood Belgians to do with London? Surely if Councillor Cowper can, as he elegantly puts it, "Open his mouth to a worse state," it is his bounden duty as a patriotic Englishman to do so. -Because a few undesirables in London take too much drink, is it fair or just to condemn the whole of the , Belgians who happen to be the guests of this country? . They may just as well retort by saying that because some Englishmen forget the laws of hospi- tality, the English nation is, as a whole, rude and inhospitable.</p>
        <p>REGINALD BARKER.</p>
        <p>Oak Lea, Statham. 30th .fune, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>SYMPAMV APPRECIATED.</head>
        <p>SYMPAMV APPRECIATED.</p>
        <p>\lr. T. J. Ridgwa3,'s Letter To The</p>
        <p>Urban Council.</p>
        <p>At Monday night's faeeting of the Urban Council, the Clerk (Mr. W. Mallard) read the following letter:— . _</p>
        <p>_ " Wildersuwor, Lynini, Cheshire, 4th June, 1915.</p>
        <p>"Dear Mr. Mullard,—l have received your letter with the resolution passed by the Urban District Council of Lymm on the death of my son, Lieutenant J. Althorp Ridgway, from wounds received in action in the Dardanelles.</p>
        <p>"Will. you please convey to the Chair-man and members of the Council' Mrs: Ridgway's and my sincere thanks for their kind. expression of sympathy, and our assurance that the resolu- tion passed by the Council is very much appreciated?</p>
        <p>"Faithfully yours, "'r. J. RIDGWAY."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>'l‘ OUNDED BY SHRAPNEL.</p>
        <p>Private Thomas Goodier Tells Of A _ Thrilling Experience.</p>
        <p>Wounded recently— in tie left arm, Private Thos. Goodier, of the 4th South Lancs. Regt., whose home is with his parents, Mr. and Mrs. Edward Goodier, at 4, Heath-street, writes to say that he is "going On all right." He was shot in the arm, which he states is "doing fine." In a former letter, he • wrote: "There were four of us crushed up by the side of the trench, when a shrapnel shell burst over our heads, killing one and wounding the other</p>
        <p>use of the opportunities for enjoyment., several of them joining in a game of bowls, prizes being awarded to the winners: Some a them also patronised the tennis courts, along with several of the hospital nurses who were with the party. In a far-off-corner of the court many were _seen _enjoying a quiet game of</p>
        <p>croquet; in fact, they; were all thoroughly out for enjoyment and made the best possible use of their opportunities. During the afternoon Mrs. Crossley very kindly gave selections on the .gramophone, and popular airs stFek ag "Ws a long, long way to Tipperary,"., &amp;c.O were sung very heartily by the wounded War- riors. A very hearty send-off was given to the visitors, who cheered the members very., lustily for their generosity in entertainin* them.</p>
