      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>PRIVATE THOS. GOODIER.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE THOS. GOODIER.</p>
        <p>three of us. It got me just below the elbow of the left arm. I must think myself very lucky to get off with what I have done. If it had hit me in the head or zne body it might have killed me outright."</p>
        <p>Two Brothers Also " Doing Their Bit."</p>
        <p>Private Goodier, who joined the Territorials at the outbreak of war, was .previously a moulder in the employ of Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co., Ltd. He is a single man and is a native of the township. As a boy, he at- tended St. Thomas' School. Two brothers of Private Thomas Goodier are in the Army— Joseph, in the Transport Section of the A.S.C., at Aldershot, and Benjamin in the 2nd/4th South Lancashire Regiment. Private Thomas Goodier is a member of the Ancient Order of Druids.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Walking Day.—Keen disappointment was felt by many of the. youngstek when it was de- cided by the various religious bodies in the village that the annual procession should not take place on Saturday, but the usual tea was provided in the various schools, and the dis- appointment was soon forgotten. After tea the children of the Wesleyan 'Sunday School proceeded to the field kindly lent by Mr. W. Bennett, where games, tic., were indulged in until dusk. The children from the Church of England Schools also enjoyed themselves in a similar way in a field lent by Mr. J. Potter, the Risley Methodists having games and sports in a field lent by Mr. A. Worrall. After the heavy showers of the morning the sun shone brilliantly, and though there was a slight shower about 6.30 in the evening, noth- ing further happened to mar the children's pleasure.</p>
        <p>Church Coughing. , If we did not cough in church, the human race would not exist." And the professor twiddling a piece of chalk in his hand, regarded the sophomores with a• quizzical smile. "What I mean'," he continued, "is that the Wounded Soldiers Entertained.—Owing to the cause of coughing is the cause of our survival. kindness and generosity of the members of the Padgate Tennis and Bowling Club, about 40 life preserver than fleetness or strength. That cause is mimicry. Mimicry is a better wounded soldiers from the Whitecross Hospi- tal were entertained to tea on Thursday after- "For by mimicry we manage not to attract noon in the Club Pavilion, which had been attention, and in prehistoric times they who very tastefully decorated for the occasion attracted attention' were hunted down by the with flowers and plants by Mr. Slater, nursery- tribe and roasted for dinner or hunted down by: man, Padgate. The soldiers were conveyed to the saber-tooth tiger and taken- raw. Padgate in a motor 'bus, kindly lent for the "Since those dark days, mimicry has heen purpose by the Warrington Corporation. Mr. instinctive with - us.. Mimicry is what causes Cook and Mr. Tunstall made the necessary us to follow the fashions. It arrangement in son behalf of the Club Commit- England, to c say 'cawn't ' and ' lawf.' tee, the lady . members having charge Of thecauses us, when tea arrangements. After tea the President ierY mimicry. If he laughs, you laugh—mi If you friend yawns, you yawn—th.at's ni (Mr. Duckworth) gave a hearty welcome to the visitors, and spoke in glowing terms of the work they were doing or the honour of and all the rest of the congregation cough. the sup ported by Dr. Bennett, the vice-president. The reason being the same one which saved Oil'. fore' country, and he was very warmly - soldiers, who were con.valescent, made gooa albeit from atamination.~ l again. If he snuffles, you snuffle—mimictl• And if he coughs in church, straightway yyouf</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>Wedding.—On Thursday St. Wilfrid's Church wast the scene of a very interesting wedding, the contracting parties being Mr. William John Lomax, son of Mr. and Mrs. Lomax, Botany, Knutsford-road, Grappenhe,ll, and Miss Beatrice Goulden, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Goulden, Ravensdale, Knutsford-road. The officiating clergyman was. the Rev. H. V. l'igot.' • The bride, who was given away by her father, was attired in ivory satin, and carried white lilies. Miss Annie Elliot (who wore shantung silk) and Miss Goulden (white embroidered voile) were the brides- maids, and wore gold brooches, the gift of the bridegroom. Mr. Walter Wilks (the bride's uncle) acted as best man. • A reception was held:: at Ravensdale, after which the happy pair left for Llandudno for the honeymoon. The bride's travelling dress was a navy blue costume, with cerise hat. There were many presents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>ORFORD.</head>
        <p>ORFORD.</p>
        <p>ONE HUNDRED MEN FOR THE FRONT.</p>
        <p>Old Village School_ Boys_ _Respond</p>
        <p>NO-bly 'fo "The Call."</p>
        <p>About a hundred "old boys" who received their education at the Orford village school have responded to the call for men to fight for King and country, and are now acquitting them- selves with valour either on the battlefield or on the main decks of the great battleships which have been instrumental in saving this country from invasion by a ruthless foe. This fact was brought •by Mr. T. S. Steel, the well- known Warrington solicitor, to the notice • of the large congregation at the recent war service at Orford Parish Church. A Task For. All.</p>
        <p>Some of the hundred, Mr. Steel went on to add, had laid down their lives for their country. All the men, of course, could.not be soldiers and sailors, but there was work that all Of them could do. The highest service, how- ever, that those who were fit could render was to enlist; nay, it was more than a service—it was a duty! The nation had an arduous task before them, and thousands more men would be required. They must not be behind their brothers in their patriotism and desire to stand by their country in her hour of need. To the men who had to stay at home he would saY "Get to work and keep at work!" Let them do their job as well as they could, and if they felt that there was work where they could be. more useful to their country, let them -change&amp; their work and go where they could be more useful. If by a bit of extra work they could release a man for the fighting line, let thent buckle to and feel proud in that fact that by. their efforts one more man had gone to swell the numbers in the fighting line. It was to the shame a the men of this country if they did not do all that was possible to keep the country free from invasion and to uphold the heritage. received from their forefathers. A Word to the Womenfolk..</p>
        <p>To the women he would say that a word from them would encourage many a man who was. in doubt to do his duty. 'They must not keep one back who ought to be at the Front. Let them think of the treatment received by the women of Belgium! Mr. Steel Went on to im- press upon the congregation the desirability of writing to the .men at the Front regularly, and spoke of the joy it must be to the men to receive letters from home;• it made them feel that they were .not forgotten. He advised the visiting of those who were lonely and had lost. relatives in the war, and the preparation of comforts for the soldiers for the coming winter. He also impressed upon them the necessity for economy—of living simply, of burning less gas and coal, of having no debts, of paying ready money, and of not spending money in pleasure, as undoubtedly hard times were in store for them. They must magnify the' spirit of com- radeship. It was their duty to support the soldiers at the Front. They must fill them with the conviction that the whole nation was at their back, and that their " pals " were coming- out in their tens of thousands to fight by their side, or were toiling unremittingly to provide them with all they needed to beat back ana overthrow the well-equipped and vast enemy. "They Help Also Who Only—Save!".</p>
        <p>_ Captain G. Crane, fro in Orford Barracks, also addressed the congregation, observing that a. great many at present were doing their duty. but there were shirkers, and they must be com- pelled to join the ranks of those who were doing their duty.' If compulsory service—, which, he believed, was not far off—were adopted the shirkers would fare badly. It was the duty of them all to save every penny they could to ,help the Chancellor of the Exchequer to carry on the war to a successful issue. He urged upon those present the advisability of stocking their garden so as to be in readiness for the coming winter; as prices would undoubt- edly go up. The war was costing 0,000,000 per day, and it was necessary that they should. save and do all that they possibly could. Captain Crane promised that if the women in the parish would work at making socks. mufflers and shirts for their soldier relatives. he would put them in the way of getting them delivered at the seat of war. They must all do what they possibly could to break the German Emperor, to crumble his army in the dust, and make them realise that this country was fight- ing for a righteous and just cause.</p>
      </div>
