      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>RUNCORN GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Electric Light Suggested For The Children's Home.</p>
        <p>At the fortnightly meeting. of Runcorn Board (I Guardians, held at-the Workhouse, Dutton, Tuesday morning, Under the chairmanship 4.°Lf Mr. C. E. Linaker, jun., it was reported at the Children's Home Committee had con- "ered the ,question of lighting the home with VectricitY, and had decided that the Electrical ngineer to the Warrington Corporation be wi ,ask-ed to. inspect the existing electricity plant th a view to seeing whether it was in good condition, and whether it was sufficient for the Pose of lighting the institution.. The. committee's decision was ,endorsed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>FEWER VAGRANTS.</head>
        <p>FEWER VAGRANTS.</p>
        <p>It was reported that the inmates of the Work- last week numbered 117 as against 130 in "le same period of last year, whilst for the Drevious week the total was 120, as compared with 128 the year before. The .vagrants last Week totalled 71, as against 84, and, for the Week before, 86, as against-123 last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL</p>
        <p>XX omen 'Workers Not lel anted For Farm Work.</p>
        <p>The question of the employment of women us outdoor farm hands during the war, was raised at the monthly meeting of the Runcorn District Council, on Tuesday, by the receipt of a fetter from the lion. J. E. Cross, which stated that a county committee had been formed for Cheshire, with Lord Newton as its chairman, to do what it could in order to make good the shortage of male farm labour consequent upon. 80 many of the young men from the rural dis- tricts having gone to the war. The help of the Rural Council was asked in order to make the St?iherle known. The county committee felt that must be a scarcity. of farm labourers. thateltallY lii the hay and harvest times, and ina6 the r e was no doubt raat women could wav-s- ''''eulselves most useful on farm in many sid and more especially in milking. A con- t erable number of women who were willing 2) undertake farm work or dairy work had regieitered at wona . the Labour Exchanges. Those en were anxious to take the places of men • had enlisted, ,or to release more men for '`eure service. They had only volunteered to replace Iv men during the time of the war, and not With any idea of superseding them per- ilentlY• Some had had previous experience farm work. Others had received. a short Period of training in agricultural schools, and more were being trained. The question of Wages rested between the employers and the "uPloyed, but a minimum of 15s. per week was auggested for outside workers. Mr. C. E. Linaker; sen., failed to se bow the Council could take any action in the matter until it had been established that the farmers in the Runcorn Rural District were really in Want of labour. Isonl the Barracks to Field,—and Back Again. Mr. C. E. Linker, jun., Said that if a farmer had lost a man through recent enlistment in, Say, .the Cheshires, arrangements had been made by which that man could obtain ten days or a fortnight's leave of absence in order that he r'auld give a hand with the harvesting, provided aPnlieation was made to the commanding officer. tie (the speaker) knew farmers who had got heir men back under such conditions. T• he Chairman (Mr. W. Antwis) thoughf that ;was a most sensible arrangement. The diffi- • ItY about female labour was that it was Vthcult and sometimes unsafe for them to go 'work in the open when the weather was -11„"rerY, or bad. ;,Vll the proposition of Mr. C. E. Linaker, jun., ", was decided to reply to the Cheshire corn- to the effect that at present the Council ild not consider that female labour was required the Runcorn district, as no shortage of male auunr was felt.</p>
        <p>THE COUNCIL'S SYMPATHY. vasb T -0 Council, all present standing ill silence, i,:sed a vote of sympathy with Mr. Lister-Kay t 4 loss he has sustained by the recent death t is wife. • t 0.- similar resolution wa passed by the Run- t! l'n Guardians at their meeting held the same ay.</p>
        <p>14A letter was read from the Chairman thank- g, the Council for their expression of sym- '4'll37 with him in the loss of his wife.</p>
        <p>Not Good Form. ken shall I call again with this bill, Mr. rqu„o„ I think, yiyang man, as a concession to the "veutionalitiesyou'd better not come any illor t• e I have returned at least one of your</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>Summer School.</head>
        <p>Summer School.</p>
        <p>The Bangor Summer School, which was at- tended by some of our members last year, is its be held again this summer during the months of July and August, when it is hoped that many of our members will be able to take advantage of the splendid facilities offered. Some of the best tutors and lecturers have been engaged, and no efforts will be spared to make the school even more successful than last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0246" n="art0246">
        <head>Rambles.</head>
        <p>Rambles.</p>
        <p>Last summer ,we had the pleasure, in co- operation with the Adult Schools, of partici- pating in a number of rambles, and visits were made to the following places: Bewsey Old Hall, the Parish. Church, and also the residences of Messrs. F. W. Monks and R. F. Williams at lull Cliffe and Grappenhall respectively. The rambles to the two first named places were extremely useful from the • historical point of view, whilst the visit to the beautiful gardens of Mr. Monks at Hill Cliffe was enjoyed im- mensely by all those who took part. On the- visit to Grappenhall Lodge., we had Mr. Shim. mm with us, who spoke a few words to the students present on the programme of study he had ou.tlined for the class.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0247" n="art0247">
        <head>An Appeal.</head>
        <p>An Appeal.</p>
        <p>We earnestly appeal to all friends of popular and democratic education to do whatever lies in their power to strengthen our branch, and to use their influence to increase our membership and thereby our activities, and also to subscribe as generously as they can to our funds, so that our work will not be unduly hampered by reason of the present crisis, and that when the time comes for the cessation of hostilities we shall go forward with renewed vigour to meet the demand for education which will make for the building up of an enlightened democracy in this beloved land of ours.</p>
        <p>On-behalf of the Council, F. RENNIE and T. PRENDERGAST, Hon. Secretaries. W. H. SHERI3IJRN. Hon. Treasurer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <head>PROTECTING CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>PROTECTING CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>A l'ear's P.C.C. Vtork In AVarrington.</p>
        <p>The annual report •of the Warrington am! District Branch of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children, states that there is a hrinkage in income. This was, perharps, to be expected, in view of the times through which the country i passing; but it is hoped that the lowest mark has been reachW. To the. lady collectors most hearty, thanks are due. Thu, have persevered in un- daunted fashion with their task. At no time is their work easy, and the way they have over- come the added difficulties augurs well for the future.</p>
        <p>The League of Pity makes a really. splendid contribution to the financial success. Indeed, had the League failed, the branch. would have been faced with a deficit. Its energy, therefore, has been doubly valuable. The branch has again enjoyed the co-operation of the Press, police, school and all public officials, and for this help, as well as for that from all other sources, the committee are grateful. The complaints investigated during the year ended March 31, 1915, numbered 223, and were thus classified: Neglect and starvation 201, ex- posure 1, assault and ill-treatment 17, indecent assault 2, other wrong 1. The Society's action in the 221 cases found true was: Warned 198, prosecuted (all con- victed) 15, otherwise dealt with 8. The complaints came from the following sources: 128 were reported by the general public. 26 were reported by the police, 34 were reported by school officials, 27 were reported by other officials, 8 were discovered by the Society's Inspector. The cases found true involved 597 children- -320 boys and 277 girls' (of whom 3 died)—anl there were 295 offenders (161 males and 134 females). 1,468 supervision visits were made by the In- spector. Not recorded above were 15 inquiries made by the Inspector on behalf of other Branches of the Society, 'which always work reciprocally.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
        <head>GIFT RESPIRATORS BANNED.</head>
        <p>GIFT RESPIRATORS BANNED.</p>
        <p>The War Office has issued the following:—</p>
        <p>"With reference to the War Office announce- ment that an improved type of respirator has been 'adopted on the recommendation of an ex- pert committee and supplied .to the troops in France, a general order has been issued to the troops forbidding the use of any other type, and the public are asked to refrain from send- ing respirators to their soldier friends."</p>
        <p>Men would be just wild to pay their taxes if it was against the law.</p>
        <p>0</p>
        <p>S</p>
        <p>- •</p>
      </div>
