      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0251" n="art0251">
        <head>RECRUITS FROM "THE SPIKE."</head>
        <p>RECRUITS FROM "THE SPIKE."</p>
        <p>Enlistment Agent To Visit Warrington Casual Ward.</p>
        <p>GUARDIANS AND AN APPOINTMENT.</p>
        <p>At the fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians on Wednesday morning, the Vice-Chairman (Mr. W. Furness) \vas, on the recommendation of the House Visiting Com- mittee, authorised to pay visits to the tramp wards at the Workhouse "with a view to obtain- ing recruits for the. Army." • The Rev. A. M. Mitchell said it had been sug- gested to him that possibly he might do a little work in connection with obtaining recruits for officerships, and added, amid laughter: "Would any gentleman: on the Board who deems himself eligible for the Officers' Train- ing Corps kindly hand me his name? I shall be most happy to receive names and particulars. I received my appointment as a recruiting ser- geant this morning. The post is quite honor- ary."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0252" n="art0252">
        <head>CONCERNING A CLERKSHIP.</head>
        <p>CONCERNING A CLERKSHIP.</p>
        <p>In accordance with notice of motion previously given, the Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley) form- ally moved that Findley Cecil Dale be appointed junior assistant clerk in the Clerk's :office at a salary after the rate of £26 per annum. Mr.. J. W. Knowles asked whether the situa- tion had been advertised.</p>
        <p>The Chairman replied in the negative. Mr. -lino%Vles : Is the applicant. any relation if any member of the Board?</p>
        <p>The Chairman: I don't think so. Mr. Knowles said his feeling was that the post should be advertised. There were a great many young men in the town who would be glad to get into such an office as that of the Clerk to the Guardians, seeing the opportunities and openings to which it formed the first step. A great many parents would be prepared to pay a premium if only they could get their boys in. As an amendment he moved that the post be advertised "so as to give our local- lads a chance."</p>
        <p>The Chairman said that the youth in ques- tion had already been in the office nearly three years, working without salary. Mr. Knowles: These are grand positions to get into, and if we are to make another appoint- ment let's have a selection. _ _</p>
        <p>The Vice-Chairman thought that the prin- ciple of advertising posts enunciated by Mr. Knowles vas a step in the right direction, but pointed out that the youth in question had, in a sense, served an apprenticeship. For the future, however, it ought to be laid down as a principle that boys e\itering such positions should do so after competitive examination. Mr. Mather urged that the appointment would be Creating a new office at a time when the Local Government Board were calling upon public bodies to be as economical as possible. He seconded the amendment. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell thought that all ap- pointments, or temporary engagements, ought to be .notified to the Board, Whether their ap- proval was necessary or not. Otherwise it might lead to a suspicion that officials were, so to speak, getting into, a public department by a side-door, or a back-door. The Board should try to avoid creating ally suspicion in the public mind. Only three members voted for the amend- ment, there being 13 against. AI P. E. L. Bent was desirous of moving a fur- ther amendment to the effect that the matter of the appointment should be deferred for six months, but the Chairman ruled him out of order.</p>
        <p>Miss Broadbent: I wish the Guardians had a little better idea of the value of time. Person- ally, I think it,is the most valuable thing of all. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: We are spending a great deal of time at this meeting that we might have spent more profitably.</p>
        <p>B.S.P. AND OLD-AGE PENSIONS. Mr. T. Sherratt, the secretary to the local branch of the British Socialist Party, wrote as follows: "At a recent meeting of the National Workers' Committee, correspondence was re- ported in which it was intimated that the Chan- cellor of the Exchequer was not prepared to make special provision for old-age pensioners in distress as a result of the war, but that he was considering the possibility of allowing additions to the pensioners to be made by local Old-age Pension Committees out of local rates or through the Guardians. In view of the baneful and pauperising effect which the carrying out of any such proposal must have upon the recipi- ent, I, on I)ehalf of the members of the..War- rington Branch of the British Socialist Party, have to inform you that any attempt to associ- ate the payment of old-age pensions with the administration .of the Poor Law will be strenu- ously opposed by all- sections of the Socialist and Labour bodies. I therefore call opon you to bring ,this under the notice of the Chancellor of ,the Exchequer at once—(laughter)and to use whatever influence you may possess with a view to ensuring the necessary powers and facilities being granted to the Old-age Pensions Committee, so as to make such grant, as the cir- cumstances demand, to pensioners in distress." No action was taken in the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <head>OFFICIALS WANT WAR BADGE.</head>
        <p>OFFICIALS WANT WAR BADGE.</p>
        <p>Seven of the Workhouse officials appended their signatures to a petition praying,. the Board's," kind consideration with regard to the provision of a uniform, badge, or any other dis- tinction" they might suggest, pointing out that "a military hospital having been opened within the institution, and being more or less employed there," they took it that they were doing a cer- tain amount of Government work. They sin- cerely hoped that. their Application would meet with the Board's "favourable approval." The Board decided that the matter was one for the War Office, and not for them, the Chair- man remarking that they had no authority to issue a badge. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: I think I might now add my name to that petition. (Laughter.) Mr. Knowles: Would there be any objection to adorning the clerical hat with the recruiting .sergeants' three ribbons? The Rev. A. 31. Mitchell: Not the least!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0254" n="art0254">
        <head>SYMPTOMS OF KIDNEY TROUBLE.</head>
        <p>SYMPTOMS OF KIDNEY TROUBLE.</p>
        <p>Are You In Danger Through Ignorance ?</p>
        <p>Nearly all our readers suffer from aches and pains of some kind at one time or another. Aches and pains very often mean kidney troubles. Such symptoms as Constipation, Backache, improper attion of the Bowels, pains in muscles and joints,' nasty taste in the mouth first thing in the morning, swelling of the feet. cloudy urine, a general feeling of weakness and irritability, backache, all these are symptoms of kidney trouble.</p>
        <p>They are caused by the poisonous acid in the blood which the kidneys should remove, but they do not owing to their being in a weak state. To cure Kidney Trouble of any kind, you must liositively get rid of the cause—the poisonops Uric Acid. Remember, to do this, a real remedy must pass through the kidneys .and the bladder, and not through the bowels as most kidney pills do. When you see the urine change to a muddy bluish colour—a feature which _distinguishes De Witt's Pills from all others—you know beyond all doubt that they have put their healing touch on the right spot —the kidneys and the bladder. A wonderful pill—straight to the 'kidneys—that is the reason why De Witt's give such amazingly quick relief every time. A permanent cure follows in almost every ease. _ _</p>
        <p>You should go to your chemist at once for a 2s. 9d. box orDe Witt's. Kidney and Bladder Pills, and prove for yourself that within twenty-four hours these wonderful pills have commenced their healing work. Do not put off, because weak kidneys very often mean Bright's disease in the end. But when you buy the pills be sure you get De Witt's in large blue and gold boxes.</p>
