      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0267" n="art0267">
        <head>After</head>
        <p>After</p>
        <p>WORKERS' EDUCATIONAL ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>An Eventful Two Years.</p>
        <p>The Second annual report of the Warrington -Branch of the Workers' Educational Associa- tion states that there are now eleven affiliated societies:—The Adult Schools, „Warrington, Sankey Bridges, and Bewsey-road; National Union of Clerks, Women's Co-operative Guild, Warrington Field Club, -Women's Suffrage Society, ,Warrington 'Teachers' Association, Labour Exchange Clerks' Association, War- rington Co-operative Society, and the War- rington Guild of 11**Ip. The second year of our work has been a very eventful and anxious one for all. We were anticipating a good- winter's class work and educational campaign in the town, when, in the early dayse August, the war clouds burst over our heads, and all our projects seemed, almost doomed to failure. While our work has, of course, been seriously handicapped, a useful session has been the result, and we hope that the coming yhr will be much more favourable, not only for • our movement, but for every organisation that has for its object the uplifting of humanity to a higher Wane of life and the distribution of knowledge to the workers throughout the civilised world.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
        <head>Tutorial Class.</head>
        <p>Tutorial Class.</p>
        <p>The second year's session of the above class has been of a very trying nature owing to the heavy demands made upon the class by en- listment, overtime, and other inevitable causes.. The class has 'met on 24 evenings and a total of 357 attendances have been wade by 22 students. Eight students are with the Forces, and two have been absents through re- moval frail the town. The subject this ses- sion has been "Economics," and the class has had Mr. A. N. Shimrain, M.A. (Viet.) for its tutor. The written essay work has been fairly satisfactory, about one hundred essays being sent in. Taking into consideration the un- precedented ijemands made upon the students in all classes, the Board of Education relaxed the regulations governing University. Tutorial Classes, and we have the satisfaction of know- ing that the class has again earned the full grant from this source. The subject for next session will be "Modern Social Problems," and we again hope to have 'Mr. Shimmin as our tutor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0269" n="art0269">
        <head>War Class.</head>
        <p>War Class.</p>
        <p>With the co-operation of the Women's Suf- frage Society we held a series of six lectures on the "Geography of the War," by the Misses Hubback,of Lymm, illustrated by maps and card views. The average attendance was about 14, and the class was a success in every respect. Our best thanks are due to these two friends for their generous services so wil- lingly given to our work. Many interesting views of the places spoken about were passed round the class.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0270" n="art0270">
        <head>Evening Schools.</head>
        <p>Evening Schools.</p>
        <p>In connection with th 9 special Adult Schools in various subjects arranged by the Borough Education Committee, a few of our prospective students have been induced to study the ele- mentary subjects in preparation for future tutorial class work, and the' report received on the classes is regarded as being satisfactory. No doubt much more will be done in this way when the country resumes its normal life again— These classes are strongly recom- mended to all intending tutorial students.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0271" n="art0271">
        <head>Public Lectures.</head>
        <p>Public Lectures.</p>
        <p>The series of about -It dozen lectures which we had arranged had unfortunately to be can- celled, but two lectures were given during February in the Co-operative Hall on "War and Wages," by Mr. A. N. Shimmin, the leader ox the tutorial class. Although the attendances were somewhat disappointing, we feel sure that these lectures proved most in- teresting and were -productive of healthy dis- cussion, which ;wide them all the more enjoyable. We hope to arrange a much larger series next winter with the help of the Co-operative Education Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0272" n="art0272">
        <head>In the Dominions.</head>
        <p>In the Dominions.</p>
        <p>Our General Secretary, Mr. A. Mansbridge, visited Australia, South Africa and Canada., and was delighted with the progress being made in these Colonies in the efforts to establish our Association there. Already a very large num- ber. Of classes of all description* are being held and are in a very healthy state.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0273" n="art0273">
        <head>Shakespeare Tercentenary.</head>
        <p>Shakespeare Tercentenary.</p>
        <p>The year 1916 will witness the celebration ot the above event, and although the time is some- what distant, the necessary preliminaries for the worthy celebration of this great event are being arranged in the large towns. Our Association is preparing to take its part in this movement, and our branch will be happy to co-operate with any body or bodies in the dis- trict .to further any prbject in commemoratioir of this notable event</p>
        <p>41‘ (Regd. Trade Mark.) • COFFEE 105ENCE The Coffee Maid, of Synzington's, says: "No Coffee like mine, I only use Symington's." Be sure the water is boiling, HQ ruzxarias I ca. Adlnbort4 arid I,ondats.</p>
      </div>
