      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0282" n="art0282">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Making One's Flesh Creep. While a certain speech was being made in the neighbourhood of Warrington that was calculated to make one's flesh creep, I - was listening to an unusually long public deliver- ance by a strangely alarmist archdeacon miles away from this district. He was extraordin- arily downhearted, and among other things predicted that the Germans would invade this country in force within three weeks of that date. As I left the church I fell in with a party among whom was the indignant wife of a soldier who a day or two before had been reported wounded. She criticised the sermon in these perhaps rather blunt but eminently sane terms: " Yond man ought to have been pulled out of the pulpit long before he fin- ished. Germans coming here, indeed! Why, even if he himself believes that, you would have thought common sense would have pre- vented his saying it. Nobody's a right to talk such stuff, in public. I'in surprised at anybody wanting to do it, or feeling like it; but there certainly should ho some way of stopping such work." t</p>
        <p>By Way of Contrast It is a relief to turn from these considera- tions to a little story recalled by what I read in last week's angling column as to the ex- tent to which the Gowy had been reduced owing to the prolonged drought. Our old friend Mr. Carter Platts narrates that once, while waiting at a wayside railway station in an out-of-the-way -part of Ireland, he noticed that the metals, which were of the flanged variety and bolted direct to the sleepers' were so far worn that the heads of the rails had practically disappeared, and it seemed that in a very short time the trains would be running on the thin neck of the rail. He expressed amazement that trains could be run on such a "scrap-iron, marine stores, kind of a perma- nent way." " Och, begorrah, that's nothing at all, at all, yer 'anner," cheerfully responded the porter. " Last year we ran the Bally- •slutter and Skigberguttery express for three months after there was nothing left av the rails but two streaks av rust." The racon- teur adds that similarly he has heard it stated that nothing will ever stop a really enthusias- tic Yorkshire angler from trout-fishing in summer so long as there is a streak of damp in the .river bed and, the smell of whisky at the other end of it. Of course, from a practical sporting point of view the streak of damp is a trifle exaggerated, and the whisky is entirely uncalled for.</p>
        <p>The Great Evangelical Revival. The preacher on the occasion of Padgate Methodist choir anniversary dealt expressly with the spiritual power of Methodism. It is claimed by representatives of the Wesleyan and daughter churches that the evangelical revival under John Wesley saved England from the horrors of *volution. The late Rev. Hugh Price Hughes, founder and for many years editor of the " Methodist Times," re- peatedly asserted it by voice and pen. A while ago I discovered that the statement was originally made by the ,French historian, M. Taine. In the meantime it has been endorsed and ratified by many sober and competent judges; in short, authorities generally. agree that Methodism exercised potent control over the public mind of England at the crucial period of unexampled disquietude to which allusion is made.</p>
        <p>Catholicity of Hymns. For whatever it may be worth I do not mind recording my own judgment in favour of the fbregoing claim, though I am just as much, or as little, of a sectarian or denominationalist as the average journalist accustomed to inti- mate relations with all sorts and conditions of men. True, the differences in question are nowadays seldom emphasised in any ecclesias- tical quarter. After all, the essential oneness of Christians is easily demonstrable by a dip into almost any church hymnal. • Even the one so extensively used in Anglican churches, Hymns Ancient and Modern," inchides ex- amples not a few by Nonconformist authors; and so of the hymn-books of the Congregation- alists, the Baptists, and the various branches of Methodism. Roman Catholics, Church of England bishops, Unitarians and all shades of Nonconformity are laid under contribution by tho compilers of almost every hymnal used. None of the hymns thus constantly sung by churches of all denominations contain senti- ments that any section can object to, and the fact may fairly be cited in proof of the real unity of churches beyond which the compara- tively unimportant organic .union earnestly desired by some good folk is but as a dream. Last week's reference to the anniversary of tile birth of Mrs. Barbauld set my thou:4las along these lines. Care of the Teeth.</p>
        <p>The quotation under this headline in last week's " Hints on Health" recalled for me the remarkable fact that, contrary to popular supposition, the revelation of the school clinics --taking the country as a whole, I mean—is that the teeth of the little ones dealt with therein are really very good. To take by way of et! ample one with which I happen to be familiar, of 76 five-year-old children examined, not one had an unsavable tooth; indeed, the same was true of 100 others a year older, and of the ildren of seven years old only 12 per cent. of the had teeth were beyond help. So the pro- portion continued with advancing age until the thirteenth year was reached-, at which period 55 per cent. of the permanent carious teeth were reported unsavable. The moral is plain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0283" n="art0283">
        <head>Education Question.</head>
        <p>The Education Question.</p>
        <p>The report of the speeches at Orford Hall prize day brought to my mind some of the faddists and knowing ones I have come across in my wanderings. One of the more reason- 'able of these used to insist that there are more necessary subjects than mathematics and the classical languages, which it is of momen- tous importance that every individual should learn—the art of living. That art ..(he was wont complacently to say) is comprised in a few simple subjects upon all of which the millions are profoundly ignorant. It is a hun- dred times more important (he would dog- matically declare) that the ninety and nine should understand the composition and func- tions of food and clothing, air .and water, than that the. hundredth should become a mathe- matician, a teacher of Greek, or a professor of music, and (he would conclude with an " I'm- not-a-argyin' -w iv-yer-but-I' m-a-tellin-of-yer " air) not until we educate the young upon some such basis can we expectihappy homes, healthy infants, robust Citizens, a low death- rate, and a prosperous nation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0284" n="art0284">
        <head>DREAMS.</head>
        <p>DREAMS.</p>
        <p>Dream, old Man, your pipe in your hand, Dream in the firelight glow; Touch the lips and the forms you loved, Loved in the Long Ago. .</p>
        <p>Come they at twilight, stealthily, slow, Out of the bygone years, Light as the moon-flower's breath their tread Smiling at you through their tears. .</p>
        <p>Phantoms are they, old man, of your youth, Youth with its roses red; Fragrance still clings to their petals torn, Bruised, and withered, and dead.</p>
        <p>Dream, old man, your pipe is out, Dream in the firelight glow; Faces you loved and lips You kissed Are dust of the Long Ago!</p>
        <p>Started Young. She: " What an air of well-bred repose Cholly has." He: " Yes, but Cholly was naturally lazy to begin ,with."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0285" n="art0285">
        <head>"SILENT AND BUSY."</head>
        <p>"SILENT AND BUSY."</p>
        <p>Eloquent Serbian Tribute To</p>
        <p>England.</p>
        <p>AN ISLAND'S LEGIONS.</p>
        <p>The following eloquent tribute to England, moving in its expression of the deep faith which the smaller nationalities have in our country, and inspiring in its incentive to act In the present according to the traditions of a great past, is from the pen of a Serbian writer, M. Lazare Kossoyac, and has been con- tributed to the official Serbian journal, " Samouprava."</p>
        <p>The English people are silent; but it is re- markable that when these northern men begin to speak they are more eloquent than we of the south. More eloquent is Macaulay than Mira- beau, and Carlyle than Renal', and Gladstone than Mazzini. As if the chill northern mists pressed upon the mouth, their thoughts come to the • tongue and go back from the tongue again unexpressed. Englishmen are silent and thoughtful. Never was this island more silent, more thoughtful, or more busy than it is now. The war has given to the whole of Great Britain a solemn aspect. As I stepped upon British soil at Folkestone, I felt as though I had entered Westminster Abbey. All this island is transformed .into an abbey; every man is silent, thoughtful, and busy. _ _ _</p>
        <p>In the?'DardaneUes five great battleships have gone down. In tlFe southern lands such losses would cause countless comments; but they cause Englishmen to become only more silent, thoughtful and busy. In the Atlantic there was committed the " Superman" crime of the Lusitania, which like some vast sarcophagus was laid on the floor of the deep. But in Eng- land, as the news arrives, lips are only pressed more tightly, the brain is more intensely con- centrated, and tasks are more bravely seized. The Zeppelins make daily competition with •their brothers the submarines in the destruc- tion of private property and of unarmed and innocent people. At home poisonous bombs may fall from Airships, while poisonous gases choke the heroic men in the trenches. Day by day appear the long columns of killed and wounded. But tI4 granite island is silent, thoughtful, and busy! Thus it makes answer to all calamity.</p>
        <p>The English have to-day a veritable sea dominion from Pole to Pole. If their duty to the Allies was to free the. waters, they have done that duty brilliantly, To-day a Serbian can embark at Salonika, and travel through Suez to the Antipodes, around the globe and back through Gibraltar to Salonika again., During the whole journey he will travel upon the friendly English green sea-fields. English power ppon the waters was never realised in such measure—and so effectively—as now. Never was there in history upon land such power as the English are exercising now- upon the seas. You will say, "The waters are onlv a highway, nothing more." No, the waters are more than a highway. They represent ion e- tenths of the best strategic positions, which, thanks to these Britons, are now tn curt)asses- sion; but which, without these Britons, had certainty been, in German hands. A free high- way; of the Sea preserves the Allies from starva- tion, makes possible the transport of men ~nd munitions, and transforms What would be °the:- wise widely-scattered parts into a well-knit and inseparable whole. Imagine' if it were not so; imagine it' the Germans had such dominion on the waters! Their battleships would now be at Salonika, Kronstadt, Vladivostok, at Naples, Marseilles, and Odessa, at Jaffa and Bombay. Then, from all these sides would creep the Ger- man hosts; and who knows how many tribes and nations would not now be fighting against us on the Prussian side? It is, indeed, our happiness that these nine-tenths of the best strategic positions the English are holding now and not the Germans.</p>
        <p>English people know all this very • well, but they never talk about it. They keep silent and think about their own merits! But they are not silent in regard to the merits of their Allies. There has not been a single success, not the smallest achievement of Russian, French, or Serbian arms, which has not been -generously praised by these silent Britons. Our Serbian successes at Rudnik and Kosmai have been acknowledged by none so warmly as the English 'People. Merely chronicling their own suc- cesses, they have described in glowing details the successes of their Allies. How beautiful and delicate are the letters of English correspondents from Russia. And as they have been eloquent in praise of the successes of their Allies, so these thoughtful island people 'have been not less eloquent in sympathetic descriptions of the losses and pains of their friends. One could make books of the articles depicting Serbia's sufferings from typhus, devastation, and want.</p>
        <p>. . The Same is true in regard to Belgium. One might think that the English had.no pain and suffering of their own. Oh! yes they have, and 'not a little; but they are silent, and in silence they tend their own wounds :And bear their own .distress. Silently and thoughtfully they have freed all the seas, from the frigid to the torrid zone. But they have not stopped there; they are helping now to free the dry land. Their troops are fighting day and night in Belgium, in Gallipoli, in Mesopotamia, in South Africa. They have made superhuman efforts tci create land forces in as many months as any Continental Power has taken years. We from a distance behold only the glorious sea- power of Great Britain which has just been dis- played to all the world. But we do not see these gigantic internal moral efforts which are not less magnificent than her sea-dominion.</p>
        <p>Marius said, " As soon as I .stamp my foot upon 69 ground, so soon- will arise the legions!" That., however, .was only the idle word of a southern mind. King George has silently put his foot upon the earth, and there has in very truth arisen legion after legion. And they are arising still! In this point Ger- many alone has been self-deceived, and the whole world surprised. All the world regarded the British Isles as the empty wooden horse before the gates of Troy. Albion has shown herself more deft than Ulysses; but she remains very silent, very thoughtful, and very busy. That a land may gain a nimbus of grandeur and beauty, there must appear upon it a great race or a great man. In this point Carlyle was right. Before the English people, this misty and humid island stood low and uncertain as ,if it might sink beneath the waters. But to-day it stands as fast as granite, appears to be firmer than ihe European Continent, and rises higher than the Alps. And upon this lofty rock stands a nation as one man and as if placed by Provi- dence as sentinel to • view with watchful eye every corner of our planet and every movement of nearly two milliard of human beings of all races, all religions, and all States.—" The Times."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0286" n="art0286">
        <head>ODDFELLOWSH IP.</head>
        <p>ODDFELLOWSH IP.</p>
        <p>Merit Jell-el For Bro. Frank AVebster. Monday evening,. the 28th June, will long be remembered by the brethren as an historical landmark in the progress of the " Admiral Nelson" Lodge, Manchester Unity, Indepen- dent. Order ..of Oddfellows.</p>
        <p>P.P.G.M. • Frank • Webster, a brother of whom the members are proud and who has done miich to bring the Lodge to its present strong. position, was the recipient of a Merit Jewel, inscribed "An Appreciation."</p>
        <p>. P.P.G.M. J. T. Perry ,made the presentation, and said it was due to men of the' calibre of Bro. Webster that Oddfellowship enjoyed such an enviable reputation. Bro. Perry also indulged in many reminiscences, and as he was Grand Master of the Warrington District so long ago as 1876, his remarks were extremely interesting. P.P.G.M. P. Emson and P.P.G.M... W. Hol- brook also spoke of the esteem in which Bro. Webster is held by the " Admiral Nelson" Lodge.. On behalf of visiting brethren, P.P.G.M. A. Boyle and P.P.G.M. G. Higham paid a tribute to Bro. Webster.</p>
        <p>Bro. Webster, after. expressing 'appreciation for the spirit which protupted tbe gift, empha- sised the point that sentiment was the essence of true Oddfellowship. Although the pre- sent tendency was towards commercialism, he had no fears that the principles of the Order would hold their own.</p>
        <p>The social side of the proceedings in the shape* of a smoker, with light refreshments, was a success, songs being rendered by various brethren of the Lodge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0287" n="art0287">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0288" n="art0288">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 3, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 3, 1915.</p>
        <p>ip THE DARDANELLES.</p>
        <p>Corporal Frank Kay Breaks A Long Silence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0289" n="art0289">
        <head>COMING VICTORY.</head>
        <p>THE COMING VICTORY.</p>
        <p>. Corporal Frank Kay, a reporter on the staff of " The Examiner," who joined the A.S.C. and went to Egypt, has written us the following letter from, the Dardanelles, giving the follow- ing vivid sketches of the progress we have made and are Making towards the resounding victory foretold by Mr. Churchill:— , Dear Mr. Weston,—After having a rather un- eventful voyage from England, • and 'having worked supply depots at Alexandria, Egypt, for nearly' two months, we have been transferred to a supply depot off the/ of • Our work is to supply the troops with food and with forage for the horses wherever transports are running, but, knowing that war ,items are by far the more sought after, I will not trouble you or " Examiner " refider with details of A.S.C.' work, but will leave such references until a later date.</p>
        <p>We left Egypt abouVa fortnight ago and, as one of the passenger " Tommies " put it, after " sailing on an iron ship, sleeping on an iron deck and living on iron rations." for two days, we landed at .the 2Egan Sea. We sailed from the harbour in the evening and, as we drew nearer the peninsula, we could see the flashes from the big pins as they bombarded the hill a short distance in- land. We arrived off at about three a.m., and slept until about 6.30 on a lighter which was drawn up alongside the pier, which had been built by our enterprising engineers. Just a little way out to sea we could see -part of the which had been sunk a few days before.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire Landing. All along the shore of the Cape—which has been renamed the "Lancashire Landing," in honour of those brave and courageous " Tommies " from the " Cotton County" who, despite long odds against them, effected a land- ing—were piles and piles of stores and ammunition, and these were being moved with great rapidity to the firing line. With three of the other men from our unit I went ashore to see some friends we had met on the boat com- ing over from England. Up the steep cliff path we went, past rows of skilfully made " dug- outs," and out to where our friends ' were stationed, quite close to one of the trenches which had been left by our advancing troops. Bits of abandoned Turkish clothing were lying all around, and here and there were large pieces .of shrapnel, silent symbols of a Most awful battle. From a ridge near by we had a,good view of the valley, and during the, time we were standing there we saw the artillery come galloping along the road, surrounded by a thick cloud of dust, and swing round into position, ready to send their death dealing missiles into the Turkish position. 'Eager to see more; We made our down to to the valley but the firing being fairly slack at the time, we were not rewarded as much as we expected to be. .I.picked up several small pieces of shrapnel which,- needless to say, I shall try and save until I come home. I was very much struck with the orderliness of every- thing. If it were not for the booming of the guns one would have thought that the firing line was miles ahead, so comparatively tidy did everything appear.* Down in the abandoned trenches one .Would find here and There an empty bully-beef tin, the portion nf a letter from home, parts of, equipment and hundreds •of empty cartridge eases; but upon the level the poppies and daisies •swayed gently with the. breeze. and beyond a bit of clothing and a. few shell holes here and, there, there was nothing to distingnieh the land from an ordinary large nnsture field' at home. But annearances are deceptive, for only a few feet below that calm rlnd n'esleeful surface, there were hundreds of men fully " rationed " with - atumuniti,n and nursing rifle 4 tenderly, ready to make the enemy feel their sting at any moment. The Australian Landing.</p>
        <p>From we proceeded by tug along the coast and lay for a short time off , where the Australian troops made such an auspicious landing. Here the firing was more rapid; and, whether purposely or by accident, a few bullets whistled over the boat we were on. A member of the crew informed me that every time they proceeded up the coast • they were given atten- tion by the enemy, so it was more than likely that we were the mark for ostensibly bad 'marksmen, - seeing that neither the boat nor any of the men were hit. Tp land at this particular spot reflects the, greatest of credit and praise on the brave Colonials, for they had to rush up a steep cliff against a heavy tire aml get a hold on the ridge. They did this, and more, for they took the first /ridge with the bayonet, chased the enemy down into the valley, and right over the second ridge. 'lkre the boys from the Land of the Sofithern CrosS are holding their own; and will continue to do so until such time as they are. needed in the rapidly approaching " geeitt move" on this peninsular draught-board. The French are also doing well on the opposite flank, and I don't think we shall have long to wait for a great victory in this area of the great ;4r. The heavy bombardments they are getting from sea aml land will -just about knock all the fight out of the Turks for ever. Indeed, they appear to be getting pretty sick of it all now, for the prisoners I have seen seem very glad to be away from it all and in the hands of human beings, not of unfeeling inanities. •</p>
        <p>First-Hand Story of the Landing. I have had the story of the Lancashire land- ing first-hand, and the day I heard it could not have been better appointed, so well did it fit in with the story and give to it an added significance, for it was on the day that there was a general advance out here.</p>
        <p>' On June 4th I was just about to pass our</p>
        <p>tent when I was arrested by the fallowing re- mark made by one of our boys.: " Well, he died like an Englishman." Looking inside the tent, I saw a sturdy Fusilier surrounded by several members of our unit, who were listening most intently to the story he unfolded of the great lauding. Ile had just related how a friend of his had passed away in his arms and how, despite the fact that he was bleeding to death from an ugly wound, his last words and thoughts were of the suCcess of his comrades. "There they go!" have they got the hill?" "Keep it up, boys!" These were his last words, so the remark which caused me to pause and enter the tent was not by any means uncalled for. With monotonous continuity the big guns boomed and spluttered, far more than we had heard them before, as the warships fired broad- sides .and oUr guns on land, as well as the French " 75's" made. their .presence felt by the enemy, as the, Fusilier, who, by the way, formed part of an escort for Turkish prisoners, proceeded to relate a story which will bring a prOud feeling to every Lancastrian the world over. Along with the Munster and Dublin Fusiliers •. the Lancashire lads upheld the glorious traditions of ,our Empire in a thoroughgoing Lancashire fashion, and every praise is due to them. In plain, simple jerms, the Fusilier told us the story—a story bf glorious heroism, of per- sistent tenacity against great odds, and, above all, a story of victory—the reward of valour. Every man knew that he had to take part in a fierce, hellish battle, but the knowledge only added to his fervour and gave additional strength to Ais arm. Under heavy fire the men were ttilfen as far. as possible in lighters and small boats, and then they had to wade to the shore. . _ _</p>
        <p>"That wading was terrible," exclaimed my informant. "Barbed-wire entanglements were laid well out from the shore, and we had to hack and cut our way through the 'intentioned treacherous mass the best way we could. The men Who were breaking down this barrier were covered- by their comrades as much as possible, but a lot, of our boys were 'potted off' whilst engaged in this arduous task." • I can assure you that it must have been a most arduous task, for I hate seen the barbed- wire since, and it is terrible stuff—thick and strong, with quite a half-inch "needle-pointed" barb on it. _</p>
        <p>"At the point of the bayonet," continued my informant, " we, along with the other landing parties, gained a hold on the foreshore, and soon had the Turks on .the run. Up the cliffs they went, with our boys close on their heels, and we kept them going right down into the valley. Since then the Artillery which has been landed has done •good work, in conjunc- tion with our warships, in an endeavour, which has all promise of being successful, to drive the enemy from the hill just In front of us: and when that is accomplished there is not the slightest doubt but that a great and decisive „advantage will be gained out here." • The Lancashire lads have given, and are still giving, a good account of themselves, so those who sit and wait within sound of the loom and spinning' mill can rest assured that whoever their relative might be who. is with the " Lancashires," he has brought honour to him- self, his home, his County,. and his. country., Personal.</p>
        <p>As for myself, I am getting along all right, and have slipped into a soldier's life more easily than I ever dreamed I should. The bully beef and biscuits go down without much trouble now, and so long as we are anywhere on terra firma. there is a place to sleep. We simply throw down our watenfroof sheets, get the blankets on top of them, fold our tunics for pillows, and pull our great coats over us for warmth, if necessary, and•then "turn in." I am glad say I have become so accustomed to sleeping anywhere that by now I-very soon fall asleep, the gentle art of "rocking to sleep" being quite unnecessary. I am writing this on a few boxes piled up in front of the tent, so if you have much trouble in reading this letter you will no doubt C,NCUSO one, knowing the circumstances under which it is written. The guns are booming away again, but we have got accustomed to that deafening noise, which at first almost made me sick to hear. By the way, on looking round just now I. saw something which gave me the idea to inform you that there will be some good, useful husbands after the war, for there is a long line full of washed clothes a few yards from my " roll-top." That is another thing which I have got accustomed to—having a good washing day at intervals. It would make some of the People at home laugh to see the boys over the pails, rubbing and scrubbing their various articles of underclothing, and then hanging them ..upon the line. Darning socks and mend- ing holes in clothing are two more Of my newly- acquired accomplishments. but I must not go any further on these lines for reasons quite pro- nounced and apparent. -We get a good share of hard work to do, but so long Os we are able we do not mind, having the full knowledge that every little helps. Individually and collectively the men are giving of their best • and, as the chaplain who preached to us on the boat coming across said "If we all do our duty success is bound to Come to the arms of the righteous against those of the unrighteous."</p>
        <p>Well, I hope all at the office are going strong, and thal the paper is progressing as rapidly as it ought to do. By the way, you must not worry over long intervals between letters, for it is not so easy to write here as it is upstairs in the old "Art Gallery." With best wishes to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0290" n="art0290">
        <head>NEW BOOKS.</head>
        <p>NEW BOOKS.</p>
        <p>What is Living and What is Dead of the Philosophy of Hegel. By Benedetto Croce. Translated by Douglas Ainslie. London : Macmillan am! Co., Ltd. 7/6 net. _ _ _</p>
        <p>English students of philosophy who do not read Italian owe a considerable debt to Ur. Douglas Ainslie for introducing to their notice the writings of the great Italian thinker, Bene- detto Croce. He has so large a grasp oi :the modern problems, his acquaintance with philoso- phical literature is so vast, his thought is, in our opinion, likely to have so deep and perma- nent au influence, that it would, indeed, be de- plorable were English readers ,unable to obtain access to his works.. Mr. A.ifislie• has come to our aid and, after translating two out of three of Croce's principal works, now presents us with this study of the Philosophy. of Hegel-. The book is not long, and is.. written in a style • too seldom to be met with in philosophical writings, a style so readable that it will appeal lalmost to the nnphilosophical and those not versed . in philosophical terminology. Signor Croce gives an account of the dialectic, and an historical chapter shows the approxima- tions in earlier philosophers to the theory of the dialectic. Then .follows an exposition of what Signor Croce considers Hegel's fundamental error, the confusion of distincts and apposites. One of the important results of this error is the position assigned by Hegel to Art. "Hegel could not," says Signer Croce,' "as Others did, make the testlietiC activity complementary to the pliilosophical.activity,. solving in its why- the problems that were insoluble to philosophy. Still less could he make it an activity superior to the philosophical. his logical assumption was bound to lend him to the usual solution of the dialectic, in its application to distiiet con- cepts. The artistic aCtivity is distinct fihm the philosophical only through its imperfection, only because it apprehends' the absolute in a sensible and immediate . form, whereas phil- osophy apprehendsk it in the pure medium of ' thought. This means, logically, that art is not at all distinct; and that for Hegel it is prac- tically reduced (whether he like it or not) to a philosophical error, or an illusory philosophy. True at would be philosophy, which addresses</p>
        <p>itselr%ain to the same problem upon which art has worked in vain and attains a perfect solu- tion of it."</p>
        <p>Signor Croce contributes a vigorous criticism of H.egel's philosophy of Nature and philosophy of history; and he shows that their failure of acceptance by the, modern world is due to the initial error which he has exposed and which has vitiated a great part of Hegel's work. Signor Croce speaks highly of the work of English students of Hegel, which he considers of far greater value than the German studies. The publication of this book will contribute .materially to the understanding of itegel's thought, and it should prove a. powerful stimu- lus to the study of liegel's works, which is pre- cisely the effect desired by its author.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0291" n="art0291">
        <head>WAR BUDGET.</head>
        <p>WAR BUDGET.</p>
        <p>As July Ist was " Dominion Day." the cur- rent issue of " The War Budget," dated July Ist, takes on an even more than ordinary in- terest, because ,of the special article about the Canadian soldier, which has been contributed by Fred. A. Talbot, the well-known author on Canadian subjects. The article is called "Johnny Canuck," and is illustrated • by 14 photographs. ,</p>
        <p>As -is -the custom of "The War Budget," every phase and theatre of the war is dealt with, and from Canada, for .the special article, "The War Budget" goes to the Italian front for its cover, which shows in a stirring picture the six gallant Italian soldiers who held the Garibaldi Rock. against a vastly superior number of Austria ns.</p>
        <p>The issue is. full of interesting war pictures from the various theatres of war, and the articles maintain the high standard set by this threepenny war weekly.</p>
        <p>Keeping Their Minds Occupied. " Y,ou didn't really care When you daughter eloped with young Nabob?" , "Of course not." "Then, why (10 your motor car pursue theirs 80 niHes?" "I didn't want to give them leisure to repent and tome back."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0292" n="art0292">
        <head>[ALL Ittams ItEscrevEll</head>
        <p>[ALL Ittams ItEscrevEll</p>
        <p>THE SENSITIVE JAPANESE.</p>
        <p>Writing to you, Japanese ladies will in- variab:y commence their letters by apolo- gising for their rudeness on the last occasion that they met TOII. Apparently they are con- scious of not having treated you with the courtesy that is due to your honourable self, and thus they unconsciously help to maintain the exalted standards of etiquette and gen- tility with which tradition and a, noble an- cestry has downed them. You often receive such letters, too, when actually you are feeling that they have' not onlv welcomed you in their home but have overwhelmed you with politeness and atten- tion. If you are resident in Japan acts of, this nature continue to amaze you until you be- gin to realise the acute scu6itivemiis. of .these people in the land of the cherry-blossom, a delicacy of feeling which has earned for them their reputation of being THE POLITEST PEOPLE ON ARTIL</p>
        <p>A Japanese girl will ask pardon of a shop- keeper for troubling him, and will curtsey to another customer standing by, and beg her to mike her purchase .first, and again bow in response to the curtsey of that lady, who in her turn gently insists' that she .lias much time to -spare and need not take precedence. "Excuse me " is a term that is for ever on their lips, and it is always accompanied by their characteristic grace of manner. Sometimes you think that they are even apologetic for having intruded their pres- ence upon the earth. A similar spirit perme- ates the men. A white man may tell them the grossest of lies • and their reply, accom- panied by a bow and a smile, will always be —" Is that so ?" Japanese never contradict. It is impo:ite. But they will tight you if you insult them, and kill you if you strike them. One may not return them -discourtesy for their politeness. Personal reputation is very dear to these facile-tongued little lions. You must not, for instance, address one of them as "John."</p>
        <p>"What, John ?" he will say indignantly, "I am no Chinaman." •</p>
        <p>Nor speak of him as a "Jap" or "na- tive." "Do you take me for a blackman ?" American jack-tars when their ships are in the Japanese ports often find that insults of- fered to Japanese coolies bring ultimate dis- aster. A batch of them from the cruiser Chatanooga," during an over-indulgent evening in the early part of 1909; unluckily thought fit to -elbow a Rickshanian off 'the footpath in a low quarter of Yokohama. Shortly afterwards the American fleet drew out of the harbour—to return in the autumn. Batches of sailors came ashore, as ,usual, and particularly did some 50 or 60 Japanese men, unobservedly scattered in the . vicinity of the harbour, observe the 'jack tars • from that same " Chatanooga." By-and-by they ' had shadowed their man, and presently lie and his companions, enjoying taemselves in the same low quarter of the settlement, were treated to 'the roughest •ten minutes that they had perhaps ever undergone. They :were nearly beaten to death.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0293" n="art0293">
        <head>STANDARD OF MORALITY</head>
        <p>THE STANDARD OF MORALITY</p>
        <p>Few insults outrage Japanese sensibilities tnore completely- than the - aspersions which are occasionally cast by Europeans upon pub- lic morality. Citizens in Tokyo are proud to answer these comments by recalling incidents such as that which occurred not very long ,ago in one of the old baronial mansions in the interior. A highly situated young retainer had been smitten by the .beauty of one of the daughters of. a branch of the 'household. He wrote to her, and she ignored the commu- nication, as became a Japanese Maid.,A sec- ond letter was similariy disregarded, and, overcome by the fervency of his passion, the young ',nail 'visited the girl's apartments in person and threw himself on his knees before her. She turned from' him, keeping silent, and only when, be again threw 'himself • in front• of her did she appear to recognise his presence. A girl of high station, 'she was carrying at her side the silver-mounted dirk which such maidens are accustomed to wear as a -symbol of sel2-protecting maidenhood. Quickly drawing the dagger from its sheath the girl stabbe4 the youth in the e,%c, kill- ing him instantly. She was arraigned 'shortly afterwards before the private court of the household, which at once exonerated and con- gratulated her, the presiding Baron remark- ing that her behaviour had been simply in accordance With the traditions of Japanese maidenly virtue.</p>
        <p>• What. saves these . extraordinary people from the prudery which- characterises some of the peoples of the .Western world is the ex- treme delicacy of their outlook upon life. It is delicacy unadulterated, for instance, which enables .the sexes to swim together in large numbers,, clothed, say, in those natural gar- ments of purity, .modesty and a smile. This purity of instinct is in. dominance when a Ja- panese housewife deputes a young serving- girl to attend 4pou a male guest when he takes a bath in the house, she herself calmly asiisting Aiim in the transfer of clothing: The children that are bred in this perfectly na- tural atmosphere grow up eventually to share with their parents the conception that for- eign notions of modesty are barbarous and indelicate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0294" n="art0294">
        <head>ASHAMED TO BE UNEMPLOYED.</head>
        <p>ASHAMED TO BE UNEMPLOYED.</p>
        <p>A Japanese family resembles one sensitive organisation composed 'of separately oper- ating parts. The s:ightest pa-rental wish is intorprdted as , a command. Many fsuuadoina puno4su undcr ii, sn!p.›)3oad but, on the other hand, there are aspects of this complexity of nature which put Euro7 pean points of view to shame. - A Japanese man is ashamed, for instance, to be unem.- ployed. Observe the sensitiveness of mind manifested by the writer df the following let- ter, which was written by an hotel manager who had failed in business, and was seeking employment in a European business house:</p>
        <p>•' Dear Sir, "As you know, I am obliged to give up my present business not in the further.. All in the respect I must find opportunity to get one. My wife especially anxious that I should not remain laughed at by the world. You offered me situation, and I proposed you to give me over GO yen as salary, but now I have, I remember, some amount Zlf pension, which I getting annually. I do not want 60 much. I wou:d be satisfied only that the world recognise me that lam working. I will never forget your kindness in my life. While waiting 'the favour of your reply, "1 remain, "Yours very truly," etc</p>
        <p>An this man wanted was that the world should not lautli at him. The Japanese accept next ,to nothing rather than remain unemployed for a long period. Another fea- ture of the delicacy .of their nature: they never make disturbances in a public street. They are very rarely_ participants in a drunk- en brawl anywhere. Drink themselves drunk? Yes, but if it can be so, like .gentlemen. If a man insu b :ted a woman or a he would be ejected from 'the neighbourhood in which he lived. Their behaviour to strangers is cour- tesy personified. Their morality may be ques- tioned, judged by our Western standards: but their decency • never. Even the walls of their houses are sensitive and delicate. You could drive your fist through a who!e row of the paper-panelled partitions which divide their rooms. And the land they live in—it is nothing but a sensitive throbbing organism, which ever and anon quakes and rumbles and Upheaves—to the terror of its wondering lit- tle people, a land of sunshine and flowers, and beautiful streams and soft bracing breezes, an Eden in which a people could scarcely be aught other than sensitive, and delicate, and clean. •</p>
        <p>One Thing Certain. "Your baby cries a great deal." "yes," answered Mr. Bliggins. "It isn't well." "What's the ailment?" "I don't know. The only thing I'm sure of is that„it isn't the sleeping sickness."</p>
        <p>Not Much of a Sailor. Small Boy: "'Did you ever catch any whales?" "No." Small Boy: "Ever shipwrecked?" Sailor: "No." Small Boy: 7 Ever cast on a desert island?" Sailor: "No." Small Boy: "Ever caught by cannibals?" Sailor: "No." Small Boy (disgusted) "Why, you might as well have totaled on land :"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0295" n="art0295">
        <p>a</p>
        <p>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</p>
        <p>Poor Saddles Are To Be Rigorously Avoided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0296" n="art0296">
        <head>CYCLOIVIETER.</head>
        <p>THE CYCLOIVIETER.</p>
        <p>(Queries and correspondence should be addressed to The Hub," c/o The Editor. Replies can- not be sent by post and all queries will be answered in our columns.)</p>
        <p>Probably not one cyclist in 'a hundred who listens to the miles ticking off on his cyclometer realises that the measurement ift after all only an approximate .one, and liable to variation. For all practical purposes- the cyclometer is satisfactory enough, but the underlying prin- ciple is quite interesting. In itself, the cyclometer does not measure the distance, but the number of, revolutions of the front wheel required to traverse a certain length of • the road, the revolutions being transformed into distance by the mechanism which is constructed in accordance with accurate mathematical cal- culation. The makers allow for the difference between 26 and 28 inch wheels, but that does mit ensure perfect accuracy, as a front wheel well inflated has a bigger radius than one where the tyre is soft, and the circumference is conse- quently greater perhaps by several. inches. Though slight in one revolution, the variation would make an .appreciable total when the figures of the cyclometer mount up into thousands, were it' not for the fact that the tyre has not always the same degree of inflation. Of course, the state of the back tyre is of no im- portance, as the two wheels run free of each other, and the cyclometer is only affected by the one to which it is aktually connected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0297" n="art0297">
        <head>WATER FOR CLEANING.</head>
        <p>WATER FOR CLEANING.</p>
        <p>I have often seen it advocated that the best cleaning medium for cycles is water. Unless .this statement is qualified it is certainly incor- rect. Under certain circumstances, however, it can be used successfully. Enamel is impervious to moisture; therefore no quantity of water can hurt it. . Care must be taken, of course, that it does not reach the bearings; but ,is used for the frame alone.. To .guard against •this, it should be used sparingly.. Don't turn the hose pipe on the machine. A damped cloth is. the best thing to employ. The whole of the frame and mud guards Can be rubbed over with, the cloth, and in this way the mud will be removed. As long as the enamel is intact the water cannot do any damage. If any of the enamel has been chipped off, however, no water should be used. In this case the water will,he retained, and this will act upon the metal, In time•the part may become weakened, and when any extra strain is put on the machine, an accident may fesult.• •</p>
        <p>will be a strong odour of burning, vulcanite; owing to the current leaking thrOu;ll the and burning this material. The carenxii should be an easy fit in the holders, 01,(1 be their faces are fitted .or greasy they shan't' trimmed with a *file. The. collecting ring he-1 be cleaned if .a rag soaked in petrol is puller:ter through the hole into which the brush bnd fastens. The brushes should then be replB4o and the machine run at varying speeds. case of a twin the strength of the current C 3, be determined by the distance it will •in,111,1 when the engine is at good speed. The bridg7: should be at in. The sparking at the Pliltln;',ll,. should be noted to see that it is not exceshe With full advance and at high speed 00 machine is in good order if there is very it' sparking. If the armature or cOndeWeroto defective the best plan is to return the ningthiio to the maker so- that the new parts fitted.</p>
        <p>PETROL FIRES. The large number of petrol fires brought 1111°A by collision, leakage, &amp;c. which have oecurr; • lately, naturally directs attention to the vision of suitable appliances for their sPeeter extinction. In any case of burning spirit wal is worse than useless, merely serving to sPrel'e.(r the tire. It should never be employed unld any circumstances. Most garages, pu n blie,':"ch private, have a bin containing sand, usually proves effective, but recent es}(l ments in America seem to indicate that str will soon be replaced by sawdust. A nt of tests were made, and in every ease it reported that the flames were extinguished I, from 25 to 50 seconds, and that with a Terii thin sprinkling of sawdust. - The latter, appears, can be made even more efficaci°n'. mixing with it a small quantity of e bicarbonate, which liberates carbon 411°"01 when heated. While talking about Pe`;,l.;t fires, the importance' of care in handling sPai., on all occasions cannot be over-emphasised, Pal, tieularly In a garage. where *t is never sal" to expose a naked light. Even a slight er plosion may be sufficient to fire a car.</p>
        <p>PROTECTION AGAINST THEFT' Ail No sensible person ever dreams of leavi valuable jewellery lying about for anyone nick up who may feel inclined. But with bulky thing, such as a motor car, the 'Ole practice seems to be to throw all caution to t; winds, and'N trust that nothing will haleo The theft of motor cars is far more con"111;t, than 9ne would suppose, and really if is cre. prising that such cases are not even more I re quent. Cars are becoming more and 4.0 standardised every year: those from the s3P-0 maker can hardly .be distinguished . one the other, and even more obvious features. as brightly coloured bodies, can he alteL-te beyond recognition. Of course, the ideal of affairs would be for no car to be left ".7 A out an occupant, or failing this, the use °l,hp satisfactory device. There are several nu g market, especially in the form of a lock 110011. the change speed lever in the neutral p051t6 although a good stout chain with pBl,l ';vs would serve equally well, although Per'' rather troublesome to manipulate. THE lilll3'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0298" n="art0298">
        <head>SADDLE HINT.</head>
        <p>A SADDLE HINT.</p>
        <p>One acknowledged defect of the cheap cycle is tnat tne manuiacturers always seem to save money by fitting a very inferior saddle. From the point of view of actual reliability of the cycle the action is hardly open to question, as tnere must be economy somewhere, and far better for it to be in the saddle than in the frame. . Unfortunately, however, a poor .saddle, often makes cycling a mild form of torture. It is very little use recommending a particular variety, as individual tastes vary so much, but most cyclists will probably agree with the dic- tum " When you get a good saddle stick to it." I have been riding the same saddle for a matter of eight years or so, and like it better every time. Not the most expensive example of saddle-maker's art would tempt me to change. A year or two ago I sent it off to the makers who fitted a new seat on the old frame in a manner thoroughly satisfactory, and at about one-third the cost of a new one. The same re- juvenated saddle now accompanies a new machine, and when it again shows Signs of wear back it goes to the maker. Even the most per- suasiVe cycle dealer or attractive catalogue will never induce me to purchase a new one. At any rate, not until I am forced to do -so. OVERHAULING THE MAGNETO.</p>
        <p>Comparatively few riders are recommended to undertake the delicate task of overhauling the magneto. In ninety-nine ont of a hundred eases this is best left to the profesitional or to the maker. There are some riders, however, who possess a considerable amount of knowledge re- garding automobile engineering, and this and the following .note are especially for them. When tackling the work it is much better to do so systematically than in a haphazard manner. The contact breaker should first of all be removed and the platinum points examined. They should be clean, and tho platinum. faces should meet squarely with. a distance of about half a millimetre (1/50in.) between, The bell crank lever should work freely and the spring which works it must not become weak. Retini- ing the magneto can be done in the following way: The timing lever should lie placed over in full advance position, and after removing the water-tight cover, the armature should be rotated, until the platinum points just break. The space between the 1)010 51106 and the arma- ture edge should at this point be 'about 1/16m. In the metric system this is about 11 milli- metres.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0299" n="art0299">
