      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0309" n="art0309">
        <head>BIG WAR Ft ND HANDICAP.</head>
        <p>THE BIG WAR Ft ND HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>ORFORD ST. JOHN'S F.C. MEN.</p>
        <p>Closing Games and Prize Distribution To-day. What They Have Done And How They The final games in the successful War Fund Are Faring. Handicap promoted by the Works' Bowling Association, will be played on the Horse and Private John Smith, 21, Norris-street, secre- Jockey green, Winwick-road, this afternoon, tary of the Orford St. John's F.C., the club when the presence of a large crowd of enthusi- that enlisted to a man, who is engaged on asts is anticipated. As a result of the handicap ammunition work at Birmingham, sends us two the sum of £3O has been raised, and it will be letters he has received from hist brothers, and divided in equal portions amongst the Terri- news as to the condition of other members of tonal Equipment Fund, the Liberal Women's his most patriotic team. He was on active War Sewing Class and the Conservative service himself, but was invalided home, and Women's War Sewing Class. when convalescent, put to his present important It is hoped that Sir Arthur Crosfield, Bart., occupation. will be present, together with the Mayor and As regards the Orford St. John's men, he says, Mayoress (Alderman Peter and Mrs. Peacock), they are doing as well as can be expected. He and other well-known local ladies and gentle- adds : men. Mesdames E. C. Fairclough (wife of Private Wm. Stratford makes a strong appeal Colonel Fairclough) and J. D. Fairclough. have for recruits. promised to attend and receive the share of the Driver John Ward is recovering in Maidstone proceeds allotted to the Territorial Equipment Hospital. Fund, whilst • Mrs. Emma C. Osborne will Corporql Luke Atherton is recovering nicely. accept the Liberal Women's share and Mrs. W. Private Thomas Tickle still is in good health, Peter Rylands the Conservative Women's por- and hopes all the other Junior Clubs have ion. followed our example (Orford St. John's). Private Geo. Tomlinson says their Battalion , (South Lancs.) are still doing well, and hopes they will complete another battalion over at Warrington. Driver John Devine, who has been wounded, has recovered, and again ready for action.</p>
        <p>It is expected that the final game will be reached about 5 o'clock.</p>
        <p>WOUNDED IN THE GREAT CHARGE.</p>
        <p>His brother, Private Ernest Snith, wounded in the great Territorial charge, writes to him from the Second Canadian Hospital, B.E. Force:—</p>
        <p>"1 am very glad indeed to know that you are busy with, ammunition, as that is what is needed at present over here. I am feeling ' champion ' now. My leg is a bit stiff, but my wound is healing up Al.</p>
        <p>"I have drop-I-x.(l- in lucky being in hospital, especially here; the nurses are a treat. You could not believe how _nice and cheerful they are. I am in a nice ward and tle hospital is on the coast. Yes, we are now having a gentle- man's life. I can tell you it is quite a treat to be out of the sounding of the guns and in a nice soft bed instead of being in the trenches waiting for the Germans to show a leg. I can knock about a little now, and I along with some other ward mates often go for a walk round the cliffs.</p>
        <p>"Oh, the appetite I have got now—all sorts of courses for supper:"</p>
        <p>REFLECTIONS FROM THE TRENCHES.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Fred Smith, Royal Munster Fusiliers, writes to his brother from the Front ::</p>
        <p>"I am sorry to read of the death of W. Newns and his brother in less than a month's time. It seems rather herd on their parents, but God's will be done. I also see a letter in the paper from Joe Newns, his brother, to his father, telling 'him that his two brothers have one to the One above, the One that loved them. Isn't that a glorious testimony to the comfort- ing power that Jesus gives. Ah, John, if we could only trust God a little more than we do we should see a great difference in this war, for I firmly believe that the Lord is on our side, and also this that if the Kaiser had his own way it would not be for God, therefore, what have we to fear? Why, nothing at all. "Thank you very much for paper, as it is the only paper that links me to the South Lancashire Regiment, and shows me how the other Warrington B'hoys are going on 'in their new sphere of life.</p>
        <p>"Glad you are still busy with_the ammuni-</p>
        <p>tion. The Quicker you send it the sooner the war will end."</p>
        <p>All end with best wishes to their friends in Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0310" n="art0310">
        <head>SOUTH LANCASHIRES AT lIORLEY</head>
        <p>SOUTH LANCASHIRES AT lIORLEY</p>
        <p>Natives Beaten.</p>
        <p>The return match between No. 1 Supply Com- pany of the 2/ 4th South Lancashire Regiment stationed at Horley, Surrey, and the Holley team was played on Thursday with the follow- ing result :-- South Lancashires. Honey. Lce.-Cpl. J. F. Naylor Mr. Woods Sergt. J. Lloyd Pte. W. Shaw' 01 Mr. Landway Coml. B. Naylor.... Mr. Ross Pte. H. Baguley Pte. W. Allen Corpl. B. Wicks Pte. J. Slater Pte. W. H. Thornely.. 21 Mr. Strap Corpl. B. Naylor Mr. Ferriss L.-Cpl. H. Broadhurst Mr. Cox Pte. J. W. Cartwright 20 Mr. West</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0311" n="art0311">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKING MEN'S ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>N.A. Centre.</p>
        <p>A GOOD OPENING.</p>
        <p>The opening match at Hartford on Sunday provided the members with good sport, although the river did not appear in the best condition. Moss and rubbish at the bottom kept anglers busy cleaning their baits,- but this did not mar the match, as results show. The conveyance left the Cooper's Arms at 8 a.m., and a pleasant journey to Hartford was enjoyed. Several mem- bers brought along their wives, the ladies enjoy- ing the outing up to the mark. Secretary Mr. Swarbrick soon had members at their pegs, and a start was made about one o'clock.</p>
        <p>Result: 1, F. Jolley, 21b.; 2, E. Hill, 11b. 12oz Bdr.; 3, H. Moore, 11b. 7oz. Bdr.; 4, A. Stout, llb 3oz. 4dr.; 5, B. Bradbury, 11b. 2oz. Bdr.; 6, J. Preston, 11b. 20z.; 7, G. Hamblett, 11b. 4dr.; 8 J. Green, 12oz. 3dr.; 0, W. Whitfield, 120 z.; 10, F Trigger, lloz. Bdr.; and so on down to 4oz. 3dr., which was the lowest weight. Several (rood bream were weighed in by North. wich members last week-end from the Weaver.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0312" n="art0312">
        <head>ELLESMERE.</head>
        <p>ELLESMERE.</p>
        <p>Two members who visited this fishery last week-end had good sport, taking about 451 b. of fish in two days' angling. Mr. E. Brookes weighed in a bream of 21b. 7oz. Mr. T. Single- ton weighed in a roach of 11b. 10z.,. and a perch of 12oz. Other fish registered ranged from 14oz. down to Boz. The Mere here always provides good sport early in the season before it breaks up, and again at the latter end of September.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0313" n="art0313">
        <head>NORTON – NO POLLUTION.</head>
        <p>NORTON - NO POLLUTION.</p>
        <p>The canal here is the scene of the great match to-day (Saturday), and , when being overlooked on Thursday was in fine condition. One mem- ber took a couple of nice roach in fine condition_ in the first few minutes. I am sorry to say that some unkind person set out the rumour that this length was polluted. When our society is working for a cause that will be beneficial to our wounded soldiers and District Nursing Association, it is unfair to us to set such rumours afloat without having definite informa- lien. However, we wrote the Runcorn secre- tary on the matter, and this is his reply : " Runcorn Anglers' Association, Runcorn, June 30th, 1915. Dear Mr. Stout,—l am awfully sorry that a report of the kind you mention has been circulated With the intention of doing harm to so good An object, but I hope it will not inter- fere with the success of the match. It is quite true that a quantity of fish have died in the canal, but our length has not been affected any more than the Warrington ,portion in this re- spect. We attribute the cause of this to the exceptionally dry spell of weather we have been having lately, combined with the natural losses after spawning. However, we are certain that pollution is not the cause. Our end of the canal is fishing quite as well as it has done other years. I have not seen anyone who has been to Norton yet, but there' is no doubt that there are plenty of fish in the canal, and, as you know, it will improve later in. the season.—l remain, yours, &amp;c., L CLARE."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0314" n="art0314">
        <head>NOTICES.</head>
        <p>NOTICES.</p>
        <p>Club night at Bay liorse Monday next. Mem- bers please note, all must be straight on .the books to take part in the opening match on Sunday week. Trains to Norton for the match, 12.30 and 2 p.m. Trains to Preston Brook for the match, 12.30 and 1.17 p.m. , A conveyance will leave headquarters, Glad- stone Aims, fol. the match at 1.15. A. S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0315" n="art0315">
        <head>JOLLY ANGLERS •</head>
        <p>JOLLY ANGLERS •</p>
        <p>Have A Good Time Within</p>
        <p>Sight Of The Huns.</p>
        <p>Driver G. l'ye, a Northern AngleP; writing from the Front to his uncle, Mr. Joseph Cox, baker. Church-street Warrington, chairman of the Warrington Section of the Northern Anglers' Association, says "I am sorry to hear of your being so unfor- tunate with the fish at Norton, but truet you enjoyed the outing. My chum and I have been more fortunate. We each bought a cheap rod and some tackle in the town last week- end and -have had several nice catches. We had a heavy thunderstorm yesterday, which coloured the dykes nicely. We went out after tea last night and had a couple.of hours at it and landed 101 b. or 121 b. of fish. My chum got a 'fine tench 211 b., and we had four perch all scaling over 11b., so we are having fine times in this snug part of the firm's line. I'll just mention our menus to-day.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0316" n="art0316">
        <head>Breakfast.</head>
        <p>Breakfast.</p>
        <p>" Coes bread and butter (with good luck not damaged). "Fried fish of four varicties—perch, tench, roach and a young pike. "Marmalade.</p>
        <p>D inner. "Fried steak, new potatoes, green peas and oxtail soup. Tea.</p>
        <p>" Cox's bread and butter. "Salmon. "Stewed cherries, of which we are able to buy any amount at 1(1. a capful. "What do you think of that lot for soldiers on active service, and only a quarter of a mile from the Huns?</p>
        <p>"I do the • cooking, for which I get plenty of praise (in soldiers' fashion). We intend making the most of it while we are in this quarter. I don't suppose we shall ever get such another, and if we are to, have a second winter of it we must build ourselves up."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0317" n="art0317">
