      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0318" n="art0318">
        <head>RIFLE SHOOTING.</head>
        <p>RIFLE SHOOTING.</p>
        <p>A match between teams representing Fletcher, Russell's and the Cheshire Volunteer Regiment (Stockton Heath Platoon) took place on Friday evening last at the range of the former. Ten rounds at 50 yards. Result</p>
        <p>Fletcher, Russell.—J. Phillips 97, G. Legge 95. D. Fleming 94, A. Dennett 94, J. Quarmby 90, J. Towers 89, H. Whiteley 89, G. MePhie 89, J. Colley 87, J. 'Nicholson 87, W. Bankg 86, Mr. T. W. Fletcher 73, E. Daintith 83, A. Mason 83, J. Buchanan 82, D. Richardson 81, W. Graham 77, H. 0. Bennett 71, J. Bradshaw 55- 1,602.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Volunteer Regiment (Stockton Heath Platoon).—A. Shepherd 97, J. Ferguson 87, J. Gamble (sub.) 87, E. L. Robinson 86, R. M. Swinton 86, Mr. W. Russell 84, E. R. Row- son 84, T. Fletcher 83, J. McDermid 81, E. Brad- shaw 80, F. Warburton 79, J. H. Fletcher 78, W. Leach (sub.) 78, H. B. Houghton 77, J. Ben- nett 76, H. Stirrup 73, J. Corlett (sub.) 73, J. Cox 71, R. Turner (sub.) 68-1.528. Win for Fletcher, Russell's, 74.</p>
        <p>MONKS, HALL &amp; CO. v. RYLANDS' BROS.</p>
        <p>on the Rylands's range. Scores : Monks, Hal I.—H. Westmoreland 98, C. Shepley 97, W. Kellie 93, A. Highley 91, W. Hutchison 90, G. Copeland 87, G. Cox 87, Wm. Houghton 86, A. Lester 84, A. Webb 82, J. Sisson 60-955—less J. Sisson 60-895.</p>
        <p>Rylands's.—H. Gaunt 90, R. Maskell 88, John Bramhall 87, J. Critehley 87, W. Newton 84, Joe Bramhall 81, Hosler 87, S. Williams 78. Paskin 78, S. Garnett 76, E. Balmer 69-896— less E. Balmer 69-827. Monks, Hall won by 68 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0319" n="art0319">
        <head>[ALL MGIITS RESERVED. j</head>
        <p>[ALL MGIITS RESERVED. j</p>
        <p>THE LIGHTER SIDE OF CRICKET. ,</p>
        <p>Although cricket is perhaps not wit•ty in itbell, there is nevertheless a great deal of un to be got out of it. Most of it comes of course from the crowd, and one has only to 'walk round the ring at a first class court match to hear a number of remarks full of caustic and personal humour, as for iustance, when a particularly stout player was hit in the stomach by the ball an enthusiast imme- diately shouted to the batsman, "Don't play 'qlll agin the wind. Play 'em the other way." But it is when cricket is dull that the spectators get their chance and as a rule they make the most of it. Every single is cheered as though it were a sixer, and in between whiles snatches of the "Dead March" will be whistled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0320" n="art0320">
        <head>CRICKET "DOWN lINEIER."</head>
        <p>CRICKET "DOWN lINEIER."</p>
        <p>An Australian crowd is especially free and outspoken, and is not at all particular whe- ther anybody is offended or not. In a %big game 'down under" Alec Bannerman was batting, and the Enfflish fieldsmen clustered all round him, "W. G." edging as near as possible in the hope of being able to get a snap catch. Suddenly a wag shouted from the stand: "Look out, Alec, W. G.'s after 3Ter ipocket " A. C. MacLaren, although a great favourite with the Colonials has come in for his share of chaff, and a funny story is told of how they tried to bribe him. It was in a Test Match and MacLaren was fieldiitg. A. tremendous skyer was sent up, and as he waited patiently for the ball a,. voice called out appealingly: " Please miss it, Archie. Then I'll let you kiss my sister !" That catch was not missed ! A commoh trick, and one that • disconcerts the .fielder is 17r someone to yell out "Now !" just as the ball reaches his hands, and when it is dropped as it often is in such cases the crowd shows its glee by shouting "Butter fingers." Talking of catches W. G. Grace tells a very amusing story at the expense of .Mr. E. H. D. Sewell. London County .were playing Warwfek at Coventry and, Sewell missed three catches at short slip which in nine cases out of ten he would have held. "Seeing his great disgust,'; said, the Doctor, "I shouted 'Cheer up, you'll catch the next.'" Soon afterwards a fast ball was sent down whieh the batsman just touched. It went straight to Sewell, who was unprepared for it, and hit him in the chest ! Next day when the team were on their way back to London what should they see on a bookstall, but 'a 1 magazine containing an article on "The Art I of Catching," by E. H. D. Sewell. It was tiii. animously resolved by his comrades that Se- well be asked to contribute a second article on "How to Drop 'Em."-_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0321" n="art0321">
        <head>TIIE ANNUAL HANDICAP.</head>
