      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0338" n="art0338">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Making One's Flesh Creep. While a certain speech was being made in the neighbourhood of Warrington that was ' calculated to make one's flesh creep, I was listening to an unusually long public deliver- ance by a strangely alarmist archdeacon miles away from this district. He was extraordin- arily downhearted, and among other things predicted that the Germans would invade this country in force within three weeks of that date. As I left the church I fell in with a party among whom was the indignant wife of a soldier who a day or two before had been reported wounded. She criticised the sermon in these perhaps rather blunt but eminently sane terms: " Yond man ought to have been pulled out of the pulpit long before he fin- ished. Germans coming here, indeed! Why, even if he himself believes that, you would have thought common sense would have pre- vented his saying it. Nobody's a right to talk such. stuff, in public. I'in surprised at anybody wanting to do it, or feeling like it; but there certainly should be some way of stopping such work."</p>
        <p>By Way of Contrast It is a relief to turn from these considera- tions to a little story recalled by what I read in last week's angling column as to the ex- tent to which the Gowy had been reduced owing to the prolonged drought. Our old friend Mr. Carter Platis narrates thab once, while waiting at a wayside railway station in an out-of-the-way -part of Ireland, he noticed that the metals, which were of the flanged variety and bolted direct to the sleepers were so far worn that the heads of the rails had practically disappeared, and it seemed thabrin a very short time the trains would be running on the thin neck of the rail. He expressed amazement that trains could be run on such a "scrap-iron, marine stores, kind of a perma- nent way." " Och, begorrah, that's nothing at all, at all, yer 'anner," cheerfully responded the porter. " Last year we ran the Bally- •slutter and Skigberguttery express for three months after there was nothing left av the rails but two streaks av rust." The racon- teur adds that similarly he has heard it stated that nothing will ever stop a really entfiusias- tic Yorkshire angler from trout-fishing in summer so long as there is a streak of damp in the .river bed and, the smell of whisky at the other end of it. Of course, from a practical sporting point of view the streak of damp is a trifle exaggerated, and the whisky is entirely uncalled for.</p>
        <p>The Great Evangelical Revival. The preacher on the occasion of Padgate Methodist choir anniversary dealt expressly with the spiritual power of Methodism. It is claimed by representatives of the Wesleyan and daughter churches that the evangelical revival under John Wesley saved England from the horrors of *volution. The late Rev. Hugh Price Hughes, founder and for many years editor of the " Methodist Times," re- peatedly asserted it by voice and pen. A while ago I discovered that the statement was originally made by the French historian, M. Taine. In the meantime it has been endorsed and ratified by ninny sober and competent judges; in short, authorities generally. agree that Methodism exercised potent control over the public mind of England At the crucial period of unexampled disquietude to which allusion is made.</p>
        <p>Catholicity of Hymns. For whatever it may be worth I do not mind recording my own judgment in favour of the foregoing claim, though 'I am just as much, or as little, of a sectarian or denominationalist as the average journalist accustomed to inti- mate relations with all sorts and conditions of men. True, the differences in question are nowadays seldom emphasised in any ecclesias- tical quarter. After all, the essential oneness of Christians is easily demonstrable by a dip into almost any church hymnal.. Even the one so extensively used in Anglican churches, "Hymns Ancient and Modern," inchides ex- amples not a few by Nonconformist authors; and so of the hymn-books of the Congregation- alists, the Baptists, and the various branches of Methodism. Roman Catholics, Church of England bishops, Unitarians, and all shades of Nonconformity are laid under contribution by tho compilers of almost every hymnal used. None of the hymns thus constantly sung by churches of all denominations contain senti- ments that any section can object to, and the fact may fairly be cited in proof of the real unity of churches beyond which the compara- tively unimportant organic .union earnestly desired by some good folk is but as a dream. Last week's reference to the anniversary of the birth of Mrs. Barbauld set my thou4nts along these lines. Care of the Teeth.</p>
        <p>The quotation under this headline in last week's "Hints on Health" recalled for me the remarkable fact that, contrary to popular supposition, the revelation of the school clinics --taking the country as a whole, I mean--is that the teeth of the little ones dealt with therein are .really very good. To take by way of exl! ample one with which I happen to be familiar, of 76 five-year-old children examined, not one had an unsavable tooth; indeed, the same was true of 100 others a year older, and of the children of seven years old only 12 per cent. of the bad teeth were beyond help. So the pro- portion continued with advancing age until the thirteenth year was reached., at which period 55 per cent. of the permanent carious teeth were reported unsavable. The moral is plain. The Education Question.</p>
        <p>The report of the speeches at Orford Hall prize day brought to my mind some of the faddists and knowing ones I have come across in my wanderings. One of the more reason- 'able of these used to insist that there are more necessary subjects than mathematics and tho classical languages, which it is of momen- tous importance that every individual should learn----the art of living. That art ,(he was wont complacently to say) is comprised in a few simple subjects upon all of which the millions are profoundly ignorant. It is a hun- dred times more important (he would dog- matically declare) that the ninety and nine should understand the composition and func- tions of food and clothing, air .and water, than that the. hundredth should become a mathe- matician, a teacher of Greek, or a professor of music, and (he would conclude with an " I'm= not-a-argyin' -wiv-yer-but-I' m-a-tellin-of-yer air) not until we educate the young upon some such basis an we expectihappy homes, healthy infants, robust Citizens, a low death- rate, and a prosperous nation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0339" n="art0339">
        <head>DREAMS.</head>
        <p>DREAMS.</p>
        <p>Dream, old Man, your pipe in your hand, Dream in the firelight glow; Touch the lips and the forms you loved, Loved in the Long Ago. • Come they at twilight, stealthily, slow, Out of the bygone years, Light as the moon-flower's breath their tread, Smiling at you through their tears. Phantoms are they, old man, of your youth, Youth with its roses- red: Fragrance still clings to their petals torn, Bruised, and withered, and dead.</p>
        <p>f. Dream, old man, your pipe is out, Dream in the firelight glow; Faces you loved and lips you kissed Are dust of the Long Ago!</p>
        <p>Started Young. . She: "What an air of well-bred repose r, I Cholly has." L I He: "Yes, but Cholly was naturally lazy to begin ,with." .</p>
      </div>
