      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0346" n="art0346">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 3, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 3, 1915.</p>
        <p>IJ'l THE DARDANELLES.</p>
        <p>Corporal Frank Kay Breaks A Long Silence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0347" n="art0347">
        <head>COMING VICTORY.</head>
        <p>THE COMING VICTORY.</p>
        <p>. Corporal Frank Kay, a reporter on the staff of "The Examiner," who joined the A.S.C. and went to Egypt, has written us the following letter from, the Dardanelles, giving the follow- ing vivid sketches of the progress we have made and are Making towards the resounding victory foretold by Mr. Churchill:— Dear Mr. Weston,—After having a rather un- eventful voyage from England, - and having worked supply depots at Alexandria, Egypt, for nearly' two months, we have been transferred to a supply depot off the/ of . Our work is to supply the troops with food and with forage for the horses wherever transports are running, but, knowing that war ,items are by far the more sought after, I will not trouble you or " Examiner " reddeni. with details of A.S.C. work, but will leave such references until a later date.</p>
        <p>We left Egypt abouVa fortnight ago and, as one of the passenger- " Tommies " put it, after " sailing on an iron ship, sleeping on an iron deck and living on iron rations " for two days, we landed at ,. a beautiful island in .the /Egan Sea. We sailed from the harbour in the evening and, as we drew nearer the peninsula, we could see the flashes from the big guns as they bombarded the hill a short distance in- land. We arrived off and slept until about 6.30 on a lighter which was drawn up alongside the pier, which had been built by our enterprising engineers. Just a little way out to sea we could see part of the which had been sunk a few days before.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire Landing. All along the shore of the Cape—which has been renamed the "Lancashire Landing," in honour of those brave and courageous " Tommies " from the "Cotton County" who, despite long odds against them, effected a land- ing—were piles and piles of stores and ammunition, and these were being moved with great rapidity to the firing line. With three of the other men from our unit I went ashore' to see some friends we had met on the boat com- ing over from England. Up the steep diff path we went, past rows of skilfully made " dug- outs," and out to where our friends were stationed, quite close to one of' the trenches which had been left by our advancing troops. Bits of abandoned Turkish clothing were lying all around, and here and there were large pieces .of shrapnel, silent symbols of a Most awful battle. From a ridge near by we bad a.good view of the valley,- and during the, time we were standing there we saw the artillery come galloping along the road, surrounded by a thick cloud of (lust, and swing round into position, ready to send their death dealing missiles into the' Turkish position. Eager to see more, we made our way down to the valley but the firing being fairly slack at the time, we were not rewarded as much as we expected to he. I- picked up several small pieces of shrapnel which, • needless to say, I shall try and save until I -come home. I was very much struck with the Orderliness of every- thing. If it were not for the, booming of the guns one would have thought that the firing line was miles ahead, so comparatively tidy did everything appear., Down in the abandoned trenches one 'Would find here and There an empty bully-beef tin, the portion of a letter from home, parts of, equipment and hundreds •of empty cartridge eases; but upon the level the poppies and daisies .swayed gently with the• breeze. and beyond a hit of clothing and a few shell boles here and, there, there was nothing to distingnich the land from an ordinary large ”nsture field- at home. .But apnparqnces are deeeptive for only a few feet below that "aim and TTeaeeful surface: there were hundreds of men fully " rationPd " with atnmuniti,n nursing rifle 4 tenderly, ready to make the enemy feel their sting at any •moment. The Australian Landing.</p>
        <p>From we proceeded by tug along the coast and lay for a short time off , where the Australian troops made such an auspicious landing. here the firing was more rapid; and, whether purposely or by accident, a few bullets whistled over the boat we were on. A member of the crew informed me that every time they proceeded up the coast • they were given atten- tion by the enemy, so it was more than likely that we were the mark for ostensibly bad 'marksmen, seeing that neither the boat nor any of the men were hit. Tp land at this particular spot reflects the, greatest of credit and praise on the brave Colonials, for they had to rush up a steep cliff against a heavy fire and get a hold on the ridge. They did this, and more, for they took the first /ridge with the bayonet, chased the enemy drown into the valley, and right over the second ridge. 'Here, the boys from the Land of the Sofithern Cross are holding their own', and will continue to do so until such time as they are. needed in the rapidly approaching "geoid move" on this peninsular draught-board. The French are also doing well on the opposite flank, and I don't think we shall have long to wait for a great victory in this area of the great war. The heavy bombardments they are getting from sea and land will just about knock all the fight out of the Turks for ever. Indeed. they appear to be getting pretty sick of it all now, for the prisoners I have seen Seem very glad to be away from it all and in the bands of human beings, not of Unfeeling maniacs. First-Hand Story of the Landing. .</p>
        <p>I have had the story of the Lancashire land- ing first-hand, and the day I heard it could not have been better appointed, 60 well did it fit in with the story and (rive to it an added significance, for it was on the day that there was a general_ advance out here,</p>
        <p>' On June 4th I was just about to pass our</p>
        <p>tent when I was arrested by the fallowing re- mark made by one of our boys.: "Well, he died like- an Englishman." Looking inside the tent, I saw a sturdy Fusilier surrounded by several members of our unit, who were listening most intently to the story he unfolded of the great lauding. Ile had just related how a friend of his had passed away in his arms and how, despite the fact that he Was bleeding to death from an ugly wound, his last wards and thoughts were of the success of his comrades. "There they go!" "Have they got the hill?" _</p>
        <p>„, "Keep it up, boys!” These were his last words, so the remark which caused me to pause and enter the tent was not by any means uncalled for. With monotonous continuity the big guns boomed and spluttered, far more than we had heard them before, as the warships fired broad- sides ;and our guns on land, as well as the French " 75's " made. their .presence felt by the enemy, as the Fusilier,. who, by the way, formed part of an es,ort for Turkish prisoners, proceeded to relate a story which will bring prod feeling to every Lancastrian the world over. Along with the Munster and Dublin Fusiliers •. the Lancashire lads unheld the glorious traditions of ,our Empire in a thoroughgoing Lancashire fashion, and every praise is due to them. In plain, simple terms, the Fusilier told us the -story—a story of glorious heroism, of per- sistent tenacity against great odds, and, above all, a story of victory—the reward of valour. Every man knew that he had to take part in a fierce, hellish battle, but the knowledge only added to his fervour and gave additional strength to 41is arm. -Under heavy fire the men were ttillen as far as possible in lighters and small boats, and then they had to wade to the shore. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>" That wading was terrible," exclaimed my informant. " Barbed-wire entanglements were laid well out from the shore, and we had to hack and cut our way through the Intentioned treacherous mass the best way we could. The men who were breaking down this barrier were covered by their comrades as much as possible, but a lot of our boys were potted off' whilst engaged in this arduous task." I can assure you that it must have been a most arduous task, for I hate seen the barbed- wire since, and it is terrible stuff—thick and strong, with quite a half-inch "needle-pointed" barb on it. _</p>
        <p>"At the point of the bayonet," continued my informant, " we, along with the other landing parties, gained a hold on the foreshore, and soon had the Turks on the run. Up the cliffs they went, with our boys close on their heels, and we kept them going right down into the valley. since then the Artillery which has been landed has done •good work, in conjunc- tion with our warships, in an endeavour, which has all promise of being successful, to drive the •enemy from the bill just in front of us: and when that is accomplished there is not the slightest doubt but that a great and decisive „advantage will be gained out here." The Lancashire lads have given, and are still giving, a good account of themselves, so thoso who sit and wait within sound of the loom and spinning' mill can rest assured that whoever their relative . might be who- is with the "Lancashires," he has brought honour to him- self, his home, his County, and his. country., • Personal. _ _</p>
        <p>- As for myself, lam getting along all right, and have slipped into a soldier's lite more easily than 1 ever dreamed 1 should. The bully beef and biscuits go down without much trouble now, and so long as we are anywhere on terra firma there is a place to sleep. We simply throw down or waterifroof sheets, get the blankets on top of them, fold our tunics for pillows, and pull our great coats over us for warmth, if necessary, and then "turn in." I am glad ttie say I have become so accustomed to sleeping anywhere that by now I'very soon fall asleep, the gentle art of " rocking to sleep" being quite unnecessary. I am writing this on a few boxes piled up in front of the tent, so if you have much trouble in reading this letter you will no doubt excuse one, knowing the circumstances under which it is written. The guns are booming away again, but we have. got accustomed to that deafening noise, which at first almost made me sick to hear. By the way, on looking round just now I saw something which gave me the idea to inform you that there will he some good, useful husbands after the war, for there is a long line full of washed clothes a few yards from my " roll-top." That is another thing which I have got accustomed to—having a good washing day at intervals. It would make some of the people at home laugh to see the boys over the pails, rubbing and scrubbing their various articles of underclothing, ana then hanging them .upon the line. Darning socks and mend- ing holes in clothing are two more of my newly- acquired accomplishments. -but I must not go any further on these lines for reasons quite pro- nounced and apparent. We get a good share of hard work to do, but so long as we are able we do not mind, having the full knowledge that every little helps. Individually and collectively the men are giving of their best and, as the chaplain who preached to us on the boat coming across said "If we all (Jo our duty success is bound to Come to the arms of the righteous against those of the unrighteous."</p>
        <p>Well, I hope all at the office are going strong, and that the paper is progressing as rapidly as it ought to do. By the way, you must not worry over long intervals between letters, for it is not so easy to write here as it is upstairs in the old "Art Gallery." With best wishes to all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0348" n="art0348">
        <head>NEW BOOKS.</head>
        <p>NEW BOOKS.</p>
        <p>What is Living and What is Dead of the Philosophy of Hegel. By Benedetto Croce. Translated by Douglas Ainslie. London : Macmillan and Co., Ltd. 7/6 net.</p>
        <p>, English students of • philosophy who do not read Italian owe a considerable debt to Mr. Douglas Ainslie for introducing to their notice the writings of the great Italian thinker, Bene- detto Croce. He has so • large a grasp oi :the modern problems, his acquaintance with philoso- phical literature is so .vast, his thought is, in our opinion, likely to have so deep and perma- nent an influence, that it would, indeed, be de- plorable were English readers ,unable to obtain access to his works Mr. Airslie" has come to our aid and, after translating two out of three of Croce's principal works, now presents us with this study of the Philosophy. of Hegel.. The book is not long, and is written in a style too seldom to be wet with in philosophical writings, a style so readable that it will appeal almost. to the unphilosophical and those not versed . in Philosophical terminology. Signor Croce gives an account of the dialectic, and an historical chapter shows the approxima- tions in earlier philosophers to the theory of the dialectic. Then follows an exposition of what Signor Croce considers Hegel's fundamental error, the confusion of distinets and apposites. One of the important results of this error is the position assigned by Hegel to Art. "Hegel could not," says Signor Croce,' "as Others did, make the aesthetic activity complementary to the philosophical.activity,. solving in its why the problems that were insoluble. to philosophy. Still less could he Make it an activity superior to the philosophical. his logical assumption was bound to lead him to the usual solution of the dialectic, in its application to distivt con- cepts. The artistic activity is distinct frlm the philosophical only through its imperfection, only because it apprehends the absolute in a sensible and immediate form, whereas phil- osophy apprehends it in the pure medium of thought. This means, logically, that art is not at all distinct; and that for Hegel it is prac- tically reduced (whether he like it or not) to a philosophical error, or an illusory philosophy. True art would be philosophy, which addresses</p>
        <p>itself gain to the same problem upon which art has worked in vain and attains a perfect solu- tion of it."</p>
        <p>Signor Croce contributes a vigorous criticism of Hegel's philosophy of Nature and philosophy of history; and he shows that their failure of acceptance by .the. modern world is due to the initial error which he has exposed and 'which has vitiated a great part of 'Hegel's work. Signor Croce speaks highly of the work of English students of Hegel, which he considers of far greater value than the German studies. The publication of this book will contribute .materially to the miderstanding of ilegers thought, and it should prove a. powerful stimu- lus to the study Of Hegel's works, which is pre- cisely the effect desired by its author.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0349" n="art0349">
        <head>WAR BUDGET:</head>
        <p>THE WAR BUDGET:</p>
        <p>As 'July, Ist was " Dominion Day." the cur- rent issue of " The War Budget," dated July Ist, takes on an even more than ordinary in- terest, beeause of the special article about the Canadian soldier, which has been contributed by Fred. A. Talbot, the well-known author on Canadian subjects. The article is Called "Johnny Canuek," and is illustrated by 14 photographs.</p>
        <p>_ As is the custom of " The War Budget," every phase and theatre of the war is dealt with, and from Canada, for .the special article, " The War Budget" goes to the Italian front for its cover, which shows in a stirring picture the six gallant Italian soldiers who held the Garibaldi Rock, against a vastly • superior number of Austrians.</p>
        <p>The issue is, full of interesting war pictures from the various theatres of war, and the articles maintain the high standard set by this threepenny war weekly.</p>
        <p>Keeping Their Minds Occupied. "You didn't really care when you daughter eloped with young Nabob?" "Of course not." • "Then, why did your motor car pursue theirs 80 miles?" • "I didn't want to give them leisure to repent and Come back."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0350" n="art0350">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0351" n="art0351">
        <head>(ALL Ittotris ICEsEnvED.]</head>
        <p>(ALL Ittotris ICEsEnvED.]</p>
        <p>THE SENSITIVE JAPANESE.</p>
        <p>Writing to you, Japanese ladies will in- variably commence their letters by apolo- gising for their rudeness on the last occasion that they met you. Apparently they are con- scious of not having treated you wifh the courtesy that is due to your honourable self, and thus they unconsciously help to maintain the exalted standards of etiquette and gen- tility with which tradition and a, noble an- cestry has downed them. You often receive such letters, too, when actually you are feeling that they have' not on welcomed you in their home but have overwhelmed you with politeness .al atten- tion. If you are resident in Japan acts of this nature continue to amaze you until you be- gin to realise the acute seusitivenc.ss. of .these people in the land of the cherry-blossom, a delicacy of feeling which has 'earned for them their reputation of being THE POLITEST PEOPLE Qlsi tiRTIL</p>
        <p>A Japanese girl will, ask pardon of a shop- keeper for troubling him, and will curtsey to another customer standing by, and I.g her to mike her purchase first, awl again bow in response to the curtsey of that lath, who in her .turn gently insists' that she .fias much time to -spare and need not take precedence. " Excuse me " is a term that is for ever on their lips, .and it is -always accompanied by their characteristic grace of manner. Sometimes you think that they are even apologetic for having intruded their pres- ence upon the earth. A similar spirit perme- ates the men. A white man may tell them the grossest of lies • and their reply, accom- panied by a bow and a smile, will always be —" is that so ?" Japanese - never contradict. It is impo:ite. But they will tight you if you insult them, and kill you if you strike them. One may not return them discourtesy for their politeness. Personal reputation is very dear to these facile-tongued little lions. You must not, for instance, address one of, them as "John."</p>
        <p>"What, John ?" he will say indignantly, "I am no Chinaman."</p>
        <p>Nor speak of him as a " Jap " or "na- tive." "Do you take me for a blackman ?" American jack-tars when their ships are in the Japanese ports often find that insults of- lered to Japanese coolies bring ultimate dis- aster. A batch of them from the cruiser Chatanooga," during an over-indulgent evening in the early part of 1909; unluckily thought fit to -e:bow a Rickshaman off 'the footpath in a low quarter Of Yokohama. Shortly afterwards the American fleet drew out of the harbour—to ritturn in the autumn. Batches of sailors came ashore, as ,usual, and particularly did some 50 or 60 Japanese men, unobservedly scattered in the vicinity of the harbour, observe the ' jack tars • from that same " Chatanooga." By-and-by they bad shadowed their man, and presently lie and his companions, enjoyinc, taemselves in the same low quarter of the settlement, were treated to the roughest • ten minutes that they had perhaps ever , undergone. They were nearly beaten to death.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0352" n="art0352">
        <head>STANDARD OF MORALITY.</head>
        <p>THE STANDARD OF MORALITY.</p>
        <p>Few insults outrage Japanese sensibilities More comp:etely • than the aspersions which are occasionally cast by Europeans upon pub- lic morality. Citizens in Tokyo are proud to answer these comments by recalling incidents such as that which occurred not very long ,ago in one of the old baronial mansions in the interior. A high:y situated young retainer had been smitten by the 'beauty of one of the daughters of. a branch of the 'household. He wrote to her, and she ignored the commu- nication, as became a Japanese inaid.,A sec- ond letter was similariy - disregarded, and, overcome by, the fervency of his passion, the young 'man visited the girl's apartments in person and threw himself on his knees before her. She, turned from him, keeping silent, and only when he again threw 'himself in front• of her did she appear to recognise his presence. A girl of high station, she was carrying at her side the silver-mounted dirk which such maidens are accustomed to wear as a symbol of seN-protecting maidenhood. Quickly drawing .the dagger from its sheath the girl stabbe4 the youth in the e&amp;e,,, kill- ing him instantly. She was arraigned 'shortly afterwards before the private court of the household, which at once exonerated and con- gratulated her, the presiding Baron remark- ing that her behaviour had been simply in accordance With the traditions of Japanese maidenly virtue. ,</p>
        <p>What saves these extraordinary people from the prudery which- characterises some of the peop:es of the Western world is the ex- treme delicacy of their outlook upon life. It is delicacy unadulterated, for instance, which enables .the sexes to swim together .in large numbers,, clothed, say, in those -natural gar- ments of :purity, modesty and a smile. This purity of instinct is in dominance when a Ja- panese housewife deputes a young serving- girl to attend iipcm a male guest when he takes a bath in the house, she herself calmly asiisting .him in the transfer of clothing: The children that are bred in this perfectly na- tural atmosphere grow up eventually to share with their parents the conception that for- eign notions of modesty are barbarous and indelicate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0353" n="art0353">
        <head>ASHAMED TO BE UNEMPLOYED</head>
        <p>ASHAMED TO BE UNEMPLOYED</p>
        <p>A Japanese family resembles one sensitive orga,nisation composed 'of separately oper- ating par. The alightest parental wish Is .intorpnited as a command. Many !sunadoina puno4se undnr in, s.flu!pmoad but, on the other hand, there are aspects of this complexity of nature which put Euro- pean points of view to shame. A Japanese man is ashamed, for instance, to be uneni- ployed. Observe the sensitiveness of mind manifested by the writer eif the following let- ter, which was written by an hotel manager who had fai:ed in business, and was seeking employment in a European business house: " Dear Sir, . _ .</p>
        <p>"As you know, I am obliged to give up my present business not in the further.. All in the respect I must find opportunity to get one. My wife especially anxious that I should not remain laughed at by the world. You offered me situation, and I proposed you to give me over at yen as salary, but now I have, I remember, some amount 2sf pension, which I getting annually. I do not want so much. I would be satisfied only that the world recognise me that lam working. I will never forget your kindness in my life. While waiting -the favour of your reply, "I remain, "yours very truly," etc</p>
        <p>An this man wanted was that the world should not laugh at him. The Japanese will accept next ,to nothing rather than remain unemployed for a long 'period. Another fea- ture of the delicacy .of their nature: they never make disturbances in a public street. They are very rarely participants in a drunk- en brawl anywhere. Drink themselves drunk? Yes, but if it can be so, like gentlemen. If a man insiCted a woman or a girl he would be ejected from' the neighbourhood in which he lived. Their behaviour to strangers is cour- tesy personified. Their morality may be ques- tioned, judged by our Western standards: but their decency .never. Even the walls .of their houses are sensitive and delicate. You could drive your fist through a whole row of the paper-panelled partitions which divide their rooms. And the land they live in—it is nothing but a sensitive throbbing organism, which ever and anon quakes and rumbles and Upheaves—to the terror of its wondering lit- tle people, a land of sunshine and flowers, and beautiful streams and soft bracing breezes. an Eden in which a people could scarcely be aught other than sensitive, and delicate, and clean. •</p>
        <p>One Thing Certain. "Your. baby cries a great deal." "Yes," answered Mr. Bliggins. "It isn't well." • What's the ailment "I don't know. The only thing I'm sure of is that, it isn't the sleeping sickness."</p>
        <p>Not Much of a Sailor. Small Boy: "Did you ever catch any whales?" Sailor: "No." Small Boy: "Ever shipwrecked ?" Sailor: "No." Small Boy: Ever cast on a desert island?" Sailor: "No." Small Boy: "Ever caught by cannibals?" Sailor: "No." Small Boy (disgusted): "Why, you might ab well have stayed on land!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0354" n="art0354">
        <head>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</p>
        <p>Poor Saddles Are To Be Rigorously Avoided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0355" n="art0355">
        <head>CYCLOMETER.</head>
        <p>THE CYCLOMETER.</p>
        <p>(Queries and correspondence should be addressed to "The Hub," c/o The Editor. Replies can- not be sent by' post and all queries will be answere&amp;in our columns.)</p>
        <p>Probably not one cyclist in .a hundred who listens to the miles ticking off on his cyclometer realises that the 'measurement itt after all only an approximate one, and liable to variation. For all practical purposes the cyclometer is. satisfactory enough, but the underlying prin- ciple is quite interesting. In itself, the cyclomaer does not measure the distance, but the number of. revolutions of the front wheel required to traverse a certain length of • the road, the revolutions being transformed into distance by the mechanism which is constructed in accordance with accurate mathematical cal- culation. The makers allow for the difference between 26 and 28 inch wheels, but that does not ensure perfect accuracy, as a front wheel well inflated has a bigger radius than one where the tyre is soft, and the circumference is conse- quently greater perhaps by several. inches. Though slight in one revolution, the variation would make an appreciable total when the figures of the cyclometer mount up into thousands, were it' not for the fact that the tyre has not always the same degree of inflation. Of course, the state of the back tyre is of no im- portance, as the two wheels run. free of each other, and the cyclometer is only affected by the one to which it is aCtually connected. WATER FOR CLEANING.</p>
        <p>I have often seen it advocated that the best cleaning medium for cycles is water. -Unless this statement is qualified it is certainly incor- rect. Under certain circumstances, however, it can be used succksfully. Enamel is impervious to moisture; therefore no quantity of water can hurt it. . Care must be taken, of course, that it does not reach the bearings, but is used for the frame alone. , To guard against •this, it should be used sparingly- Don't turn the hose Pipe •on the 'machine. A damped cloth is the best thing to employ. The whole of the. frame and mud guards can be rubbed over with, the cloth, and in this way the mud will be removed. As long as the enamel is intact the water cannot do any damage. If any of the enamel has been chipped off, however, no water should be used. In this case the water will be retained, and this will act upon the metal. in time the part may become weakened, and when any extra strain is Put on the machine, an accident may result.- '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0356" n="art0356">
        <head>SADDLE HINT.</head>
        <p>A SADDLE HINT.</p>
        <p>One acknowledged defect of the cheap cycle is that the manufacturers always seem to save money by fitting a very inferior saddle. From the point of view of actual reliability of the cycle the action is hardly open to question, as there must be economy somewhere, and far better for it to be in the saddle-- than in the frame. . Unfortunately, however, a poor .saddle, often makes cycling a mild form of torture. It is very little use recommending a particular variety, as individual tastes vary so much, but most cyclists will probably airee with the dic- tum " When you get a good saddle stick to it." I have been riding the same saddle for a matter of eight years or so, and like it better every time. • Not the most expensive example of saddle-maker's art would tempt me to change. A year or two ago I sent it off to the makers who fitted a new seat on the old frame in a manner thoroughly satisfactory, and at about one-third • the cost of a new one. The same re- juvenated saddle now. accompanies a new machine, and when it again shows Signs of wear back it goes to the maker. -Even the most per- suasiVe cycle dealer or attractive catalogue will never induce me to purchase a new one. At any rate, not until I am forced to do -so. . OVERHAULING THE MAGNETO.</p>
        <p>Comparatively few riders are recommended to undertake the delicate task of overhauling the magneto. " In ninety-nine oat of a hundred cases this is best left to the profesMonal or to the maker. There are some riders, however, who possess a considerable amount of knowledge re- guiding automobile engineering, and this and the following .note are especially for them. When tackling the work it is much better to do so systematically than in a haphazard manner. The contact breaker should first of all be removed and the platinum points examined. They should be clean, and the platinum. faces should meet squarely with. a distance of about half a millimetre (1/50in.) between.. The bell crank lever should work freely and the spring which works it must not become weak. Retiiii- ing the magneto can be done in the following way: The timing lever should 15e. placed over in full advance position, and after removing the water-tight cover, the armature should be rotated, until the platinum points just break. The space between the pole shoe and the arma- ture edge should at this point be 'about 1/16in. In tbe metric system this is about 11 milli- metres.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0357" n="art0357">
        <head>CARBON HOLDERS.</head>
        <p>THE CARBON HOLDERS.</p>
        <p>After this The carbon holders and the brushes should be inspected for cracks Or breakages. A defective holder can be detected, since there</p>
        <p>will be a strong odour of burning vulcaait owing to the current leaking through the er6‘4 and burning this . material. The cabin should be an easy fit in the holders, 011,01 their faces are fitted .or greasy they shell!" trimmed with a 'file. The. collecting ring bc-€4l be cleaned if -a rag soaked in petrol is pun%e r through the hole into which the brush linwed fastens. The brushes should then be rePla4o and the machine run at varying speeds. I° ease of a twin the strength. of the current ej be determined by the distance it will i°l.lig when the engine is at good speed. The brii should be at .in. The sparking'at the should be noted to see that it is not csees°: With full advance and at high speed :10 machine is in good order if there is very sparking. If the armature or condenser tio defective the best plan is to return the inagnebe to the maker so- that the new parts lilaY - fitted.</p>
        <p>PETROL FIRES, hat The large number of petrol fires brought 3—,.61 by collision,, leakage, &amp;.c. which have occaPr„. lately, naturally directs attention to the V(i2i vision of suitable appliances for their sPeeter extinction. In any case of burning spirit 0„I is worse than useless, merely serving to sPre:e'r the fire. It should never he employed e11(,,j any circumstances. Most garages, public ,!c"h- private, have a bin containing sand, whiri, usually proves effective, but recent eslie,,Ll ments in America seem to indicate that saup-r will soon be replaced by sawdust. A nllll/%: of tests were made, and in every case it "':-; reported that the flames were extinguished iv from 25 to 50 seconds,- and that with a cerit thin sprinkling of sawdust. The latter, appears, can be made even more efficaciou4 °to mixing with it a small quantity of s-odi'.lo bicarbonate, which liberates carbon 4h()1 when heated. While talking about fires. the importance•of care in handling sP";.,' on all occasions cannot be over-emphasised, 10,, tieularly in a garage. wherc vt is never Pi to expose a naked light. Even a slight es' plosion may be sufficient to fire a car. PROTECTION AGAINST TH EFT.</p>
        <p>- - - - No sensible 'person ever dreams of leaviiv valuable jewellery lying about for anyone nick up who may feel inclined. But bulky thing, sach as a motor car, the totr:e practice seems to be to throw all caution to ; winds, and'N.trust that nothing will 1110,,*0 The theft of motor cars is far more con"'-r, than one would suppose, and really if is prising that such .cases are not even more fie (went. Cars are becoming more and standardised every year: those from. the maker can hardly .be distinguished . one fr°,ll the other, anl even more obvious features. with as brightly coloured bodies, can be beyond recognition. Of course, the ideal To, of affairs would be for no car to be left 1";;; out an occupant, or failing this, the use satisfactory device. There are several 011,:,,g market, especially in the form of a lock bol, the change speed lever in the neutral positii. although a good stout chain with padi°,-5 would serve equally well, although perit'' rather troublesome to manipulate. THE</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
