      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0374" n="art0374">
        <head>BIG WAR FUND HANDICAP.</head>
        <p>THE BIG WAR FUND HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>Closing Games and Prize Distribution</p>
        <p>To-day.</p>
        <p>The final games in the sue,cessful War Fund Handicap promoted by the Works' Bowling Association, will be played on the Horse and Jockey green, Winwick-road, this afternoon, when the presence of a large crowd of enthusi- asts is anticipated. As a result of the handicap</p>
        <p>the sum of £.30 has been raised, and it will be divided in equal portions amongst the Terri- torial Equipment Fund, the Liberal Women's War Sewing Class and the Conservative Women's War Sewing Class. It is hoped that Sir Arthur Crosfield, Bart., will be present, together with the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman Peter and Mrs. Peacock), and other well-known local ladies and gentle- men. Mesdames E. C. Fairclough (wife of Colonel Fairclough) and J. D. Fairclough. have promised to attend and receive the share of the proceeds allotted to the Territorial Equipment Fund, whilst • Mrs. Emma C. Osborne will accept the Liberal Women's share and Mrs. W. Peter Rylands the Conservative Women's por- tion.</p>
        <p>It is expected that the final game will be reached about 5 o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0375" n="art0375">
        <head>SOUTH LANCASHIRES AT HORLEY</head>
        <p>SOUTH LANCASHIRES AT HORLEY</p>
        <p>Natives Beaten.</p>
        <p>The return match between No. 1 Supply Com- pany of the 2/ 4th South Lancashire Regiment stationed at Honey, Surrey, and the Holley team was played on Thursday with the follow- ing result :-- South Lancashires. Honey. Lce.-Cpl. J. F. Naylor Mr. Woods ... Sergt. J. Lloyd Pte. W. Shaw ' 2l Mr. Landway Coml. B. Naylor Mr. Ross Pte. H. Baguley . Mr. Cheesman Pte. W. Allen Corpl. B. Wicks Pte. J. Slater Pte. W. H. Thornely 2l Mr. Strap.. Corpl. B. Naylor- L.-Cpl. H. Broadhurst Mr. Cox Pte. J. W. Cartwright 20 Mr. West</p>
        <p>.. 17</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0376" n="art0376">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKING MEN'S ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>N.A. Centre.</p>
        <p>A GOOD OPENING.</p>
        <p>The opening match at Hartford on Sunday provided the members with good sport, although the river did not appear in the best condition. Moss and rubbish at the bottom kept anglers busy cleaning their baits,' but this did not mar the match, as results show. The conveyance left the Cooper's Arms at 8 a.m. and a pleasant journey to Hartford was enjoyed. Several mem- bers brought along their wives, the ladies enjoy- ing the outing up to the mark. Secretary Mr. Swarbrick soon had members at their pegs, and a start was made about one o'clock. :Result: 1, F. Jolley, 21b.; 2, E. Hill, 11b. 12oz. Bdr.; 3, H. Moore, 11b. 7oz. Bdr.; 4, A. Stout, 11b. 3oz. 4dr.; 5, B. Bradbury, 11b. 2oz. Bdr.; 6, J. Preston, 11b. 20z.; 7, G. Hamblett, 11b. 4dr.; 8, J. Green, 12oz. 3dr.; 9, W. Whitfield, 120 z.; 10, F. Trigger, lloz. Bdr.; and so on down to 4oz. 3dr., which was the lowest weight. Several good bream were weighed in by North- wich members last week-end from the Weaver.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0377" n="art0377">
        <head>ELLESMERE.</head>
        <p>ELLESMERE.</p>
        <p>Two members who visited this fishery last week-end had good sport, taking about 451 b. of fish in two days' angling. Mr. E. Brookes weighed in a bream of 21b. 7oz. Mr. T. Single- ton weighed in a roach of 11b. Rm.,. and a perch of 12oz. Other fish registered'ranged from 14oz. down to Boz. The Mere here always provides good sport early in the season before it breaks up, and again at the latter end of September.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0378" n="art0378">
        <head>NORTON – NO POLLUTION.</head>
        <p>NORTON - NO POLLUTION.</p>
        <p>The canal here is the scene of the great match to-day (Saturday), and , when being overlooked on Thursday was in fine condition. One mem- ber took a couple of nice roach in fine condition. in the first few minutes. I am sorry to say that some unkind person set out the rumour that this length was polluted. When our society is working for a cause that will be beneficial to our wounded soldiers and District Nursing Association, it is unfair to us to set such rumours afloat without having definite informa- tiOn. However, we wrote tlip Runcorn secre- tary on the matter, and this is his reply : Runcorn Anglers' Association, Runcorn, June 30th, 1015. Dear Mr. Stout,—l am awfully sorry that a report of the kind you mention has been circulated With the intention of doing harm to so good an object, but I hope it will not inter- fere with the success of the match. It is quite true that a quantity of fish have died in the canal, but our length has not been affected any more than the Warrington ,portion in this re- spect. We attribute the cause of this to the exceptionally dry spell of weather we have been having lately, combined with the natural losses after spawning. However, we are certain that pollution is not the cause. Our end of the canal is fishing quite as well as it has done other years. I have not seen anyone who has been to Norton yet, but there is no doubt that there are plenty of fish in the canal, and, as you know, it wil improve later in the season.—l remain,</p>
        <p>yours</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0379" n="art0379">
        <head>SiC., I. CLARE."</head>
        <p>SiC., I. CLARE."</p>
        <p>NOTICES.</p>
        <p>Club night at Bay Horse Monday next. Mem- bers please note, all must be straight on .the books to take 'part in the opening match on Sunday week. Trains to Norton for the match, 12.30 and 2 p.m.</p>
        <p>Trains to Preston Brook for the match, 12.30 and 1.17 p.m. , A conveyance will leave headquarters, Glad- stone Arms, foi• the match at 1.15. A. S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0380" n="art0380">
        <head>JOLLY ANGLERS</head>
        <p>JOLLY ANGLERS</p>
        <p>Have A Good Time Within</p>
        <p>Sight Of The Huns.</p>
        <p>Driver G. Pye, a Northern .1 nglei*; writing from the Front to his uncle, Mr. Joseph Cox, baker. Church-street Warrington, chairman of the Warrington Section of the Northern Anglers' Association, says : u I am sorry to hear of your being so unfor- tunate with the fish at Norton, but truSt you enjoyed the outing. My chum and I have been more fortunate. We each bought a cheap rod and some tackle in the town last week- end and -have • had several nice -catches. We had a heavy thunderstorm yesterday, which coloured the dykes nicely. We went out after tea last night and had a couple' of hours at it and landed 101 b. or 121 b. of fish. My chum got a 'fine tench 211 b., and we had four perch all scaling over 11b., so we are having fine times in this snug part of the firm's line. I'll just mention our menus to-day.-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0381" n="art0381">
        <head>Breakfast.</head>
        <p>Breakfast.</p>
        <p>"Coxes bread and butter (with good luck not damaged). "Fried fish of four varieties—perch, tench, roach and a young pike. "Marmalade.</p>
        <p>Dinner. "Fried steak, new potatoes, green peas an oxtail soup. Tea.</p>
        <p>" Cox's bread and butter. "Salmon. "Stewed cherries, of which we are able to buy any amount at Id. a capful. "What do you think of that lot for soldiers on.active service, and only a quarter of a mile from the Huns?</p>
        <p>"I do the cooking, for which I get plenty of praise (in soldiers' fa-shion). We intend making the most of it while we are in this quarter. I don't suppose we shall ever get such another, and if we are tc!, have a second winter of it we must build ourselves up."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0382" n="art0382">
        <head>ORFORD ST. JOHN'S F.C.</head>
        <p>ORFORD ST. JOHN'S F.C.</p>
        <p>MEN.</p>
        <p>What They Have Done And How They Are Faring.</p>
        <p>Private John Smith, 21, Norris-street, secre- tary of the Orford St. John's F.C., the club that enlisted to a man, who is engaged on ammunition work at Birmingham, sends us two letters he has rec6ived from his brothers, and news as to the condition of other members of his most patriotic team. He was on active s4vice himself, but was invalided home, and when convalescent, put to his present important occupation. _ _</p>
        <p>As regards the Orford St. John's men, he says, they are doing as well as can be expected. He adds:—</p>
        <p>Private Wm. Stratford makes a strong appeal or recruits.</p>
        <p>Driver John Ward is recovering in Maidstone Hospital. Corporal Luke At''nerton is recovering nicely. Private Thomas Tickle still is in good health, and hopes all the other Junior Clubs have followed our example (Orford St. John's). Private Geo. Tomlinson says their Battalion (South Lancs.) are still doing well, and hopes they will complete another battalion over at Warrington.</p>
        <p>Driver John Devine, who has been wounded, has recovered, and again ready for action.</p>
        <p>WOUNDED IN THE GREAT CHARGE. Ills brother, Private Ernest Snith, wounded in the great Territorial charge, writes to him from the Second Canadian Hospital, B.E. Force:—</p>
        <p>"1 am very glad indeed to know that you are busy with, ammunition, as that is what is needed at present over here. I am feeling ' champion ' now. My leg is a bit stiff, but my Wound is healing up Al.</p>
        <p>"I have dropped- in lucky being in hospital, especially here; the nurses are a treat. You could not believe how _nice and cheerful they are. I am in a nice ward -and the hospital is on the coast. 'Yes, we are now having a gentle- man's life. I can tell you it is quite a treat to he out of the sounding of the guns and in a nice soft bed instead of being in the trenches waiting for the Germans to show a leg. I can knock about a little now, and I along with some other ward mates often go for a walk round the cliffs.</p>
        <p>"Oh, the appetite I have got now—all sorts of courses for supper."</p>
        <p>REFLECTIONS FROM THE TRENCHES.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Fred Smith, Royal Munster Fusiliers, writes to his brother from the Front:—</p>
        <p>"I am sorry to read of the death of W. Newns and his brother in less than a month's time. It seems rather bard on their parents, but God's will be done. I also see a letter in the paper from Joe Newns, his brother, to his father, telling him that his two brothers have one to the One above, the One that loved them. Isn't that a glorious testimony to the comfort- ing power that Jesus gives. Ah, John, if we could only trust God a little more than we do we should see a great difference in this war, for I firmly believe that the Lord is on our side, and also this that if the Kaiser had his own way it would not be for God, therefore, what have we to fear? Why, nothing at all. "Thank you very much for paper, as it is the only paper that links me to the South Lancashire Regiment, and shows me how the other Warrington B'hoys are going on 'in their new sphere of life.</p>
        <p>"Glad you are still busy with the ammuni- tion. The nuicker you send it the sooner the war will end." All end with best wishes to their friends in Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0383" n="art0383">
        <head>RIFLE SHOOTING.</head>
        <p>RIFLE SHOOTING.</p>
        <p>A match between teams representing Fletcher, Russell's and the Cheshire Volunteer Regiment (Stockton Heath Platoon) took place on Friday evening last at the range of the former. Ten rounds at 50 yards. Result:—</p>
        <p>Fletcher, Russo Phillips 97, G. Legge 95</p>
        <p>D. Fleming 94, A. Dennett 94, J. Quarni-1;y 90, J. Towers 89, IL Whiteley 89, G. MePhie 89, J. Colley 87, J. Nicholson 87, W. Banks 86, Mr. T. W. Fletcher 73, E. Daintith 83, A. Mason 83, J. Buchanan 82, D. Richardson 81, W. Graham 77, H. 0. Bennett 71, J. Bradshaw 55- 1,602.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Volunteer Regiment (Stockton Heath Platoon).—A. Shepherd 97, J. Ferguson 87, J. Gamble (sub.) 87, E. L. Robinson 86, R. M. Swinton 86, Mr. W. Russell 84, E. R. Row- son 84, T. Fletcher 83, J. McDermid 81, E. Brad- shaw 80, F. Warburton 79, J. H. Fletcher 78, W. Leach (sub.) 78, H. B. Houghton 77, J. Ben- nett 76, H. Stirrup 73, J. Corlett (sub.) 73, J. Cox 71, R. Tunner (sub.) 68-1.528. Win for Fletcher, Russell's, 74.</p>
        <p>MONKS, HALL ex. co. v. RYLANDS' BROS.</p>
        <p>On the Rylands's range. Scores Monks, Hal 1.-11. Westmoreland 98, C. Shepley 97, W. Kellie 93, A. Highley 91, W. Hutchison 90, G. Copeland 87, G. Cox 87, Wm. Houghton 86, A. Lester 84, A. Webb 82, J. Sisson 60-955--less J. Sisson 60-895.</p>
        <p>Rylands's.—H. Gaunt 90, R. Maskell 88, John Bramhall 87, J. Critehley 87, W. Newton 84, Joe Bramhall 81, Hosier 87, S. Williams 78, Paskin 78, S. Garnett 76, E. Balmer 69-896-- less E. Balmer 69-827. Monks, Hall won by 68 point's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0384" n="art0384">
        <head>LALL RIGHTS RESERVED . ]</head>
        <p>LALL RIGHTS RESERVED . ]</p>
        <p>THE LIGHTER SIDE OF CRICKET. ,</p>
        <p>Although cricket is perhaps not witity in itself, there is neverthetess a great deal of i:un to be got out of it. Most of it comes of course from the crowd, and one has only to walk round the ring at a first class countw match to hear a number of remarks full Of caustic and personal humour, as for instance, when a particular:y stout player was hit in the stomach by the ball an enthusiast imme- diately shoutea to the batsman, "Don't play agin the wind. Play 'em the other way." But it is when cricket is dull that the spectators get their chance and as a rule they make the most of it. Every single is cheered as though it were a sixer, and in between whiles snatches of the "Dead March" will be whistled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0385" n="art0385">
        <head>CRICKET "DOWN lINVER."</head>
        <p>CRICKET "DOWN lINVER."</p>
        <p>In Australian crowd is especially free and outspoken, and is not at all particular whe- ther anybody is offended or not. In a ,big game "down under" Alec Bannerman was batting, and the English fieldsmen clustered all round him, "W. G." edging as near as possible in the hope of being a-b:e to get a snap catch. Suddenly a wag shouted from the stand: " Look out, Alec, W. G.'s after ser !pocket ! " A. C. MacLaren, although a great favourite with the Colonials has come in for his share of chaff, and a funnsy story is told of how they tried to bribe him. It was in a Test Match and MacLaren was fieldirtip A. tremendous skyer was sent up, and as he waited patiently for the ball a voice called out appealingly: "Please misg it, Archie. Then I'll let you kiss ray sister !" That catch was not missed ! A commoti trick, and one that disconcerts the fielder is someone to yell out "Now !" just as the ball reaches his hands, and when it is dropped as it often is in such cases the crowd shows its glee by shouting "Butter fingers." Talking of catches W. G. Grace tells a very amusing story at the expense of .Mr. E. H. D. Sewell. London County were playing Warwick at Coventry and ,Sewell missed three catches at short slip which in nine cases out of ten he would have held. "Seeing his great disgust,'; said, the Doctor, "I shouted 'Cheer up, you'll catch the next'" Soon afterwards a fast ball was sent down which the batsman just touched. It went straight to Sewell, who was unprepared for it, and hit him in the chest ! Next day when the team were on their way back to tondon what should they see on a bookstall, but 'a magazine containing an article on "The Art of Catching," by E. H. D. Sewell. It was un- animously resolved by his comrades that Se- well be asked to contribute a second article on "How to Drop 'Em."-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0386" n="art0386">
        <p>oamearatummulkvanosimmitismawakimem43 • Reinforce Your Health 1 I. ra, 110 Prepared only by THOMAS BEECHAM, St Helens, Lane. Sold everywhere in boxes, price 1;11, (56 pills) &amp; 219 (168 pills). 1111 SagettaiNgenallbeigigaglatilVattataZZOMattal</p>
