      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0406" n="art0406">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 3, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 3, 1915.</p>
        <p>LATE MR. T. J. GARNETT.</p>
        <p>Warrington Poor Lose An Old</p>
        <p>And Genial Friend.</p>
        <p>An old and respected resident, and one who was well-known for his work in connection with the administration of the Poor Law, has been lost to Warrington in the person of Mr.l Thomas James Garnett, of 40, Arpley-street, who passed away in his 72nd year on Monday morning, after having been in indifferent health for close upon two years. The deceased gentleman was a native of Penketh and had spent the whole of his long life in the War- rington district. For almost thirty years he acted as a relieving officer to the Warrington Union, having charge, for the greater part of that time, of the No. 1. District, his office being situated at the Workhouse. He retired from that position some six years ago, his re- tirement being made the occasion of the pre- sentation to him of a gold watch, subscribed for by his fellow officials and by the members of the Board, whom he so faithfully served. The watch bore the inscription, as epitomising the opinion of those whom he served and with whom he worked Presented to Mr. Thos. J. Garnett, by the Guardians and officers of the Warrington -Union .as*.a mark of their esteem and regard on the occasion of his re- tirement from the position of relieving officer, after 29 years' faithful service.-2nd October, 1909."</p>
        <p>A .Conservative and a Friendly Society Man. The deceased gentleman was for many years a well-known member of the Conservative and at one time 'he ranked prominently amongst local cueists, although he found his chief relaxation, when weather and business permitted, in the "gentle art," being an en- thusiastic and popular member of the War- rington Anglers' Association and delighting to ply the rod in the reach •of water near to the canal bridge at Moore. For many years Mr. Garnett took an active interest in Friendly Society work, and in addition to being an old member of the "Loyal Admiral Nelson" Lodge of the Independent Order of Oddfellows (M.V.) was also a trustee of the Lodge. He is Mourned by a widow, one son and two daughters, and is kindly remembered by hosts of the poor people, with whom his old office brought him into contact, his geniality and affability helping to rob of its terrors the visit to his office of even the most sensitive who were driven by stern necessity to have recourse to the aid of the Poor Law.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place on Thursday after- noon at the Warrington Cemetery. The ser-</p>
        <p>vice was conducted by the Rev. Carle:</p>
        <p>ton, and the chief Mourners were Mr. Fred Garnett (son), Mr. Richard Garnett (brother), Mr. R. Daintith (son-in-law), Mr. Fred Rose, and Mr. G. 11. Smith. There were also present Mr. A. Bottomley, Mir. Shearer, Mr. Gidman and Mr. Bowes, representing the Warrington Union; Mr. R.. H. Daintith, Mr. J. H. Daven- port, Mr. J. Holland, and Mr. T. Williams, representing the Conservative Association; Mr. C. E. Houghton, Mr. Harold Whittle, Mr. W. Taylor (representing the Warrington Cycling Club. of which the deceased was treasurer), Mr. W. Whittaker, Mr. Alfred Jolley, and Mr. J. H. Gittens. There were also present a few elderly clients who had received relief at his hands in years past and who paid their last tribute in silence. Messrs. J. 3lounfield and Sons carried out the funeral arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0407" n="art0407">
        <head>GUARDIANS' SYMPATHY.</head>
        <p>GUARDIANS' SYMPATHY.</p>
        <p>At Wednesday's meeting of the Warrington Guardians the Chairman Proposed that a vote of, sympathy be sent to the relatives of the deceased gentleman, the resolution being car- ried by all standing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0408" n="art0408">
        <head>OUR SPLENDID BOYS</head>
        <p>OUR SPLENDID BOYS</p>
        <p>Simply Mowed The Germans Down.</p>
        <p>- Corporal H. Read, of the Royal Engineers, has sent the following letter, dated June 16th, to his grandmother, Mrs. Travis, 110, Sharp- street, Warrington:— " No one can realise how much it bucks a chap up to receive a good, cheery letter from home, and as for thanking -kis for what we are , doing here, well I would not give much for the man's principle who would not do his bit, more especially if he had seen some of the sights that I have seen Since coming out. One day we left our billet at 2 p.m., not knowing our destination. We had a march of about 15 miles and found ourselves in this, place., We were in the town a week when the shells started coming; we had to move 'pretty quickly to get out of it. What made it worse was that we had- to go forward, instead of back; consequently we had them coining over us from three sides for the whole of the ten days' bombardment. Dur- ing the Week previous to the bombardment I had been in the trenches .mining with my section, but had come -down for a rest. On the eighth day we expected an attaek, and I went up to the trenches with an exploder to set the mines off while the Germans were on the top of them. I had only two days to ivait, but on the night of the tenth day they came. " You Ought to Have, Seen Our Boys."</p>
        <p>'You ought to have seen our boys. It was splendid. The infantry jumped on top of the trenches, and simply mowed them down. Our, artillery was sending shells just four or five feet, so, with all their shells and their great numbers, they could not breat- • through. They must have under-rated the fighting powers of the 'boys.' The biggest damage done is in the town. Everywhere you look you find just ruins —fine buildings brought to the ground. Of the beautiful cathedral nothing remains except the tower and four walls. Taking the fight all round, we did not lose anything like the number of men that one would expect after such a terrible struggle. "One bit of praise I must give, and that is</p>
        <p>to the ammunition supply drivers. There was one corner we christened Devil's Corner.' It was the only road by which horses and wagons could get. There was not one minute that this place was not being shelled, but these boys did not step; they simply put their horses on a mad gallop and dashed through it all. You would see horses ,and men blown up in the air. We were awaitink orders 50 yards from this corner, and every time we saw them come through safely we could feel a lump in our throats and our hearts swelled with pride to think we be- longed to the same little army as those lads, and along the line you would hear someone say: Thank God for bringing them through safely.' I see, the old town of Warrington has done her share, for the Territorials have done some jolly good work."</p>
        <p>You can't reconcile a woman to gray hairs even by telling her how beautiful they make' her look, but you can get mighty popular with her.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0409" n="art0409">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. RICHARD BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. RICHARD BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>We regret to announce the death of Mr. Richard Bridge, who passed away at his resi- dence, 2, Battersby-lane, On • June 25th. Mr. Bridge has carried on the business of milk 'dealer, and was `widely known and respected in the neighbourhood, and among a large circle of friends. His religious attachment was with the Primitive Methodist Church, and he gave ser- vice to the Sunday school as its secretary and to the Circuit Churches as a circuit steward.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place on Monday. The service was conducted in the Legh-street Primi- tive Methodist Church, with which Mr. Bridge had a life-long membership. After the hymn "Through the night of doubt and sorrow," the service was read by the Rev. A. J. Chessum, and the Rev. W. Cooper offered prayer. The inter-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0410" n="art0410">
        <head>LATE MIL BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MIL BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>meat took place at the Cemetery, the committal service being read by the, Rev. W. Cooper. A large number of friends assembled at the service and at the graveside. Among the chief mourners were: Mr. and Mrs. C. Worthington (son-in-law and daughter), Mr. and Mrs. A. H. Ward, Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Parkes, Mr. and Mrs. J. H. Elwell, Mr. &amp; Mrs. T. S. Bridge, Mr. J. Cann, Mrs. Bindon, Mr. W. Cann, Mr. and Mrs. T. Massey, Mr. M. Butterfield (Bradford), Mr. and Mrs. J. Griffiths, Mr. Bissell, Mr. C. Cotton, Mr. W. Turner, Mr. J. Cann, jun., and Mr. W. Massey. Floral tributes were received from: Mr. and Mrs. A. H. and Mr. W. Ward, Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Parkes, Mr. and Mrs. J. 11. Elwell, Mr. and Mrs. T. S. Bridge (Liverpool), Mrs. Martha Sindon and Mr. and Mrs. W. Wilson, Mr. and Mrs. T. Massey, J. Cann, jun., Mr. and Mrs. M. Butterfield (Bradford), Mrs. Mills, Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church, Mrs. and Mr. C. Worthington (daughter and son-in-law), Grand- daughter Dorothy, Mr. W. Turner (Walton), Mrs. W. Crampton, Mr. and Mrs. J. Griffiths, Mr. and Mrs. J. R. R. Thompson, Mr. T. Everall and family, "A few friends." Messrs. J. Mounfield and Sons carried out the funeral arrangements. It is announced that a memorial service will be held on Sunday evening, July 11th, conducted by the Rev. W. Cooper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0411" n="art0411">
        <head>DEATH OF CAPT. HALSALL OWEN. .</head>
        <p>DEATH OF CAPT. HALSALL OWEN. .</p>
        <p>With regret we announce the death of Cap- tain Halsall Owen, which took place at his resi- dence, Burfield, Appleton, on Monday, after a few weeks' illness. Captain Owen, who was the second son of the late Mr. William Owen, J.P., architect, Cairo-street, Warrington, was born in 1881, and was educated at Liverpool College. After acting as engineer with the Horsebay Bridge Building Co., of Shropshire, and later at the electrical works of P. R. Jackson, of Man- chester, he joined the firm of Monks, Hall and Co., Warrington, and was appointed secretary and engineer. In 1911 he was appointed engi- neer to the Frodingham (Lincolnshire) Steel and Iron Co., but that autumn was compelled to .retire owing to severe illness. During the pre- ceding years Captain Owen visited many steel works in Canada and America, and on the Con- tinent.,</p>
        <p>Captain Owen was a former Volunteer, and also belonged to the local Territorial Battalion. Gazetted second-lieutenant in 1902 and captain in 1906, he commanded the Earlestown and Newton-le-Willows units. So recently as 1913 he attended camp; but from this sphere also failing health forced his retirement, and in March last year Ihe resigned his commission. Although thus disqualified from active service in the present war, Captain Owen rendered in.. Valuable help to recruiting and in the work of the Battalion up to its leaving for Scotland. On the formation of the Second 4th Battalion (under Lieutenant-Colonel J. D. Fairclough) he was offered the secretaryship of the National Reserve Guard, which he accepted as an honor- ary post under Colonel de Silva. At the latter end of April, however. his ultimately fatal illness caused him to relinquish this post also. _ _ .</p>
        <p>At St. Matthew's Church. Stretton, the funeral took place at noon on Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0412" n="art0412">
        <head>WAR ITEMS.</head>
        <p>WAR ITEMS.</p>
        <p>" TOMMY " AND "THE OLD SCHOOL." The children at St. Thomas' School, St:-,,...kt0n Heath, have been sending messages to the men at the Front. Miss Doris Clarke, me- of the scholars, has received a reply postcard froiman Old Thomasian in the trench2s. "Jim Walton" (we do mot know Ws midi) solids his heartiest wishes for a successful Walking Day (alluding, to that at Stockton Heath), and his "best respects to the old school." He adds: "I hope it will not be long before 1 sce it again."</p>
        <p>LOCAL PR!SONERS OF WAR.</p>
        <p>During the present week it was officially re- ported that Privates J. Fleming 'and T. roden, of the South Lancs. Regiment, who had been previously 'reported as missing, have now been found to be prisoners in the hanis the. Ger- mans. Private . Flemine3 h ,nie is in Back Crossley-street, Warrington, an) Private Foden's in Pierpoint-street. Private F. Mer- chant, of the South. Lancs., whose home is in Percy-street, is also reported to be a prisoner of war.</p>
      </div>
