        <p>D</p>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>JOLLY REVUE AT THE ROYAL COURT.</p>
        <p>Described on the programme as "a real, ripping revue," "All Aboard" has, twice each night this week, been justifying the de- scription which the management applied to it. Large houses have revelled in it, and rightly too, for it lacks little in the matter of humour, tunefulness, scenery and cos- tumes, and those are the main things that matter in productions of the kind. All who like mirth and music, pretty girls and dainty costumes should make a point of seeing one of the two shows yet remaining. The merry two- hour trifle goes with a swing from the moment the curtain goes up on a scene on the landing. stage at Liverpool until it descends' on the lounge of the "Hotel Syncopatia, London," where all sorts of laughable doings transpire. The company is a good one and includes the producer and part-inventor of the revue—Mr. Ventom Swift. who works hard and success- fully as Irving Raggs, the Rag-time King.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0414" n="art0414">
        <head>31 I,IIVELLOU S MI STERY-MAGIC AT lIIPPODRONIE.</head>
        <p>31 I,IIVELLOU S MI STERY-MAGIC AT lIIPPODRONIE.</p>
        <p>Of course our prophecy of last week proved perfectly 'correct. From the outset it was quite clear ,that Chung Ling Soo, the world's greatest conjurer, in a performance of Oriental splen- dour and weird mysticism, assisted by Miss Sue Seen, was fated to be one of the greatest attractions ever known in the history of vaude- ville in Warrington. 'lf anything, indeed, we under-estimated the case.</p>
        <p>Part one of this popular mystic's perform- ance—the Willow Pattern Plate—was devoted to a series of small but utterly baffling tricks, to the attractiveness' of which Chung Ling added considerably by his slyly remote smiles and novel expressions: In the second part were introduced: four very fine tricks, entitled re- spectively, ." The Mystic Bottle," "The Crea- tion of a Butterfly," "A Dream of Wealth," and "Home from Market," These were bril- liant indeed; yet they pale into comparative insignificance considered with part three—" The World and its People," concluding with "Britannia's Realm" (three tableaux). Daring in scheme, dazzling in production, this is a superb masterpiece in magic, art and sentiment. Rarely is a stage so gaily and lavishly decor- ated.</p>
        <p>Harry Weste is a fine baritone, and brings the house down with "Parson and Me." His style is perfect; and he throws himself whole- heartedly into every detail of the song with unfailing success. Trumpet-Major Wix is in great and enthusiastic demand as "King of the Post Horn, Bugle and Trumpet," in the novel and remarkable feat of sounding a fan- fare on two cavalry trumpets at the same time. The Cadillos' sensational wire-act is capitally given, .the cycle-balancing being particularly effective; the "inebriated female" also calls forth much laughter. Dot and Doris give the finest turn of its kind we remember seeing for a long time, and richly merit the salvos of applause which have recalled them night after night. . _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0415" n="art0415">
        <head>Next Week's Attractions.</head>
        <p>Next Week's Attractions.</p>
        <p>Briff, Braff: and Broff, "refined comedy tumblers and laughter-makers," head the attractive bill of fare in preparation for the patrons of this popular place of amusement next week. Another strong line will be pro- vided by Harry Atkinson and Co., in a musical novelty, entitled "The unhappy Medium." The Sisters Reeve will sing sweet songs and the Martini Trio. are also expected to provide a musical treat, whilst Cole Cliffe, the well-known Yorkshire comedian, may be depended upon to provide many hearty laughs. Many other excellent turns will go to complete what bids fair to prove a most enjoyable show.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0416" n="art0416">
        <head>\XII/ ICAL COMEDY-DRAMA AT STAR KINEMA.</head>
        <p>\XII/ ICAL COMEDY-DRAMA AT STAR KINEMA.</p>
        <p>Comedy is so akin to tragedy that it is sur- prising the idea of deliberately blending them into one scheme has not been popularised before. However, the management of the always-leading Star Kinema foresaw the great possibilities of such an innovation; and as a result one has had a remarkably attractive programme at that resort during the first half of the week. In it "The Spitfire "—a nautical comedy-drama—Occupied. place premiere, being responsible for well-filled houses. Intensified by a fine foreign setting, the drama, is capitally relieved in style infallibly pleasing. " Kitch- eiter's Army Instructors" in training is a war- film only equalled by "Pathe's Animated Gazette "—whose topical scope includes this district. Cute and comical is "The Profes- sional Scapegoat," a film which 'calls forth great hilarity. "Round the Lake District" is an entrancing scenic item of much' educa- tional value.</p>
        <p>Place of honour in to-day's not-to-he-missed bills of fare is assigned to "The Conspiracy" —a detective story of peculiarly engrossing interest.</p>
        <p>• Striking Variety Next Week. Right capital bills of fare for next week are booked. In that for the first three days place premiere is assigned to "What's His Name?" whilst "The Man From Mexico?' at the week- end, will be found as engrossing as. its title would suggest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-03_WidnesExaminer_art0417" n="art0417">
        <head>FLAG DAY FINANCE.</head>
        <p>FLAG DAY FINANCE.</p>
        <p>Nearly £350 liaised For The War Funds.</p>
        <p>The committee in charge of the Flag Day recently held in Warrington have now pre- sented their report, from which it appears that the total sum collected was £392 7s. 4d. After paying the expenses of flags, prinl.ing, &amp;c., balance of £344 4s. 7d. remains, which sum has been allocated by the Executive Committee as follows :—Russian Fund; £100; French Fund, £75; Belgium Fund, £44 4s. 7d.; and Servian Fund, £25. The remaining £lOO has been divided amongst the following local institu- tions: Warrington Infirmary, £5O; White- cross Hospital, £2O; Raddon Court Red Cross Hospital, £10; Winwick War Hospital, £10; and The Oaklands and Thelwall Hospitals, £5 each.</p>
        <p>The Mayor and the Executive Committee de- sire to express their thanks to the Alliance Box Company (per Mr. Felix Maginn) for so generously giving the oardboard boxes which were used on the dtty; to the Manchester and Liverpool District Banking Co., for facilities provided; and to all the willing helpers, who, by selling the flags, did .so much towards mak- ing the day such a success, and they regKet that it is not passible to thank each helper individually.</p>
