        <p>LANCE-CORPL. F. SHAKESHAFT Loses His Right Leg. ' Lance-Corporal Frank Shakeshaft,, son of Mr. John Shakeshaft, 69, Wash-lane, who was a shop assistant when the war broke out, was Wounded in the leg, which had to be ampu- tated. He is 21 years of age, attended Christ Church School, Latchford, and was at one time a member of the Church Lads' Brigade in that</p>
        <p>RESCUED LIEUTENANT HATTON. Sergeant Hansford Among The Wounded. Sergeant Geo. Hansford, who lived with his aunt at Chester New-road, was wounded in the neck and back by shell fragments. it was Sergeant Geo. Hansford who, with Private Cesar, left the trench under fire to bring in</p>
        <p>PRIVATE THOMAS CHANIPION. Wounded Just Above The Left Knee By A Piece Of Shrapnel. Private Thos. Champion, who was wounded in the big charge, wrote to his mother, who fives at 5, Cobden-street, to say that his hurt was not serious and that he hoped to be home very soon. "My wound," his letter con-</p>
        <p>" TERRIERS' " HOLIDAY. Short Home Leave After Months In Trenches. pEROES OF THE GREAT CHARGE. Some of the gallant Warrington Territorials who so distinguished themselves in the recent glorious charge upon, and capture of, several lines of German trenches and who suffered heavily in the doing thereof, have been given brief furloughs and sent over to. England. They would appear to be coming in driblets of I five or six a time. As was recorded in our last issue, the first small handful arrived in the early hours of Friday, the 25th ult., and re- turned to home headquarters on Sunday. A second tiny draft of five, under Sergeant Spencer, landed in their native, town in the "wee sma' hours" of Wednesday morning and- they go back to-morrow. They had a perfectly ,uneventful journey• across , and: duly reached home ,safe and sound, greatly to the delight of their respective wives and families. Needless to say, from the moment of their arrival to tbe time of their departure they were cynosures for the eyes of all who knew them by sight, and the bull's-eye for congratulations and questions from all who knew,, them personally. , But they did not give much information away. An " Examiner " representative tracked three of the gallant lads down: . , A " Ticklish and Risky Charge." One was, if anything, closer than the pro- verbial. oyster, and said that he was afraid that the publication of his name even might be undesirable, another, requesting that his name be suppressed, said that the " Terriers " went through quite one of the most "ticklish and risky " charges of the whole war, and that they conducted themselves like men, and though they</p>
