        <p>CORPORAL JOHN ROSE. Died of Wounds.</p>
        <p>serving in the Army. One, William, is with the Connaught Rangers, and the two others, Sam and Charles, are in the King's Royal Rifles:- All three joined His Majesty's Forces after the war broke out. The late Corporal Rose is a son of Mr. Thomas Rose, a well- known member of the Warrington Liberal Club. As a( boy 'he went to St. Thomas' School, whilst he also attended the Buckley- street Methodist Sunday School, subsequently becoming a member of the Church and a regu- lar attendant at the services. He was keen on football and formerly played for the Winwick Juniors' F.C.</p>
        <p>W. l'ratt, of the 4th Middlesex Regiment, enclosing a photograph belonging to Private Gibson, which he had picked up on the battle- field. Part of the photograph was torn away, and the soldier who forwarded the picture wrote:—" That is where the bullet struck." Subsequently Private Pratt wrote: "I am sorry to have to tell you that your on is dead, and I helped to bury him. I did not like to tell you in my last letter, when I forwarded the photo- graph, as I thought you would be upset. I also thought that you would have been in-</p>
        <p>- - - , . . • . . *." .... t, , 446 • ' - • ' • • 141;k</p>
        <p>PRIVATE WILLIAM GIBSON. Reported Died of Wounds.</p>
        <p>OK HONOUR'S SCROLL. DIED OF WOUNDS. LANCE-CORPL. T. J. BURKE. Old Soldier Who Fought The Boers And Was Recalled To The Colours. Less than twenty-four hours after she had received a cable message assuring her that he was making good progress under all the cir- cumstances and within an hour or two of re- ceiving a letter stating that he was improving steadily, Mrs. Burke, of 14, Stephen-street, Warrington, got an official communication stat- ing that .her husband, Lance-Corporal Thomas J. Burke, of the North Staffordshire Regiment, had passed away. The gallant Lance-Corporal, who was 35 years of age, was, at the outbreak of war, employed as a linesman at the War- rington Post Office, had previously served in the Army, and, being on the reserve list, he was recalled to the colours. He fought in the South African War, and for his services there was entitled to wear both the King's and Queen's medals. He was so severely wounded by shrapnel in a recent engagement that his right leg had to be amputated, his left leg also being severely damaged. It was from these injuries that he died. Interested in Friendly Society Work. Lance-Corporal Burke, who was spoken of as a good fellow by all who knew him, was inter- ested in Friendly Society work and was a mem-</p>
